const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Before Leonato's House.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LEONATO, HERO, BEATRICE and others, with a Messenger.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I learn in this letter that Don Pedro of Arragon comes this night","to Messina."],"id":2,"m":"This letter tells me that Don Pedro of Aragon arrives in Messina tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He is very near by this: he was not three leagues off when I left","him."],"id":3,"m":"He's very close by now. He was less than nine miles away when I left him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["How many gentlemen have you lost in this action?"],"id":4,"m":"How many gentlemen did you lose in this battle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["But few of any sort, and none of name."],"id":5,"m":"Very few of any rank, and none of any note."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["A victory is twice itself when the achiever brings home full numbers.","I find here that Don Pedro hath bestowed much honour on a young","Florentine called Claudio."],"id":6,"m":"A victory is doubled when the winner brings home his whole army. This says Don Pedro has given great honour to a young man from Florence called Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Much deserved on his part, and equally remembered by Don Pedro.","He hath borne himself beyond the promise of his age, doing in the","figure of a lamb the feats of a lion: he hath indeed better bettered","expectation than you must expect of me to tell you how."],"id":7,"m":"He fully deserved it, and Don Pedro has rewarded him just as fully. He has acted far beyond what you'd expect at his age, doing a lion's deeds while looking like a lamb. He has done even better than people hoped, better than I can expect to describe to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["He hath an uncle here in Messina will be very much glad of it."],"id":8,"m":"He has an uncle here in Messina who will be very glad to hear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["I have already delivered him letters, and there appears much joy in","him; even so much that joy could not show itself modest enough without","a badge of bitterness."],"id":9,"m":"I've already given him letters, and he seemed overjoyed; so much so that his joy couldn't stay modest without a sign of grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Did he break out into tears?"],"id":10,"m":"Did he burst into tears?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["In great measure."],"id":11,"m":"A great many."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["A kind overflow of kindness. There are no faces truer than those that","are so washed; how much better is it to weep at joy than to joy at","weeping!"],"id":12,"m":"A natural overflow of feeling. No faces are more honest than those washed with tears like that. How much better it is to weep for joy than to take joy in someone's weeping!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I pray you, is Signior Mountanto returned from the wars or no?"],"id":13,"m":"Please tell me, has Signior Show-off come back from the wars or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["I know none of that name, lady: there was none such in the army","of any sort."],"id":14,"m":"I know no one of that name, lady. There was nobody like that in the army at any rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["What is he that you ask for, niece?"],"id":15,"m":"Who are you asking about, niece?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["My cousin means Signior Benedick of Padua."],"id":16,"m":"My cousin means Signior Benedick of Padua."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["O! he is returned, and as pleasant as ever he was."],"id":17,"m":"Oh, he's back, and as witty as ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["He set up his bills here in Messina and challenged Cupid at the","flight; and my uncle's fool, reading the challenge, subscribed for","Cupid, and challenged him at the bird-bolt. I pray you, how many hath","he killed and eaten in these wars? But how many hath he killed? for,","indeed, I promised to eat all of his killing."],"id":18,"m":"He once put up posters here in Messina challenging Cupid to an archery match; and my uncle's jester read the challenge, signed up on Cupid's behalf, and challenged him with blunt bird-arrows. Tell me, how many has he killed and eaten in these wars? Just how many has he killed? Because I promised to eat everything he killed."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Faith, niece, you tax Signior Benedick too much; but he'll be meet with","you, I doubt it not."],"id":19,"m":"Really, niece, you're too hard on Signior Benedick. But he'll get even with you, I'm sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He hath done good service, lady, in these wars."],"id":20,"m":"He has served well in these wars, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["You had musty victual, and he hath holp to eat it; he is a very","valiant trencher-man; he hath an excellent stomach."],"id":21,"m":"You had stale food, and he helped to eat it. He's a very brave man with a plate; he has an excellent appetite."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["And a good soldier too, lady."],"id":22,"m":"And he's a good soldier too, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["And a good soldier to a lady; but what is he to a lord?"],"id":23,"m":"A good soldier against a lady, yes. But what is he against a man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["A lord to a lord, a man to a man; stuffed with all honourable","virtues."],"id":24,"m":"A lord to a lord, a man to a man, stuffed with every honourable virtue."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["It is so indeed; he is no less than a stuffed man; but for the","stuffing,—well, we are all mortal."],"id":25,"m":"That's right. He's nothing but a stuffed dummy. As for what he's stuffed with, well, we all have our faults."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["You must not, sir, mistake my niece. There is a kind of merry war","betwixt Signior Benedick and her; they never meet but there's a","skirmish of wit between them."],"id":26,"m":"Don't misunderstand my niece, sir. There's a sort of playful war between Signior Benedick and her. They never meet without a battle of wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Alas! he gets nothing by that. In our last conflict four of his five","wits went halting off, and now is the whole man governed with one! so","that if he have wit enough to keep himself warm, let him bear it for a","difference between himself and his horse; for it is all the wealth","that he hath left to be known a reasonable creature. Who is his","companion now? He hath every month a new sworn brother."],"id":27,"m":"Sadly, he gains nothing by it. In our last fight four of his five wits limped away, and now the whole man is run by just one! So if he has enough wit to keep himself warm, let him keep it as the one thing that tells him apart from his horse; it's all he has left to show he is a thinking creature. Who's his best friend now? He has a new sworn brother every month."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Is't possible?"],"id":28,"m":"Is that possible?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Very easily possible: he wears his faith but as the fashion of his","hat; it ever changes with the next block."],"id":29,"m":"Very easily possible. He wears his loyalty like the style of his hat; it changes with every new fashion."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["I see, lady, the gentleman is not in your books."],"id":30,"m":"I can see, lady, the gentleman isn't in your good books."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["No; an he were, I would burn my study. But, I pray you, who is his","companion? Is there no young squarer now that will make a voyage with","him to the devil?"],"id":31,"m":"No; if he were, I'd burn my library. But tell me, who is his friend? Isn't there some young brawler who'll go to the devil with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He is most in the company of the right noble Claudio."],"id":32,"m":"He spends most time with the very noble Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["O Lord, he will hang upon him like a disease: he is sooner caught than","the pestilence, and the taker runs presently mad. God help the noble","Claudio! If he have caught the Benedick, it will cost him a thousand","pound ere a' be cured."],"id":33,"m":"Oh Lord, he'll cling to him like a disease. He's caught faster than the plague, and whoever catches him goes mad at once. God help the noble Claudio! If he's caught the Benedick, it'll cost him a thousand pounds before he's cured."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["I will hold friends with you, lady."],"id":34,"m":"I'll make sure to stay on your good side, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Do, good friend."],"id":35,"m":"Do, my good friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["You will never run mad, niece."],"id":36,"m":"You'll never go mad over him, niece."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["No, not till a hot January."],"id":37,"m":"No, not until we get a hot January."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Don Pedro is approached."],"id":38,"m":"Don Pedro has arrived."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON PEDRO, DON JOHN, CLAUDIO, BENEDICK, BALTHAZAR, and Others.","id":39},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Good Signior Leonato, you are come to meet your trouble: the fashion","of the world is to avoid cost, and you encounter it."],"id":40,"m":"Good Signior Leonato, you've come out to meet your troubles. Most people try to avoid expense, and you go looking for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Never came trouble to my house in the likeness of your Grace, for","trouble being gone, comfort should remain; but when you depart from me,","sorrow abides and happiness takes his leave."],"id":41,"m":"Trouble has never come to my house looking like your Grace. When trouble leaves, comfort should stay; but when you leave me, sorrow stays and happiness goes."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["You embrace your charge too willingly. I think this is your daughter."],"id":42,"m":"You take on your burden too gladly. I think this is your daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Her mother hath many times told me so."],"id":43,"m":"Her mother has often told me so."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Were you in doubt, sir, that you asked her?"],"id":44,"m":"Were you in doubt, sir, that you had to ask her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Signior Benedick, no; for then were you a child."],"id":45,"m":"No, Signior Benedick, because you were only a child then."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["You have it full, Benedick: we may guess by this what you are, being","a man. Truly the lady fathers herself. Be happy, lady, for you are","like an honourable father."],"id":46,"m":"That's got you, Benedick: we can guess from that what kind of man you are. Seriously, the lady looks just like her father. Be happy, lady, for you resemble an honourable father."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["If Signior Leonato be her father, she would not have his head on her","shoulders for all Messina, as like him as she is."],"id":47,"m":"Even if Signior Leonato is her father, she wouldn't want his old grey head on her shoulders for all of Messina, however much she looks like him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I wonder that you will still be talking, Signior Benedick: nobody","marks you."],"id":48,"m":"I'm amazed you keep on talking, Signior Benedick. Nobody is listening to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["What! my dear Lady Disdain, are you yet living?"],"id":49,"m":"What! My dear Lady Disdain, are you still alive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Is it possible Disdain should die while she hath such meet food to","feed it as Signior Benedick? Courtesy itself must convert to disdain","if you come in her presence."],"id":50,"m":"How could Disdain die when she has such perfect food to feed on as Signior Benedick? Courtesy herself would turn into disdain if you came near her."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Then is courtesy a turncoat. But it is certain I am loved of all ladies,","only you excepted; and I would I could find in my heart that I had not","a hard heart; for, truly, I love none."],"id":51,"m":"Then courtesy is a traitor. But it's certain that all ladies love me, except you; and I wish I could find it in my heart not to be so hard-hearted, because honestly, I love none of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["A dear happiness to women: they would else have been troubled with a","pernicious suitor. I thank God and my cold blood, I am of your humour","for that. I had rather hear my dog bark at a crow than a man swear he","loves me."],"id":52,"m":"What luck for women: otherwise they'd be pestered by a dreadful suitor. I thank God and my cold blood that I feel the same as you about that. I'd rather hear my dog bark at a crow than hear a man swear he loves me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["God keep your ladyship still in that mind; so some gentleman or other","shall 'scape a predestinate scratched face."],"id":53,"m":"God keep your ladyship in that frame of mind; then some gentleman or other will escape the scratched face he was fated to get."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Scratching could not make it worse, an 'twere such a face as yours","were."],"id":54,"m":"Scratching couldn't make it any worse, if it were a face like yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Well, you are a rare parrot-teacher."],"id":55,"m":"Well, you'd be great at teaching a parrot to talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["A bird of my tongue is better than a beast of yours."],"id":56,"m":"A bird that talks like me is better than a beast that talks like you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I would my horse had the speed of your tongue, and so good a","continuer. But keep your way, i' God's name; I have done."],"id":57,"m":"I wish my horse had the speed of your tongue and could keep going as long. But go your way, for God's sake; I've finished."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["You always end with a jade's trick: I know you of old."],"id":58,"m":"You always end with a cheap trick, like a horse that stops dead. I know you from before."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["That is the sum of all, Leonato: Signior Claudio, and Signior Benedick,","my dear friend Leonato hath invited you all. I tell him we shall stay","here at the least a month, and he heartly prays some occasion may","detain us longer: I dare swear he is no hypocrite, but prays from his","heart."],"id":59,"m":"That's everything, Leonato. Signior Claudio and Signior Benedick, my dear friend Leonato has invited you all. I've told him we'll stay here at least a month, and he sincerely hopes something will keep us longer. I'd swear he's no hypocrite and means it from his heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["If you swear, my lord, you shall not be forsworn.","[To DON JOHN]","Let me bid you welcome, my lord: being reconciled to the prince your","brother, I owe you all duty."],"id":60,"m":"If you swear it, my lord, you won't be proved a liar. Let me welcome you, my lord. Now that you're reconciled with your brother the prince, I owe you every duty."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I thank you: I am not of many words, but I thank you."],"id":61,"m":"Thank you. I'm a man of few words, but thank you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Please it your Grace lead on?"],"id":62,"m":"Will your Grace lead the way?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Your hand, Leonato; we will go together."],"id":63,"m":"Give me your hand, Leonato; we'll go together."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but BENEDICK and CLAUDIO.","id":64},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Benedick, didst thou note the daughter of Signior Leonato?"],"id":65,"m":"Benedick, did you notice Signior Leonato's daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I noted her not; but I looked on her."],"id":66,"m":"I didn't notice her, but I did look at her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Is she not a modest young lady?"],"id":67,"m":"Isn't she a modest young lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Do you question me, as an honest man should do, for my simple true","judgment; or would you have me speak after my custom, as being a","professed tyrant to their sex?"],"id":68,"m":"Are you asking me, as an honest man would, for my plain true opinion? Or do you want me to speak as usual, as a sworn tyrant against women?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["No; I pray thee speak in sober judgment."],"id":69,"m":"No, please give me a serious opinion."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Why, i' faith, methinks she's too low for a high praise, too brown","for a fair praise, and too little for a great praise; only this","commendation I can afford her, that were she other than she is, she","were unhandsome, and being no other but as she is, I do not like her."],"id":70,"m":"Well, honestly, I think she's too short for high praise, too dark for fair praise, and too small for great praise. The only compliment I can give her is this: if she were different from how she is, she'd be ugly, and being just as she is, I don't like her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Thou thinkest I am in sport: I pray thee tell me truly how thou","likest her."],"id":71,"m":"You think I'm joking. Please tell me honestly what you think of her."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Would you buy her, that you enquire after her?"],"id":72,"m":"Are you planning to buy her, asking about her like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Can the world buy such a jewel?"],"id":73,"m":"Could the whole world buy such a jewel?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Yea, and a case to put it into. But speak you this with a sad brow, or","do you play the flouting Jack, to tell us Cupid is a good hare-finder,","and Vulcan a rare carpenter? Come, in what key shall a man take you,","to go in the song?"],"id":74,"m":"Yes, and a box to keep it in. But are you saying this with a straight face, or are you playing the mocking joker, telling us that blind Cupid is good at spotting hares and Vulcan the blacksmith is a fine carpenter? Come on, what key are you singing in, so I can join the song?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["In mine eye she is the sweetest lady that ever I looked on."],"id":75,"m":"In my eyes she's the sweetest lady I've ever seen."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I can see yet without spectacles and I see no such matter: there's","her cousin an she were not possessed with a fury, exceeds her as much","in beauty as the first of May doth the last of December. But I hope you","have no intent to turn husband, have you?"],"id":76,"m":"I can still see without glasses, and I see nothing of the kind. Her cousin, if she weren't possessed by a fury, is as far beyond her in beauty as the first of May is beyond the last of December. But I hope you're not planning to become a husband, are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I would scarce trust myself, though I had sworn to the contrary, if Hero","would be my wife."],"id":77,"m":"I'd hardly trust myself, even if I'd sworn the opposite, if Hero would be my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Is't come to this, i' faith? Hath not the world one man but he will wear","his cap with suspicion? Shall I never see a bachelor of threescore","again? Go to, i' faith; an thou wilt needs thrust thy neck into a yoke,","wear the print of it and sigh away Sundays. Look! Don Pedro is returned","to seek you."],"id":78,"m":"Has it come to this, really? Isn't there one man in the world who won't wear his hat worrying it hides a cuckold's horns? Will I never see a sixty-year-old bachelor again? Go on, then; if you must stick your neck into a yoke, wear the mark of it and sigh away your Sundays. Look! Don Pedro has come back for you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter DON PEDRO.","id":79},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["What secret hath held you here, that you followed not to Leonato's?"],"id":80,"m":"What secret kept you here, so that you didn't follow us to Leonato's?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I would your Grace would constrain me to tell."],"id":81,"m":"I wish your Grace would force me to tell."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I charge thee on thy allegiance."],"id":82,"m":"I order you to, by your loyalty to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["You hear, Count Claudio: I can be secret as a dumb man; I would have","you think so; but on my allegiance mark you this, on my allegiance: he","is in love. With who? now that is your Grace's part. Mark how short his","answer is: with Hero, Leonato's short daughter."],"id":83,"m":"You hear that, Count Claudio. I can keep a secret like a mute, and I'd like you to believe that; but on my loyalty, notice this, on my loyalty: he's in love. With whom? That's your Grace's line. See how short his answer is: with Hero, Leonato's short daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If this were so, so were it uttered."],"id":84,"m":"If that were true, that's how he'd tell it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Like the old tale, my lord: 'it is not so, nor 'twas not so; but indeed,","God forbid it should be so.'"],"id":85,"m":"Like the old story, my lord: 'It isn't so, and it wasn't so, and God forbid it should be so.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If my passion change not shortly. God forbid it should be otherwise."],"id":86,"m":"Unless my feelings change soon, God forbid it should be otherwise."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Amen, if you love her; for the lady is very well worthy."],"id":87,"m":"Amen, if you love her, for she is very worthy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["You speak this to fetch me in, my lord."],"id":88,"m":"You're saying this to trap me, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["By my troth, I speak my thought."],"id":89,"m":"Upon my word, I'm saying what I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And in faith, my lord, I spoke mine."],"id":90,"m":"And honestly, my lord, I said what I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["And by my two faiths and troths, my lord, I spoke mine."],"id":91,"m":"And by both my words of honour, my lord, I said what I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["That I love her, I feel."],"id":92,"m":"That I love her, I feel."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["That she is worthy, I know."],"id":93,"m":"That she is worthy, I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["That I neither feel how she should be loved nor know how she should","be worthy, is the opinion that fire cannot melt out of me: I will die","in it at the stake."],"id":94,"m":"That I can't feel why she should be loved or know why she's worthy is an opinion that fire couldn't burn out of me. I'll die for it at the stake."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Thou wast ever an obstinate heretic in the despite of beauty."],"id":95,"m":"You were always a stubborn heretic in your contempt for beauty."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And never could maintain his part but in the force of his will."],"id":96,"m":"And he could only keep up his act by sheer stubbornness."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["That a woman conceived me, I thank her; that she brought me up, I","likewise give her most humble thanks; but that I will have a recheat","winded in my forehead, or hang my bugle in an invisible baldrick, all","women shall pardon me. Because I will not do them the wrong to","mistrust any, I will do myself the right to trust none; and the fine","is,—for the which I may go the finer,—I will live a bachelor."],"id":97,"m":"I thank a woman for giving birth to me; for bringing me up I humbly thank her too. But I'll never have a hunting horn blown on my forehead or hang my horn in an invisible belt, and all women will have to forgive me. Since I won't insult any woman by distrusting her, I'll do myself the favour of trusting none; and the end of it is (and I'll be the better dressed for it) I'll live a bachelor."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I shall see thee, ere I die, look pale with love."],"id":98,"m":"I'll see you looking pale with love before I die."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["With anger, with sickness, or with hunger, my lord; not with love: prove","that ever I lose more blood with love than I will get again with","drinking, pick out mine eyes with a ballad-maker's pen and hang me up at","the door of a brothel-house for the sign of blind Cupid."],"id":99,"m":"Pale with anger, with sickness or with hunger, my lord, but not with love. If you can prove I ever lose more blood through love than I can win back with a drink, then pick out my eyes with a love poet's pen and hang me over the door of a brothel as the sign of blind Cupid."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Well, if ever thou dost fall from this faith, thou wilt prove a notable","argument."],"id":100,"m":"Well, if you ever give up this belief, you'll be a famous example."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["If I do, hang me in a bottle like a cat and shoot at me; and he that","hits me, let him be clapped on the shoulder and called Adam."],"id":101,"m":"If I do, hang me in a basket like a cat and shoot arrows at me; and whoever hits me can be slapped on the back and called a champion archer."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Well, as time shall try: 'In time the savage bull doth bear the yoke.'"],"id":102,"m":"Well, time will tell. 'In time the wild bull accepts the yoke.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["The savage bull may; but if ever the sensible Benedick bear it, pluck","off the bull's horns and set them in my forehead; and let me be vilely","painted, and in such great letters as they write, 'Here is good horse","to hire,' let them signify under my sign 'Here you may see Benedick","the married man.'"],"id":103,"m":"The wild bull may; but if the sensible Benedick ever accepts it, pull off the bull's horns and stick them on my forehead. Then paint me crudely, and in big letters like the ones on signs saying 'Good horse for hire', let them write under my picture: 'Here you may see Benedick the married man.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If this should ever happen, thou wouldst be horn-mad."],"id":104,"m":"If that ever happened, you'd be raving mad, horns and all."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Nay, if Cupid have not spent all his quiver in Venice, thou wilt quake","for this shortly."],"id":105,"m":"No, if Cupid hasn't used up all his arrows in Venice, you'll soon tremble for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I look for an earthquake too then."],"id":106,"m":"Then I'll expect an earthquake too."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Well, you will temporize with the hours. In the meantime, good Signior","Benedick, repair to Leonato's: commend me to him and tell him I will","not fail him at supper; for indeed he hath made great preparation."],"id":107,"m":"Well, you'll soften with time. Meanwhile, good Signior Benedick, go to Leonato's. Give him my regards and tell him I'll be at supper without fail, because he has gone to a lot of trouble over it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I have almost matter enough in me for such an embassage; and so I","commit you—"],"id":108,"m":"I have almost enough sense in me for such an important errand; and so I leave you—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["To the tuition of God: from my house, if I had it,—"],"id":109,"m":"In the care of God: from my house, if I had one,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["The sixth of July: your loving friend, Benedick."],"id":110,"m":"The sixth of July: your loving friend, Benedick."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Nay, mock not, mock not. The body of your discourse is sometime guarded","with fragments, and the guards are but slightly basted on neither: ere","you flout old ends any further, examine your conscience: and so I leave","you."],"id":111,"m":"No, don't mock me. Your talk is sometimes trimmed with bits and pieces, and even the trimmings are loosely stitched on. Before you poke fun at old phrases any more, check your own conscience. And so I leave you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":112},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["My liege, your highness now may do me good."],"id":113,"m":"My lord, your Highness could help me now."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["My love is thine to teach: teach it but how,","And thou shalt see how apt it is to learn","hard lesson that may do thee good."],"id":114,"m":"My love is yours to teach. Just show it how, and you'll see how quickly it learns any hard lesson that could do you good."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Hath Leonato any son, my lord?"],"id":115,"m":"Does Leonato have a son, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["No child but Hero; she's his only heir.","Dost thou affect her, Claudio?"],"id":116,"m":"No child but Hero; she's his only heir. Do you love her, Claudio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O! my lord,","When you went onward on this ended action,","I looked upon her with a soldier's eye,","That lik'd, but had a rougher task in hand","Than to drive liking to the name of love;","But now I am return'd, and that war-thoughts","Have left their places vacant, in their rooms","Come thronging soft and delicate desires,","All prompting me how fair young Hero is,","Saying, I lik'd her ere I went to wars."],"id":117,"m":"Oh, my lord, when you set off on this war that's now over, I looked at her with a soldier's eye. I liked her, but I had a tougher job in hand than to turn liking into love. But now I'm back, and thoughts of war have left their places empty, and soft, delicate desires come crowding in, all telling me how lovely young Hero is and saying I liked her before I went to war."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Thou wilt be like a lover presently,","And tire the hearer with a book of words.","If thou dost love fair Hero, cherish it,","And I will break with her, and with her father,","And thou shalt have her. Was't not to this end","That thou began'st to twist so fine a story?"],"id":118,"m":"You'll soon be just like a lover, and bore your listener with a book of words. If you love beautiful Hero, hold on to it, and I'll speak to her and her father, and you'll have her. Wasn't that why you began to spin such a fine story?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["How sweetly you do minister to love,","That know love's grief by his complexion!","But lest my liking might too sudden seem,","I would have salv'd it with a longer treatise."],"id":119,"m":"How sweetly you look after love, recognising love's pain from my face! But in case my feelings seemed too sudden, I wanted to explain them at more length."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["What need the bridge much broader than the flood?","The fairest grant is the necessity.","Look, what will serve is fit: 'tis once, thou lov'st,","And I will fit thee with the remedy.","I know we shall have revelling to-night:","I will assume thy part in some disguise,","And tell fair Hero I am Claudio;","And in her bosom I'll unclasp my heart,","And take her hearing prisoner with the force","And strong encounter of my amorous tale:","Then, after to her father will I break;","And the conclusion is, she shall be thine.","In practice let us put it presently."],"id":120,"m":"Why build the bridge any wider than the river? The best gift is what's needed. Look, whatever works is right. In short, you love her, and I'll give you the cure. I know there'll be a party tonight. I'll take your place in a disguise and tell lovely Hero I am Claudio. I'll open my heart to her and take her ears prisoner with the force and power of my loving words. Then I'll speak to her father, and the result will be that she's yours. Let's put it into action right away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":121},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"A room in Leonato's house.","id":122},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LEONATO and ANTONIO, meeting.","id":123},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["How now, brother! Where is my cousin your son? Hath he provided","this music?"],"id":124,"m":"Well, brother! Where's my nephew, your son? Has he arranged this music?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["He is very busy about it. But, brother, I can tell you strange","news that you yet dreamt not of."],"id":125,"m":"He's very busy with it. But, brother, I can tell you strange news you never dreamed of."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Are they good?"],"id":126,"m":"Is it good news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["As the event stamps them: but they have a good cover; they show well","outward. The prince and Count Claudio, walking in a thick-pleached","alley in my orchard, were thus much overheard by a man of mine: the","prince discovered to Claudio that he loved my niece your daughter and","meant to acknowledge it this night in a dance; and if he found her","accordant, he meant to take the present time by the top and instantly","break with you of it."],"id":127,"m":"That depends on how things turn out, but it looks good on the outside. The prince and Count Claudio were walking along a thickly hedged path in my orchard and were overheard by one of my men. The prince told Claudio that he loved my niece, your daughter, and meant to tell her so tonight at the dance; and if she agreed, he meant to seize the moment and speak to you about it at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Hath the fellow any wit that told you this?"],"id":128,"m":"Has the man who told you this got any sense?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["A good sharp fellow: I will send for him; and question him","yourself."],"id":129,"m":"He's a sharp fellow. I'll send for him, and you can question him yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["No, no; we will hold it as a dream till it appear itself: but I will","acquaint my daughter withal, that she may be the better prepared for","an answer, if peradventure this be true. Go you, and tell her of it."],"id":130,"m":"No, no. We'll treat it as a dream until it really happens. But I'll tell my daughter about it, so she'll be better prepared with an answer in case it is true. Go and tell her about it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Several persons cross the stage.","id":131},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","cont":1,"lines":["Cousins, you know what you have to do. O! I cry you mercy, friend; go","you with me, and I will use your skill. Good cousin, have a care this","busy time."],"id":132,"m":"Cousins, you know what you have to do. Oh, I beg your pardon, friend; come with me, and I'll make use of your skill. Good cousin, take care in this busy time."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":133},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"Another room in Leonato's house.","id":134},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON JOHN and CONRADE.","id":135},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["What the good-year, my lord! why are you thus out of measure sad?"],"id":136,"m":"What the devil, my lord! Why are you so terribly gloomy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["There is no measure in the occasion that breeds; therefore the sadness","is without limit."],"id":137,"m":"There's no limit to the thing that causes it, so the gloom has no limit either."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["You should hear reason."],"id":138,"m":"You should listen to reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["And when I have heard it, what blessings brings it?"],"id":139,"m":"And when I've listened, what good does it do me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["If not a present remedy, at least a patient sufferance."],"id":140,"m":"If not an instant cure, at least the patience to bear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I wonder that thou, being,—as thou say'st thou art,—born under","Saturn, goest about to apply a moral medicine to a mortifying","mischief. I cannot hide what I am: I must be sad when I have cause,","and smile at no man's jests; eat when I have stomach, and wait for no","man's leisure; sleep when I am drowsy, and tend on no man's business;","laugh when I am merry, and claw no man in his humour."],"id":141,"m":"I'm surprised that you, born under gloomy Saturn as you say you were, try to apply moral medicine to a fatal disease. I can't hide what I am. I must be sad when I have reason, and smile at no one's jokes; eat when I'm hungry and wait for nobody's convenience; sleep when I'm tired and do no one's business; laugh when I'm happy and flatter no one in his moods."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Yea; but you must not make the full show of this till you may do it","without controlment. You have of late stood out against your brother,","and he hath ta'en you newly into his grace; where it is impossible you","should take true root but by the fair weather that you make yourself:","it is needful that you frame the season for your own harvest."],"id":142,"m":"Yes, but you mustn't show all this until you can do it without anyone controlling you. You recently rebelled against your brother, and he has only just taken you back into favour. You can't put down real roots there unless you make the good weather yourself. You need to create the right season for your own harvest."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I had rather be a canker in a hedge than a rose in his grace; and it","better fits my blood to be disdained of all than to fashion a carriage","to rob love from any: in this, though I cannot be said to be a","flattering honest man, it must not be denied but I am a plain-dealing","villain.","I am trusted with a muzzle and enfranchised with a clog; therefore I","have decreed not to sing in my cage. If I had my mouth, I would bite;","if I had my liberty, I would do my liking: in the meantime, let me be","that I am, and seek not to alter me."],"id":143,"m":"I'd rather be a wild rose in a hedge than a cultivated rose in his favour. It suits my nature better to be despised by everyone than to put on an act to steal anyone's love. In this, though no one can call me a flattering honest man, no one can deny I'm a plain, honest villain. I'm trusted only with a muzzle on and freed only with a weight on my leg, so I've decided not to sing in my cage. If I had my mouth free, I would bite; if I had my freedom, I'd do as I like. Meanwhile, let me be what I am, and don't try to change me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Can you make no use of your discontent?"],"id":144,"m":"Can't you make some use of your discontent?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I make all use of it, for I use it only. Who comes here?"],"id":145,"m":"I make every use of it, for it's all I use. Who's coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Borachio.","id":146},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","cont":1,"lines":["What news, Borachio?"],"id":147,"m":"What's the news, Borachio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["I came yonder from a great supper: the prince your brother is royally","entertained by Leonato; and I can give you intelligence of an","intended marriage."],"id":148,"m":"I've come from a great supper over there. The prince your brother is being royally entertained by Leonato, and I can tell you about a planned marriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Will it serve for any model to build mischief on? What is he for a","fool that betroths himself to unquietness?"],"id":149,"m":"Could it be a foundation to build trouble on? Who's the fool who's promising himself to a lifetime of trouble?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Marry, it is your brother's right hand."],"id":150,"m":"Why, it's your brother's right-hand man."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Who? the most exquisite Claudio?"],"id":151,"m":"Who? The ever so perfect Claudio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Even he."],"id":152,"m":"That's him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["A proper squire! And who, and who? which way looks he?"],"id":153,"m":"A fine young gentleman! And who is it? Who? Which way is he looking?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Marry, on Hero, the daughter and heir of Leonato."],"id":154,"m":"At Hero, Leonato's daughter and heir."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["A very forward March-chick! How came you to this?"],"id":155,"m":"A very forward young chick, hatched too early! How did you find this out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Being entertained for a perfumer, as I was smoking a musty room, comes","me the prince and Claudio, hand in hand, in sad conference: I whipt me","behind the arras, and there heard it agreed upon that the prince should","woo Hero for himself, and having obtained her, give her to Count Claudio."],"id":156,"m":"I'd been hired to perfume the house, and as I was burning herbs in a stuffy room, in came the prince and Claudio arm in arm, deep in serious talk. I slipped behind the tapestry and heard them agree that the prince would woo Hero for himself and, once he'd won her, give her to Count Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Come, come; let us thither: this may prove food to my displeasure. That","young start-up hath all the glory of my overthrow: if I can cross him","any way, I bless myself every way. You are both sure, and will assist me?"],"id":157,"m":"Come on, let's go there. This may feed my anger. That young upstart has all the glory of my defeat. If I can thwart him in any way, I'll count myself lucky in every way. You're both reliable and will help me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["To the death, my lord."],"id":158,"m":"To the death, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Let us to the great supper: their cheer is the greater that I am","subdued. Would the cook were of my mind! Shall we go to prove what's","to be done?"],"id":159,"m":"Let's go to the great supper. Their party is all the merrier because I've been beaten. I wish the cook felt like I do and poisoned them! Shall we go and see what can be done?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["We'll wait upon your lordship."],"id":160,"m":"We'll follow your lordship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":161},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"A hall in Leonato's house.","id":162},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LEONATO, ANTONIO, HERO, BEATRICE, and Others.","id":163},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Was not Count John here at supper?"],"id":164,"m":"Wasn't Count John here at supper?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["I saw him not."],"id":165,"m":"I didn't see him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["How tartly that gentleman looks! I never can see him but I am","heart-burned an hour after."],"id":166,"m":"How sour that gentleman looks! I can never see him without getting heartburn for an hour afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["He is of a very melancholy disposition."],"id":167,"m":"He has a very gloomy nature."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["He were an excellent man that were made just in the mid-way between","him and Benedick: the one is too like an image, and says nothing; and","the other too like my lady's eldest son, evermore tattling."],"id":168,"m":"The perfect man would be made exactly halfway between him and Benedick. One is too like a statue and says nothing, and the other is too like a spoilt eldest son, forever chattering."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Then half Signior Benedick's tongue in Count John's mouth, and half","Count John's melancholy in Signior Benedick's face,—"],"id":169,"m":"Then half of Signior Benedick's tongue in Count John's mouth, and half of Count John's gloom in Signior Benedick's face,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["With a good leg and a good foot, uncle, and money enough in his purse,","such a man would win any woman in the world if a' could get her good","will."],"id":170,"m":"With a good leg and a good foot, uncle, and enough money in his purse, a man like that could win any woman in the world, if he could win her approval."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["By my troth, niece, thou wilt never get thee a husband, if thou be","so shrewd of thy tongue."],"id":171,"m":"Honestly, niece, you'll never get yourself a husband if you're so sharp-tongued."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["In faith, she's too curst."],"id":172,"m":"Really, she's too bad-tempered."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Too curst is more than curst: I shall lessen God's sending that way;","for it is said, 'God sends a curst cow short horns;' but to a cow too","curst he sends none."],"id":173,"m":"Too bad-tempered is more than bad-tempered, so I'll reduce what God sends me that way; for they say 'God sends a bad-tempered cow short horns', but to a cow that's too bad-tempered he sends none."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["So, by being too curst, God will send you no horns?"],"id":174,"m":"So, by being too bad-tempered, God will send you no horns?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Just, if he send me no husband; for the which blessing I am at him","upon my knees every morning and evening. Lord! I could not endure a","husband with a beard on his face: I had rather lie in the woollen."],"id":175,"m":"Exactly, if he sends me no husband; and I pray to him on my knees for that blessing every morning and evening. Lord! I couldn't stand a husband with a beard on his face. I'd rather sleep in scratchy wool blankets."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["You may light on a husband that hath no beard."],"id":176,"m":"You might find a husband who has no beard."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["What should I do with him? dress him in my apparel and make him my","waiting-gentlewoman? He that hath a beard is more than a youth, and he","that hath no beard is less than a man; and he that is more than a","youth is not for me; and he that is less than a man, I am not for him:","therefore I will even take sixpence in earnest of the bear-ward, and","lead his apes into hell."],"id":177,"m":"What would I do with him? Dress him in my clothes and make him my lady's maid? A man with a beard is more than a boy, and a man with no beard is less than a man; and the one who's more than a boy isn't for me, and for the one who's less than a man, I'm not for him. So I'll take sixpence in advance from the bear-keeper and lead his apes into hell, the way old maids are supposed to."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Well then, go you into hell?"],"id":178,"m":"Well then, will you go to hell?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["No; but to the gate; and there will the devil meet me, like an old","cuckold, with horns on his head, and say, 'Get you to heaven,","Beatrice, get you to heaven; here's no place for you maids: 'so","deliver I up my apes, and away to Saint Peter for the heavens; he","shows me where the bachelors sit, and there live we as merry as the","day is long."],"id":179,"m":"No, only to the gate; and there the devil will meet me, like an old cuckold with horns on his head, and say, 'Off you go to heaven, Beatrice, off you go to heaven; there's no place here for you virgins.' So I'll hand over my apes and head off to Saint Peter at the gates of heaven. He'll show me where the bachelors sit, and there we'll live as happily as the day is long."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["[To Hero.] Well, niece, I trust you will be ruled by your father."],"id":180,"m":"Well, niece, I trust you'll be guided by your father."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Yes, faith; it is my cousin's duty to make curtsy, and say,","'Father, as it please you:'— but yet for all that, cousin, let him","be a handsome fellow, or else make another curtsy, and say,","'Father, as it please me.'"],"id":181,"m":"Yes, of course. It's my cousin's duty to curtsy and say, 'Father, as you please.' But all the same, cousin, make sure he's a handsome fellow, or else make another curtsy and say, 'Father, as I please.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Well, niece, I hope to see you one day fitted with a husband."],"id":182,"m":"Well, niece, I hope to see you one day matched with a husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Not till God make men of some other metal than earth. Would it not","grieve a woman to be over-mastered with a piece of valiant dust? to","make an account of her life to a clod of wayward marl? No, uncle,","I'll none: Adam's sons are my brethren; and truly, I hold it a sin","to match in my kinred."],"id":183,"m":"Not until God makes men out of some other material than earth. Wouldn't it upset a woman to be ruled by a lump of swaggering dust? To have to explain her life to a clod of stubborn clay? No, uncle, I won't have one. Adam's sons are my brothers, and I truly think it's a sin to marry a relative."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Daughter, remember what I told you: if the prince do solicit you","in that kind, you know your answer."],"id":184,"m":"Daughter, remember what I told you. If the prince asks you to marry him, you know your answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["The fault will be in the music, cousin, if you be not wooed in good","time: if the prince be too important, tell him there is measure in","everything, and so dance out the answer.","For, hear me, Hero: wooing, wedding, and repenting is as a Scotch jig,","a measure, and a cinque-pace: the first suit is hot and hasty, like a","Scotch jig, and full as fantastical; the wedding, mannerly-modest, as","a measure, full of state and ancientry; and then comes Repentance, and","with his bad legs, falls into the cinque-pace faster and faster, till","he sink into his grave."],"id":185,"m":"It'll be the music's fault, cousin, if you're not wooed in good time. If the prince is too pushy, tell him there's a proper rhythm to everything, and dance your answer out. Because, listen, Hero: wooing, wedding and regretting are like a Scottish jig, a stately dance and a five-step galliard. The proposal is hot and hasty, like a Scottish jig, and just as wild. The wedding is polite and modest, like a stately dance, all ceremony and tradition. And then along comes Regret, and on his bad legs he stumbles into the galliard, faster and faster, until he sinks into his grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Cousin, you apprehend passing shrewdly."],"id":186,"m":"Niece, you see things very sharply."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I have a good eye, uncle: I can see a church by daylight."],"id":187,"m":"I have good eyesight, uncle. I can see a church in broad daylight."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["The revellers are entering, brother: make good room."],"id":188,"m":"The masked dancers are coming in, brother. Make plenty of room."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, DON PEDRO, CLAUDIO, BENEDICK, BALTHASAR, DON JOHN, BORACHIO, MARGARET, URSULA, and Others, masked.","id":189},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Lady, will you walk about with your friend?"],"id":190,"m":"Lady, will you walk around with your admirer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["So you walk softly and look sweetly and say nothing, I am yours","for the walk; and especially when I walk away."],"id":191,"m":"As long as you walk slowly, look sweet and say nothing, I'm yours for the walk; especially when I walk away."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["With me in your company?"],"id":192,"m":"With me as your companion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["I may say so, when I please."],"id":193,"m":"I may say so, when I feel like it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["And when please you to say so?"],"id":194,"m":"And when will you feel like saying so?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["When I like your favour; for God defend the lute should be like","the case!"],"id":195,"m":"When I like your face; for God forbid the lute should look like its case!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["My visor is Philemon's roof; within the house is Jove."],"id":196,"m":"My mask is like the roof of Philemon's humble cottage: inside the house is the god Jove."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Why, then, your visor should be thatch'd."],"id":197,"m":"Then your mask should be thatched, like Philemon's roof."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Speak low, if you speak love.","[Takes her aside.]"],"id":198,"m":"Speak quietly, if you're speaking of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["Well, I would you did like me."],"id":199,"m":"Well, I wish you liked me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["So would not I, for your own sake; for I have many ill qualities."],"id":200,"m":"I wouldn't, for your own sake, because I have many bad qualities."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["Which is one?"],"id":201,"m":"What's one of them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["I say my prayers aloud."],"id":202,"m":"I say my prayers out loud."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["I love you the better; the hearers may cry Amen."],"id":203,"m":"I love you all the more for it; the listeners can say Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["God match me with a good dancer!"],"id":204,"m":"May God pair me with a good dancer!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["Amen."],"id":205,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["And God keep him out of my sight when the dance is done! Answer,","clerk."],"id":206,"m":"And may God keep him out of my sight once the dance is over! Your response, parish clerk."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["No more words: the clerk is answered."],"id":207,"m":"No more words: the clerk has had his answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["I know you well enough: you are Signior Antonio."],"id":208,"m":"I know you well enough. You're Signior Antonio."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["At a word, I am not."],"id":209,"m":"In a word, I'm not."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["I know you by the waggling of your head."],"id":210,"m":"I know you by the way your head wobbles."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["To tell you true, I counterfeit him."],"id":211,"m":"To tell you the truth, I'm imitating him."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["You could never do him so ill-well, unless you were the very man.","Here's his dry hand up and down: you are he, you are he."],"id":212,"m":"You could never copy his faults so perfectly unless you were the man himself. Here's his dry old hand exactly. You're him, you're him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["At a word, I am not."],"id":213,"m":"In a word, I'm not."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Come, come; do you think I do not know you by your excellent wit?","Can virtue hide itself? Go to, mum, you are he: graces will appear,","and there's an end."],"id":214,"m":"Come on, do you think I don't recognise you by your excellent wit? Can goodness hide itself? Go on, say nothing, you're him. Good qualities will always show, and that's that."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Will you not tell me who told you so?"],"id":215,"m":"Won't you tell me who told you that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["No, you shall pardon me."],"id":216,"m":"No, you'll have to excuse me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Nor will you not tell me who you are?"],"id":217,"m":"And you won't tell me who you are either?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Not now."],"id":218,"m":"Not now."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["That I was disdainful, and that I had my good wit out of the","'Hundred Merry Tales.' Well, this was Signior Benedick that said so."],"id":219,"m":"That I was scornful, and that I got my jokes from a cheap joke book. Well, it was Signior Benedick who said that."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["What's he?"],"id":220,"m":"Who's he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I am sure you know him well enough."],"id":221,"m":"I'm sure you know him well enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Not I, believe me."],"id":222,"m":"Not me, believe me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Did he never make you laugh?"],"id":223,"m":"Hasn't he ever made you laugh?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I pray you, what is he?"],"id":224,"m":"Please tell me, who is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Why, he is the prince's jester: a very dull fool; only his gift is in","devising impossible slanders: none but libertines delight in him; and","the commendation is not in his wit, but in his villany; for he both","pleases men and angers them, and then they laugh at him and beat him.","I am sure he is in the fleet: I would he had boarded me!"],"id":225,"m":"Why, he's the prince's jester, a very dull fool. His only talent is inventing unbelievable slanders. Only loose-living men enjoy him, and they like him not for his wit but for his nastiness; for he both pleases men and angers them, so they laugh at him and then beat him. I'm sure he's in the fleet of dancers here. I wish he had sailed up to me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["When I know the gentleman, I'll tell him what you say."],"id":226,"m":"When I meet the gentleman, I'll tell him what you said."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Do, do: he'll but break a comparison or two on me; which,","peradventure not marked or not laughed at, strikes him into melancholy;","and then there's a partridge wing saved, for the fool will eat no","supper that night.","[Music within.] We must follow the leaders."],"id":227,"m":"Do, do. He'll only fire off a comparison or two about me; and if nobody notices or laughs, it'll throw him into a sulk; and then a partridge wing is saved, because the fool won't eat any supper that night. We must follow the leaders."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["In every good thing."],"id":228,"m":"In every good thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Nay, if they lead to any ill, I will leave them at the next turning."],"id":229,"m":"No, if they lead to anything bad, I'll leave them at the next turning."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dance. Then exeunt all but DON JOHN, BORACHIO, and CLAUDIO.","id":230},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Sure my brother is amorous on Hero, and hath withdrawn her father","to break with him about it. The ladies follow her and but one visor","remains."],"id":231,"m":"My brother is clearly in love with Hero and has taken her father aside to discuss it. The ladies have gone with her, and only one masked man is left."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["And that is Claudio: I know him by his bearing."],"id":232,"m":"And that's Claudio. I know him by the way he carries himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Are you not Signior Benedick?"],"id":233,"m":"Aren't you Signior Benedick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["You know me well; I am he."],"id":234,"m":"You know me well; I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Signior, you are very near my brother in his love: he is enamoured","on Hero; I pray you, dissuade him from her; she is no equal for his","birth: you may do the part of an honest man in it."],"id":235,"m":"Signior, you're very close to my brother. He's in love with Hero. Please talk him out of it; she's not his equal in birth. You'd be acting as an honest man in doing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["How know you he loves her?"],"id":236,"m":"How do you know he loves her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I heard him swear his affection."],"id":237,"m":"I heard him swear his love."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["So did I too; and he swore he would marry her to-night."],"id":238,"m":"So did I, and he swore he would marry her tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Come, let us to the banquet."],"id":239,"m":"Come, let's go to the banquet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt DON JOHN and BORACHIO.","id":240},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Thus answer I in name of Benedick,","But hear these ill news with the ears of Claudio.","'Tis certain so; the prince wooes for himself.","Friendship is constant in all other things","Save in the office and affairs of love:","herefore all hearts in love use their own tongues;","Let every eye negotiate for itself","And trust no agent; for beauty is a witch","Against whose charms faith melteth into blood.","This is an accident of hourly proof,","Which I mistrusted not. Farewell, therefore, Hero!"],"id":241,"m":"I answered like that pretending to be Benedick, but I hear this bad news with Claudio's ears. It's definitely true: the prince is wooing for himself. Friendship is loyal in everything except the business of love. So all lovers should use their own tongues. Let every eye negotiate for itself and trust no go-between; for beauty is a witch whose spells melt loyalty into lust. This happens every hour of the day, and I never suspected it. So goodbye, Hero!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Benedick.","id":242},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Count Claudio?"],"id":243,"m":"Count Claudio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Yea, the same."],"id":244,"m":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Come, will you go with me?"],"id":245,"m":"Come on, will you come with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Whither?"],"id":246,"m":"Where to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Even to the next willow, about your own business, count. What fashion","will you wear the garland of? About your neck, like a usurer's chain?","or under your arm, like a lieutenant's scarf? You must wear it one way,","for the prince hath got your Hero."],"id":247,"m":"Just to the nearest willow tree, count, on your own business. How will you wear your willow garland of a rejected lover? Round your neck like a moneylender's gold chain? Or under your arm like an officer's sash? You'll have to wear it somehow, because the prince has got your Hero."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I wish him joy of her."],"id":248,"m":"I hope she makes him happy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Why, that's spoken like an honest drovier: so they sell bullocks.","But did you think the prince would have served you thus?"],"id":249,"m":"Why, that's spoken like an honest cattle dealer; that's what they say when they sell bullocks. But did you think the prince would treat you like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I pray you, leave me."],"id":250,"m":"Please leave me alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Ho! now you strike like the blind man: 'twas the boy that stole","your meat, and you'll beat the post."],"id":251,"m":"Ho! Now you're hitting out like the blind man: it was the boy who stole your meat, and you're beating the post."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If it will not be, I'll leave you."],"id":252,"m":"If you won't go, I'll leave you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":253},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Alas! poor hurt fowl. Now will he creep into sedges. But, that my Lady","Beatrice should know me, and not know me! The prince's fool! Ha! it","may be I go under that title because I am merry. Yea, but so I am apt","to do myself wrong; I am not so reputed: it is the base though bitter","disposition of Beatrice that puts the world into her person, and so","gives me out. Well, I'll be revenged as I may."],"id":254,"m":"Poor wounded bird! Now he'll creep off into the reeds. But that my Lady Beatrice should know me and not know me! The prince's fool! Ha! Maybe I'm called that because I'm cheerful. Yes, but I'm being unfair to myself; that's not my reputation. It's Beatrice's low, bitter nature that makes her think she speaks for the whole world, and so she reports me that way. Well, I'll get my revenge however I can."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Don Pedro.","id":255},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Now, signior, where's the count? Did you see him?"],"id":256,"m":"Now, signior, where's the count? Have you seen him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Troth, my lord, I have played the part of Lady Fame. I found him here","as melancholy as a lodge in a warren. I told him, and I think I told","him true, that your Grace had got the good will of this young lady;","and I offered him my company to a willow tree, either to make him a","garland, as being forsaken, or to bind him up a rod, as being worthy","to be whipped."],"id":257,"m":"Honestly, my lord, I've played the part of Rumour. I found him here as gloomy as a gamekeeper's lonely hut. I told him, and I think I told him the truth, that your Grace had won this young lady's love. And I offered to take him to a willow tree, either to make him a garland because he's been jilted, or to make him a switch, because he deserves to be whipped."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["To be whipped! What's his fault?"],"id":258,"m":"To be whipped! What has he done wrong?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["The flat transgression of a school-boy, who, being overjoy'd with","finding a bird's nest, shows it his companion, and he steals it."],"id":259,"m":"The plain offence of a schoolboy who's so delighted at finding a bird's nest that he shows it to his friend, and the friend steals it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Wilt thou make a trust a transgression? The transgression is in","the stealer."],"id":260,"m":"Would you call trusting someone an offence? The offence is the thief's."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Yet it had not been amiss the rod had been made, and the garland too;","for the garland he might have worn himself, and the rod he might have","bestowed on you, who, as I take it, have stolen his bird's nest."],"id":261,"m":"Still, it wouldn't have been wrong to make the switch and the garland too. He could have worn the garland himself and given the switch to you, since, as I see it, you've stolen his bird's nest."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I will but teach them to sing, and restore them to the owner."],"id":262,"m":"I'll only teach the birds to sing and then give them back to their owner."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["If their singing answer your saying, by my faith, you say honestly."],"id":263,"m":"If their singing matches your words, then honestly, you're telling the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["The Lady Beatrice hath a quarrel to you: the gentleman that danced","with her told her she is much wronged by you."],"id":264,"m":"The Lady Beatrice has a quarrel with you. The gentleman who danced with her told her you've insulted her badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["O! she misused me past the endurance of a block: an oak but with one","green leaf on it, would have answered her: my very visor began to","assume life and scold with her.","She told me, not thinking I had been myself, that I was the prince's","jester, that I was duller than a great thaw; huddling jest upon jest","with such impossible conveyance upon me, that I stood like a man at a","mark, with a whole army shooting at me.","She speaks poniards, and every word stabs: if her breath were as","terrible as her terminations, there were no living near her; she would","infect to the north star. I would not marry her, though she were","endowed with all that Adam had left him before he transgressed: she","would have made Hercules have turned spit, yea, and have cleft his","club to make the fire too.","Come, talk not of her; you shall find her the infernal Ate in good","apparel. I would to God some scholar would conjure her, for certainly,","while she is here, a man may live as quiet in hell as in a sanctuary;","and people sin upon purpose because they would go thither; so indeed,","all disquiet, horror and perturbation follow her."],"id":265,"m":"Oh, she abused me more than a block of wood could stand! An oak tree with only one green leaf left would have answered her back. Even my mask began to come alive and scold her. Not realising it was me, she told me I was the prince's jester and duller than a muddy thaw, piling joke on joke on me so fast and so unbelievably that I stood like a man at a target with a whole army shooting at me. She speaks daggers, and every word stabs. If her breath were as terrible as her words, nobody could live near her; she'd poison everything as far as the North Star. I wouldn't marry her even if she came with everything Adam owned before he sinned. She'd have made Hercules turn the roasting spit in her kitchen, yes, and split his club for firewood too. Come on, don't talk about her. You'll find she's Ate, the goddess of discord, in fine clothes. I wish to God some scholar would exorcise her; for while she's here, a man could live as peacefully in hell as in a church, and people sin on purpose so as to go there. So really, all trouble, horror and chaos follow her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter CLAUDIO, BEATRICE, HERO, and LEONATO.","id":266},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Look! here she comes."],"id":267,"m":"Look! Here she comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Will your Grace command me any service to the world's end? I will go","on the slightest errand now to the Antipodes that you can devise to","send me on; I will fetch you a toothpicker now from the furthest inch","of Asia; bring you the length of Prester John's foot; fetch you a hair","off the Great Cham's beard; do you any embassage to the Pygmies,","rather than hold three words' conference with this harpy. You have no","employment for me?"],"id":268,"m":"Has your Grace got any job for me at the end of the world? I'll go on the most trivial errand you can think of to the other side of the globe. I'll fetch you a toothpick from the furthest corner of Asia, bring you the size of the legendary Prester John's foot, fetch you a hair from the Emperor of China's beard, or go as your ambassador to the Pygmies, rather than have a three-word conversation with this monster. You've no task for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["None, but to desire your good company."],"id":269,"m":"None, except to enjoy your good company."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["O God, sir, here's a dish I love not: I cannot endure my Lady Tongue."],"id":270,"m":"Oh God, sir, here's a dish I don't like. I can't stand my Lady Tongue."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":271},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Come, lady, come; you have lost the heart of Signior Benedick."],"id":272,"m":"Come, lady, come. You've lost Signior Benedick's heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Indeed, my lord, he lent it me awhile; and I gave him use for it, a","double heart for a single one: marry, once before he won it of me with","false dice, therefore your Grace may well say I have lost it."],"id":273,"m":"Well, my lord, he lent it to me for a while, and I paid him interest on it: a double heart for his single one. In fact, he once won it from me with loaded dice, so your Grace may well say I've lost it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["You have put him down, lady, you have put him down."],"id":274,"m":"You've put him down, lady, you've put him down."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["So I would not he should do me, my lord, lest I should prove the mother","of fools. I have brought Count Claudio, whom you sent me to seek."],"id":275,"m":"I wouldn't want him to put me down, my lord, in case I became the mother of fools. I've brought Count Claudio, whom you sent me to find."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Why, how now, count! wherefore are you sad?"],"id":276,"m":"Well, count! Why are you sad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Not sad, my lord."],"id":277,"m":"I'm not sad, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["How then? Sick?"],"id":278,"m":"What, then? Ill?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Neither, my lord."],"id":279,"m":"Neither, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["The count is neither sad, nor sick, nor merry, nor well; but civil","count, civil as an orange, and something of that jealous complexion."],"id":280,"m":"The count is neither sad, nor ill, nor cheerful, nor well; just civil, as civil as a Seville orange, and with a touch of that jealous yellow colour."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I' faith, lady, I think your blazon to be true; though, I'll be sworn,","if he be so, his conceit is false. Here, Claudio, I have wooed in thy","name, and fair Hero is won; I have broke with her father, and, his good","will obtained; name the day of marriage, and God give thee joy!"],"id":281,"m":"Truly, lady, I think your description is accurate; though I swear, if he is jealous, his idea is wrong. Here, Claudio, I've wooed in your name, and lovely Hero is won. I've spoken with her father and got his consent. Name the wedding day, and God give you joy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Count, take of me my daughter, and with her my fortunes: his","Grace hath made the match, and all grace say Amen to it!"],"id":282,"m":"Count, take my daughter from me, and with her my fortune. His Grace has made the match, and may all grace say Amen to it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Speak, Count, 'tis your cue."],"id":283,"m":"Speak, count; it's your cue."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Silence is the perfectest herald of joy: I were but little happy, if I","could say how much. Lady, as you are mine, I am yours: I give away myself for","you and dote upon the exchange."],"id":284,"m":"Silence is the best messenger of joy. I'd be only a little happy if I could say how happy I am. Lady, as you are mine, I am yours. I give myself away for you and adore the exchange."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Speak, cousin; or, if you cannot, stop his mouth with a kiss, and let","not him speak neither."],"id":285,"m":"Speak, cousin. Or if you can't, stop his mouth with a kiss, and don't let him speak either."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["In faith, lady, you have a merry heart."],"id":286,"m":"Truly, lady, you have a cheerful heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Yea, my lord; I thank it, poor fool, it keeps on the windy side of care.","My cousin tells him in his ear that he is in her heart."],"id":287,"m":"Yes, my lord, and I thank it, the poor fool; it keeps upwind of worry. My cousin is whispering in his ear that he's in her heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And so she doth, cousin."],"id":288,"m":"And so she is, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Good Lord, for alliance! Thus goes every one to the world but I, and I","am sunburnt. I may sit in a corner and cry heigh-ho for a husband!"],"id":289,"m":"Good Lord, another marriage! So everyone gets married except me, and I'm left out in the sun. I can sit in a corner and sigh, 'Oh dear, I want a husband!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Lady Beatrice, I will get you one."],"id":290,"m":"Lady Beatrice, I'll get you one."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I would rather have one of your father's getting. Hath your Grace ne'er","a brother like you? Your father got excellent husbands, if a maid could","come by them."],"id":291,"m":"I'd rather have one your father got. Hasn't your Grace got a brother like you? Your father made excellent husbands, if a girl could get one of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Will you have me, lady?"],"id":292,"m":"Will you have me, lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["No, my lord, unless I might have another for working days: your Grace","is too costly to wear every day. But, I beseech your Grace, pardon me;","I was born to speak all mirth and no matter."],"id":293,"m":"No, my lord, unless I could have another one for working days; your Grace is too expensive to wear every day. But, I beg your Grace, forgive me. I was born to speak nothing but jokes and no sense."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Your silence most offends me, and to be merry best becomes you; for out","of question, you were born in a merry hour."],"id":294,"m":"Your silence would offend me most, and being merry suits you best; for there's no doubt you were born at a happy hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["No, sure, my lord, my mother cried; but then there was a star danced,","and under that was I born. Cousins, God give you joy!"],"id":295,"m":"No, surely, my lord, my mother cried; but then a star danced, and I was born under it. Cousins, God give you joy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Niece, will you look to those things I told you of?"],"id":296,"m":"Niece, will you see to those things I told you about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I cry you mercy, uncle. By your Grace's pardon."],"id":297,"m":"I beg your pardon, uncle. Excuse me, your Grace."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":298},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["By my troth, a pleasant spirited lady."],"id":299,"m":"Upon my word, a lively, cheerful lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["There's little of the melancholy element in her, my lord: she is never","sad but when she sleeps; and not ever sad then, for I have heard my","daughter say, she hath often dreamed of unhappiness and waked herself","with laughing."],"id":300,"m":"There's very little sadness in her, my lord. She's only serious when she's asleep, and not always then, because I've heard my daughter say she has often had sad dreams and woken herself up laughing."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["She cannot endure to hear tell of a husband."],"id":301,"m":"She can't stand hearing about a husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["O! by no means: she mocks all her wooers out of suit."],"id":302,"m":"Oh, not at all. She mocks all her suitors until they give up."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["She were an excellent wife for Benedick."],"id":303,"m":"She would make Benedick an excellent wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["O Lord! my lord, if they were but a week married, they would talk","themselves mad."],"id":304,"m":"Oh Lord, my lord, if they were married just a week they'd talk each other mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Count Claudio, when mean you to go to church?"],"id":305,"m":"Count Claudio, when do you plan to go to church?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["To-morrow, my lord. Time goes on crutches till love have all his rites."],"id":306,"m":"Tomorrow, my lord. Time walks on crutches until love has had all its ceremonies."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Not till Monday, my dear son, which is hence a just seven-night; and a","time too brief too, to have all things answer my mind."],"id":307,"m":"Not until Monday, my dear son, which is exactly a week from now; and even that's too short a time to get everything as I want it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Come, you shake the head at so long a breathing; but, I warrant thee,","Claudio, the time shall not go dully by us. I will in the interim","undertake one of Hercules' labours, which is, to bring Signior","Benedick and the Lady Beatrice into a mountain of affection the one","with the other.","I would fain have it a match; and I doubt not but to fashion it, if","you three will but minister such assistance as I shall give you","direction."],"id":308,"m":"Come, you shake your head at such a long wait, but I promise you, Claudio, the time won't pass slowly for us. Meanwhile I'll take on one of the labours of Hercules: to bring Signior Benedick and the Lady Beatrice into a mountain of love for each other. I'd love to make it a match, and I'm sure I can arrange it, if you three will give whatever help I direct."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["My lord, I am for you, though it cost me ten nights' watchings."],"id":309,"m":"My lord, I'm with you, even if it costs me ten sleepless nights."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And I, my lord."],"id":310,"m":"And so am I, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["And you too, gentle Hero?"],"id":311,"m":"And you too, gentle Hero?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["I will do any modest office, my lord, to help my cousin to a good","husband."],"id":312,"m":"I'll do anything proper, my lord, to help my cousin to a good husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["And Benedick is not the unhopefullest husband that I know. Thus far","can I praise him; he is of a noble strain, of approved valour, and","confirmed honesty.","I will teach you how to humour your cousin, that she shall fall in","love with Benedick; and I, with your two helps, will so practise on","Benedick that, in despite of his quick wit and his queasy stomach, he","shall fall in love with Beatrice. If we can do this, Cupid is no","longer an archer: his glory shall be ours, for we are the only","love-gods. Go in with me, and I will tell you my drift."],"id":313,"m":"And Benedick is not the most hopeless husband I know. I can praise him this far: he's of noble family, of proven courage and of tested honesty. I'll teach you how to handle your cousin so that she falls in love with Benedick; and I, with the help of you two, will work on Benedick so that, despite his quick wit and his weak stomach for marriage, he'll fall in love with Beatrice. If we can do this, Cupid is no longer the archer. His glory will be ours, for we'll be the only gods of love. Come in with me, and I'll tell you my plan."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":314},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Another room in Leonato's house.","id":315},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON JOHN and BORACHIO.","id":316},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["It is so; the Count Claudio shall marry the daughter of Leonato."],"id":317,"m":"So it's true. Count Claudio will marry Leonato's daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Yea, my lord; but I can cross it."],"id":318,"m":"Yes, my lord, but I can block it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Any bar, any cross, any impediment will be medicinable to me: I am","sick in displeasure to him, and whatsoever comes athwart his affection","ranges evenly with mine. How canst thou cross this marriage?"],"id":319,"m":"Any barrier, any block, any obstacle will be medicine to me. I'm sick with hatred for him, and whatever cuts across his desires suits mine exactly. How can you block this marriage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Not honestly, my lord; but so covertly that no dishonesty shall appear","in me."],"id":320,"m":"Not honestly, my lord, but so secretly that I won't seem dishonest at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Show me briefly how."],"id":321,"m":"Show me how, briefly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["I think I told your lordship, a year since, how much I am in the","favour of Margaret, the waiting-gentlewoman to Hero."],"id":322,"m":"I think I told your lordship a year ago how much Margaret, Hero's lady's maid, likes me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I remember."],"id":323,"m":"I remember."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["I can, at any unseasonable instant of the night, appoint her to look","out at her lady's chamber window."],"id":324,"m":"I can arrange for her, at any unlikely hour of the night, to look out of her lady's bedroom window."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["What life is in that, to be the death of this marriage?"],"id":325,"m":"What life is there in that, to be the death of this marriage?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["The poison of that lies in you to temper. Go you to the prince your","brother; spare not to tell him, that he hath wronged his honour in","marrying the renowned Claudio,—whose estimation do you mightily hold","up,—to a contaminated stale, such a one as Hero."],"id":326,"m":"It's up to you to mix the poison. Go to the prince your brother. Don't hold back from telling him that he has damaged his honour by marrying the famous Claudio, whom you must praise to the skies, to a filthy whore like Hero."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["What proof shall I make of that?"],"id":327,"m":"What proof will I give of that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Proof enough to misuse the prince, to vex Claudio, to undo Hero,","and kill Leonato. Look you for any other issue?"],"id":328,"m":"Proof enough to deceive the prince, torment Claudio, ruin Hero and kill Leonato. Do you want any other outcome?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Only to despite them, I will endeavour anything."],"id":329,"m":"Just to spite them, I'll try anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Go then; find me a meet hour to draw Don Pedro and the Count Claudio","alone: tell them that you know that Hero loves me; intend a kind of","zeal both to the prince and Claudio, as—in love of your brother's","honour, who hath made this match, and his friend's reputation, who is","thus like to be cozened with the semblance of a maid,—that you have","discovered thus.","They will scarcely believe this without trial: offer them instances,","which shall bear no less likelihood than to see me at her","chamber-window, hear me call Margaret Hero, hear Margaret term me","Claudio; and bring them to see this the very night before the intended","wedding: for in the meantime I will so fashion the matter that Hero","shall be absent; and there shall appear such seeming truth of Hero's","disloyalty, that jealousy shall be called assurance, and all the","preparation overthrown."],"id":330,"m":"Go then. Find me a suitable time to get Don Pedro and Count Claudio alone. Tell them you know that Hero loves me. Pretend to be full of concern for both the prince and Claudio, as if, out of love for your brother's honour, since he made this match, and for his friend's reputation, since he's about to be cheated with something that only looks like a virgin, you've found this out. They'll hardly believe it without proof. Offer them evidence, no less convincing than seeing me at her bedroom window, hearing me call Margaret 'Hero' and hearing Margaret call me 'Claudio'. Bring them to see this on the very night before the wedding. In the meantime I'll arrange things so that Hero is away, and there'll be such convincing evidence of Hero's unfaithfulness that suspicion will be taken for certainty, and all the wedding plans overturned."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Grow this to what adverse issue it can, I will put it in practice.","Be cunning in the working this, and thy fee is a thousand ducats."],"id":331,"m":"However badly this turns out, I'll put it into action. Be clever in carrying it out, and your fee is a thousand ducats."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Be you constant in the accusation, and my cunning shall not shame me."],"id":332,"m":"You stick firmly to the accusation, and my cleverness won't let me down."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I will presently go learn their day of marriage."],"id":333,"m":"I'll go straight away and find out their wedding day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":334},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"Leonato's Garden.","id":335},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Benedick.","id":336},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Boy!"],"id":337,"m":"Boy!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Boy.","id":338},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Signior?"],"id":339,"m":"Signior?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["In my chamber-window lies a book; bring it hither to me in the","orchard."],"id":340,"m":"There's a book on my bedroom windowsill. Bring it here to me in the orchard."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["I am here already, sir."],"id":341,"m":"I'm here already, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I know that; but I would have thee hence, and here again."],"id":342,"m":"I know that, but I want you to go away and come back here again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Boy.","id":343},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","cont":1,"lines":["I do much wonder that one man, seeing how much another man is a fool","when he dedicates his behaviours to love, will, after he hath laughed","at such shallow follies in others, become the argument of his own","scorn by falling in love: and such a man is Claudio.","I have known, when there was no music with him but the drum and the","fife; and now had he rather hear the tabor and the pipe: I have known","when he would have walked ten mile afoot to see a good armour; and now","will he lie ten nights awake, carving the fashion of a new doublet.","He was wont to speak plain and to the purpose, like an honest man and","a soldier; and now is he turned orthography; his words are a very","fantastical banquet, just so many strange dishes. May I be so","converted, and see with these eyes?","I cannot tell; I think not: I will not be sworn but love may transform","me to an oyster; but I'll take my oath on it, till he have made an","oyster of me, he shall never make me such a fool. One woman is fair,","yet I am well; another is wise, yet I am well; another virtuous, yet I","am well; but till all graces be in one woman, one woman shall not come","in my grace.","Rich she shall be, that's certain; wise, or I'll none; virtuous, or","I'll never cheapen her; fair, or I'll never look on her; mild, or come","not near me; noble, or not I for an angel; of good discourse, an","excellent musician, and her hair shall be of what colour it please","God. Ha! the prince and Monsieur Love! I will hide me in the arbour."],"id":344,"m":"It amazes me that one man, seeing what a fool another man is when he devotes himself to love, will laugh at such silly behaviour in others and then become the object of his own scorn by falling in love. And Claudio is just such a man. I've known a time when the only music he liked was the military drum and fife; now he'd rather hear the little drum and pipe of a village dance. I've known a time when he'd have walked ten miles to see a good suit of armour; now he'll lie awake ten nights designing the style of a new jacket. He used to speak plainly and to the point, like an honest man and a soldier; now he's turned into a dictionary. His words are a very fancy banquet, so many exotic dishes. Could I ever be changed like that and see with these same eyes? I can't say; I don't think so. I won't swear that love couldn't turn me into an oyster; but I'll swear this: until he's made an oyster of me, he'll never make such a fool of me. One woman is beautiful, yet I'm fine; another is clever, yet I'm fine; another is good, yet I'm fine. But until all the virtues are in one woman, no woman will win my favour. She'll be rich, that's certain; clever, or I won't have her; good, or I won't even bargain for her; beautiful, or I won't look at her; gentle, or she'd better not come near me; noble, or I wouldn't take her for a gold coin; a good talker, an excellent musician, and her hair can be whatever colour God likes. Ha! The prince and Monsieur Love! I'll hide in the arbour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Withdraws.","id":345},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON PEDRO, LEONATO, and CLAUDIO, followed by BALTHAZAR and Musicians.","id":346},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Come, shall we hear this music?"],"id":347,"m":"Come, shall we listen to this music?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Yea, my good lord.","How still the evening is,","As hush'd on purpose to grace harmony!"],"id":348,"m":"Yes, my good lord. How still the evening is, as if it were hushed on purpose to show off the music!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["See you where Benedick hath hid himself?"],"id":349,"m":"Do you see where Benedick has hidden himself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O! very well, my lord: the music ended,","We'll fit the kid-fox with a penny-worth."],"id":350,"m":"Oh, very well, my lord. When the music's over, we'll give this sly young fox more than he bargained for."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Come, Balthazar, we'll hear that song again."],"id":351,"m":"Come, Balthazar, let's hear that song again."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["O! good my lord, tax not so bad a voice","To slander music any more than once."],"id":352,"m":"Oh, my good lord, don't make such a bad voice insult music more than once."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["It is the witness still of excellency,","To put a strange face on his own perfection.","I pray thee, sing, and let me woo no more."],"id":353,"m":"It's always the sign of real excellence to pretend not to recognise its own perfection. Please sing, and don't make me woo you any longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["Because you talk of wooing, I will sing;","Since many a wooer doth commence his suit","To her he thinks not worthy; yet he wooes;","Yet will he swear he loves."],"id":354,"m":"Since you talk of wooing, I'll sing; for many a wooer starts courting a woman he doesn't think worthy, and yet he woos; and yet he'll swear he loves her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Nay, pray thee come;","Or if thou wilt hold longer argument,","Do it in notes."],"id":355,"m":"No, please, come on; or if you want to keep arguing, do it in musical notes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["Note this before my notes;","There's not a note of mine that's worth the noting."],"id":356,"m":"Note this before my notes: there's not a note of mine worth noting."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Why these are very crotchets that he speaks;","Notes, notes, forsooth, and nothing!"],"id":357,"m":"Why, he's talking in crotchets: quarter notes and quirks! Notes, notes, indeed, and nothing!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Music.","id":358},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Now, divine air! now is his soul ravished! Is it not strange that","sheep's guts should hale souls out of men's bodies? Well, a horn for","my money, when all's done."],"id":359,"m":"Now, heavenly music! Now his soul is carried away! Isn't it strange that sheep's guts strung on a lute can drag souls out of men's bodies? Well, give me a hunting horn, when all's said and done."},{"t":"sd","text":"Balthasar sings.","id":360},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","cont":1,"lines":["~Sigh no more, ladies, sigh no more,","~Men were deceivers ever;","~One foot in sea, and one on shore,","~To one thing constant never.","~Then sigh not so,","~But let them go,","~And be you blithe and bonny,","~Converting all your sounds of woe","~Into Hey nonny, nonny.","~Sing no more ditties, sing no mo","~Of dumps so dull and heavy;","~The fraud of men was ever so,","~Since summer first was leavy.","~Then sigh not so,","~But let them go,","~And be you blithe and bonny,","~Converting all your sounds of woe","~Into Hey nonny, nonny."],"id":361,"m":"Sigh no more, ladies, sigh no more; men were always deceivers. With one foot in the sea and one on the shore, they're never faithful to one thing. So don't sigh like that, but let them go, and be happy and pretty, turning all your sounds of sadness into 'Hey nonny, nonny.' Sing no more songs, sing no more, of gloomy, heavy moods. Men have always cheated like this, ever since the first summer had leaves. So don't sigh like that, but let them go, and be happy and pretty, turning all your sounds of sadness into 'Hey nonny, nonny.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["By my troth, a good song."],"id":362,"m":"Upon my word, a good song."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["And an ill singer, my lord."],"id":363,"m":"And a bad singer, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Ha, no, no, faith; thou singest well enough for a shift."],"id":364,"m":"Ha, no, no, really. You sing well enough when needed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["[Aside.] An he had been a dog that should have howled thus, they would","have hanged him; and I pray God his bad voice bode no mischief. I had","as lief have heard the night-raven, come what plague could have come","after it."],"id":365,"m":"If a dog had howled like that, they'd have hanged it; and I pray to God his bad voice isn't an omen of trouble. I'd rather have heard the night-raven, whatever plague might have followed it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Yea, marry; dost thou hear, Balthazar? I pray thee, get us some","excellent music, for to-morrow night we would have it at the Lady","Hero's chamber-window."],"id":366,"m":"Yes, indeed. Are you listening, Balthazar? Please get us some excellent music, because tomorrow night we want to play it under the Lady Hero's bedroom window."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["The best I can, my lord."],"id":367,"m":"The best I can, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Do so: farewell."],"id":368,"m":"Do that. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt BALTHAZAR and Musicians.","id":369},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","cont":1,"lines":["Come hither, Leonato: what was it you told me of to-day, that your","niece Beatrice was in love with Signior Benedick?"],"id":370,"m":"Come here, Leonato. What was it you told me about today, that your niece Beatrice is in love with Signior Benedick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O! ay:—","[Aside to DON PEDRO] Stalk on, stalk on; the fowl sits. I did never","think that lady would have loved any man."],"id":371,"m":"Oh yes. Keep stalking, keep stalking; the bird has settled. I never thought that lady would love any man."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["No, nor I neither; but most wonderful that she should so dote on","Signior Benedick, whom she hath in all outward behaviours seemed ever","to abhor."],"id":372,"m":"No, nor did I. But the most amazing thing is that she should be so infatuated with Signior Benedick, whom she has always seemed to loathe, judging by her behaviour."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["[Aside.] Is't possible? Sits the wind in that corner?"],"id":373,"m":"Is it possible? Is that the way the wind is blowing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["By my troth, my lord, I cannot tell what to think of it but that she","loves him with an enraged affection: it is past the infinite of thought."],"id":374,"m":"Honestly, my lord, I don't know what to think, except that she loves him with a wild passion. It's beyond all imagining."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["May be she doth but counterfeit."],"id":375,"m":"Perhaps she's only pretending."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Faith, like enough."],"id":376,"m":"Really, that's quite likely."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["O God! counterfeit! There was never counterfeit of passion came so near","the life of passion as she discovers it."],"id":377,"m":"Oh God, pretending! No pretended passion ever came as close to real passion as hers does."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Why, what effects of passion shows she?"],"id":378,"m":"Why, what signs of passion does she show?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["[Aside.] Bait the hook well: this fish will bite."],"id":379,"m":"Bait the hook well; this fish will bite."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["What effects, my lord? She will sit you; [To Claudio.] You heard","my daughter tell you how."],"id":380,"m":"What signs, my lord? She'll sit there, you heard my daughter tell you how."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["She did, indeed."],"id":381,"m":"She did, indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["How, how, I pray you? You amaze me: I would have thought her spirit","had been invincible against all assaults of affection."],"id":382,"m":"How, how, please tell me? You amaze me. I'd have thought her spirit was invincible against every attack of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I would have sworn it had, my lord; especially against Benedick."],"id":383,"m":"I'd have sworn it was, my lord, especially against Benedick."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["[Aside] I should think this a gull, but that the white-bearded","fellow speaks it: knavery cannot, sure, hide itself in such reverence."],"id":384,"m":"I'd think this was a trick, except that the white-bearded old man is saying it. Surely villainy can't hide behind someone so respectable."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["[Aside.] He hath ta'en the infection: hold it up."],"id":385,"m":"He's caught the infection. Keep it up."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Hath she made her affection known to Benedick?"],"id":386,"m":"Has she told Benedick about her love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["No; and swears she never will: that's her torment."],"id":387,"m":"No, and she swears she never will. That's her torment."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Tis true, indeed; so your daughter says: 'Shall I,' says she, 'that","have so oft encountered him with scorn, write to him that I love him?'"],"id":388,"m":"It's true, indeed; that's what your daughter says. 'Shall I,' she says, 'who have so often met him with scorn, write to him that I love him?'"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["This says she now when she is beginning to write to him; for she'll","be up twenty times a night, and there will she sit in her smock till","she have writ a sheet of paper: my daughter tells us all."],"id":389,"m":"That's what she says now, when she's starting to write to him. She'll get up twenty times a night and sit there in her nightdress until she's written a whole sheet of paper. My daughter tells us everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Now you talk of a sheet of paper, I remember a pretty jest your","daughter told us of."],"id":390,"m":"Now that you talk of a sheet of paper, I remember a funny joke your daughter told us."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["O! when she had writ it, and was reading it over, she found","Benedick and Beatrice between the sheet?"],"id":391,"m":"Oh, when she'd written it and was reading it over, she found 'Benedick' and 'Beatrice' lying together between the sheet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["That."],"id":392,"m":"That's the one."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["O! she tore the letter into a thousand halfpence; railed at herself,","that she should be so immodest to write to one that she knew would","flout her: 'I measure him,' says she, 'by my own spirit; for I should","flout him, if he writ to me; yea, though I love him, I should.'"],"id":393,"m":"Oh, she tore the letter into a thousand tiny pieces and scolded herself for being so shameless as to write to someone she knew would mock her. 'I judge him,' she says, 'by my own spirit; because I would mock him if he wrote to me; yes, even though I love him, I would.'"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Then down upon her knees she falls, weeps, sobs, beats her heart, tears","her hair, prays, curses; 'O sweet Benedick! God give me patience!'"],"id":394,"m":"Then down she falls on her knees, weeps, sobs, beats her breast, tears her hair, prays and curses: 'Oh sweet Benedick! God give me patience!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["She doth indeed; my daughter says so; and the ecstasy hath so much","overborne her, that my daughter is sometimes afeard she will do a","desperate outrage to herself. It is very true."],"id":395,"m":"She does indeed, my daughter says so. And the frenzy has overcome her so much that my daughter is sometimes afraid she'll do something desperate to herself. It's quite true."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["It were good that Benedick knew of it by some other, if she will","not discover it."],"id":396,"m":"It would be good if Benedick heard about it from someone else, if she won't reveal it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["To what end? he would make but a sport of it and torment the poor","lady worse."],"id":397,"m":"What for? He'd only make fun of it and torment the poor lady even more."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["An he should, it were an alms to hang him. She's an excellent sweet","lady, and, out of all suspicion, she is virtuous."],"id":398,"m":"If he did, it would be a good deed to hang him. She's an excellent, sweet lady and, beyond all suspicion, virtuous."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And she is exceeding wise."],"id":399,"m":"And she's extremely clever."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["In everything but in loving Benedick."],"id":400,"m":"In everything except loving Benedick."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["O! my lord, wisdom and blood combating in so tender a body, we have","ten proofs to one that blood hath the victory. I am sorry for her, as","I have just cause, being her uncle and her guardian."],"id":401,"m":"Oh, my lord, when reason and passion fight in such a young body, the odds are ten to one that passion wins. I'm sorry for her, as I've every reason to be, being her uncle and guardian."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I would she had bestowed this dotage on me; I would have daffed all","other respects and made her half myself. I pray you, tell Benedick of","it, and hear what a' will say."],"id":402,"m":"I wish she'd lavished this adoration on me. I'd have brushed aside every other consideration and made her my wife. Please tell Benedick about it, and hear what he says."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Were it good, think you?"],"id":403,"m":"Do you think that would be good?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Hero thinks surely she will die; for she says she will die if he love","her not, and she will die ere she make her love known, and she will die","if he woo her, rather than she will bate one breath of her accustomed","crossness."],"id":404,"m":"Hero is sure she'll die. She says she'll die if he doesn't love her, and she'll die before she reveals her love, and she'll die if he woos her rather than give up one breath of her usual contrariness."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["She doth well: if she should make tender of her love, 'tis very","possible he'll scorn it; for the man,—as you know all,—hath a","contemptible spirit."],"id":405,"m":"She's right. If she offered him her love, it's very likely he'd scorn it; for the man, as you all know, has a scornful nature."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["He is a very proper man."],"id":406,"m":"He's a very handsome man."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["He hath indeed a good outward happiness."],"id":407,"m":"He does indeed have good looks."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Fore God, and in my mind, very wise."],"id":408,"m":"By God, and in my opinion, very clever."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["He doth indeed show some sparks that are like wit."],"id":409,"m":"He does indeed show some sparks that look like wit."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And I take him to be valiant."],"id":410,"m":"And I believe he's brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["As Hector, I assure you: and in the managing of quarrels you may say","he is wise; for either he avoids them with great discretion, or","undertakes them with a most Christian-like fear."],"id":411,"m":"As brave as Hector, I promise you. And in handling quarrels you could say he's wise, because either he avoids them very sensibly, or he takes them on with a most Christian fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["If he do fear God, a' must necessarily keep peace: if he break the","peace, he ought to enter into a quarrel with fear and trembling."],"id":412,"m":"If he fears God, he must keep the peace. If he breaks the peace, he ought to start a quarrel with fear and trembling."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["And so will he do; for the man doth fear God, howsoever it seems not","in him by some large jests he will make. Well, I am sorry for your","niece. Shall we go seek Benedick and tell him of her love?"],"id":413,"m":"And so he will, because the man does fear God, even if it doesn't seem so from some of his crude jokes. Well, I'm sorry for your niece. Shall we go and find Benedick and tell him about her love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Never tell him, my lord: let her wear it out with good counsel."],"id":414,"m":"Never tell him, my lord. Let her get over it with good advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Nay, that's impossible: she may wear her heart out first."],"id":415,"m":"No, that's impossible. She may wear out her heart first."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Well, we will hear further of it by your daughter: let it cool the","while. I love Benedick well, and I could wish he would modestly","examine himself, to see how much he is unworthy so good a lady."],"id":416,"m":"Well, we'll hear more about it from your daughter. Let it cool for now. I'm very fond of Benedick, and I wish he'd take a humble look at himself and see how unworthy he is of such a good lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["My lord, will you walk? dinner is ready."],"id":417,"m":"My lord, shall we go? Dinner is ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["[Aside.] If he do not dote on her upon this, I will never trust my","expectation."],"id":418,"m":"If he doesn't adore her after this, I'll never trust my judgment again."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["[Aside.] Let there be the same net spread for her; and that must your","daughter and her gentle-woman carry. The sport will be, when they hold","one an opinion of another's dotage, and no such matter: that's the","scene that I would see, which will be merely a dumb-show. Let us send","her to call him in to dinner."],"id":419,"m":"Let the same net be spread for her, and your daughter and her maid must handle that. The fun will be when each one believes the other is madly in love, and there's nothing in it. That's the scene I'd like to see, and it will be pure mime, since neither will dare to speak. Let's send her to call him in to dinner."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt DON PEDRO, CLAUDIO, and LEONATO.","id":420},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["[Advancing from the arbour.] This can be no trick: the conference was","sadly borne. They have the truth of this from Hero. They seem to pity","the lady: it seems her affections have their full bent. Love me! why,","it must be requited.","I hear how I am censured: they say I will bear myself proudly, if I","perceive the love come from her; they say too that she will rather die","than give any sign of affection. I did never think to marry: I must","not seem proud: happy are they that hear their detractions, and can","put them to mending.","They say the lady is fair: 'tis a truth, I can bear them witness; and","virtuous: 'tis so, I cannot reprove it; and wise, but for loving me:","by my troth, it is no addition to her wit, nor no great argument of","her folly, for I will be horribly in love with her.","I may chance have some odd quirks and remnants of wit broken on me,","because I have railed so long against marriage; but doth not the","appetite alter? A man loves the meat in his youth that he cannot","endure in his age.","Shall quips and sentences and these paper bullets of the brain awe a","man from the career of his humour? No; the world must be peopled. When","I said I would die a bachelor, I did not think I should live till I","were married. Here comes Beatrice. By this day! she's a fair lady: I","do spy some marks of love in her."],"id":421,"m":"This can't be a trick. They talked seriously. They have the truth of it from Hero. They seem to pity the lady. It seems her love is stretched to the limit. Love me! Why, it must be returned. I hear how I'm criticised. They say I'll be proud if I realise the love comes from her. They also say she would rather die than show any sign of love. I never thought I'd marry. I mustn't seem proud. Happy are those who hear their faults and can correct them. They say the lady is beautiful; that's true, I can vouch for it. And good; that's so, I can't deny it. And clever, except for loving me. Honestly, that doesn't add to her cleverness, but it's no great proof of her foolishness either, because I'm going to be terribly in love with her. Some odd jokes and scraps of wit may be cracked at my expense, because I've attacked marriage for so long. But don't tastes change? A man loves food in his youth that he can't stand in old age. Shall clever remarks and sayings and these paper bullets of the brain scare a man off following his desires? No, the world must be populated. When I said I'd die a bachelor, I didn't think I'd live until I got married. Here comes Beatrice. By heaven! She's a beautiful lady. I do see some signs of love in her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BEATRICE.","id":422},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Against my will I am sent to bid you come in to dinner."],"id":423,"m":"Against my will I've been sent to tell you to come in to dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Fair Beatrice, I thank you for your pains."],"id":424,"m":"Lovely Beatrice, thank you for your trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I took no more pains for those thanks than you take pains to thank me:","if it had been painful, I would not have come."],"id":425,"m":"I took no more trouble earning those thanks than you take to thank me. If it had been any trouble, I wouldn't have come."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["You take pleasure then in the message?"],"id":426,"m":"So you enjoy bringing the message?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Yea, just so much as you may take upon a knife's point, and choke","a daw withal. You have no stomach, signior: fare you well."],"id":427,"m":"Yes, about as much as you could balance on a knife's point and choke a jackdaw with. You have no appetite, signior. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":428},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Ha! 'Against my will I am sent to bid you come in to dinner,' there's","a double meaning in that. 'I took no more pains for those thanks than","you took pains to thank me,' that's as much as to say, Any pains that","I take for you is as easy as thanks. If I do not take pity of her, I","am a villain; if I do not love her, I am a Jew. I will go get her","picture."],"id":429,"m":"Ha! 'Against my will I've been sent to tell you to come in to dinner.' There's a double meaning in that. 'I took no more trouble earning those thanks than you took to thank me.' That's as good as saying, 'Any trouble I take for you is as easy as saying thanks.' If I don't take pity on her, I'm a villain; if I don't love her, I'm a heartless unbeliever. I'll go and get her portrait."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":430},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Leonato's Garden.","id":431},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HERO, MARGARET, and URSULA.","id":432},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Good Margaret, run thee to the parlour;","There shalt thou find my cousin Beatrice","Proposing with the prince and Claudio:","Whisper her ear, and tell her, I and Ursala","Walk in the orchard, and our whole discourse","Is all of her; say that thou overheard'st us,","And bid her steal into the pleached bower,","Where honey-suckles, ripen'd by the sun,","Forbid the sun to enter; like favourites,","Made proud by princes, that advance their pride","Against that power that bred it. There will she hide her,","To listen our propose. This is thy office;","Bear thee well in it and leave us alone."],"id":433,"m":"Good Margaret, run to the drawing room. You'll find my cousin Beatrice there talking with the prince and Claudio. Whisper in her ear and tell her that Ursula and I are walking in the orchard and that all our talk is about her. Say you overheard us, and tell her to slip into the arbour of woven branches, where honeysuckle, ripened by the sun, won't let the sun come in, like royal favourites who are made proud by princes and then turn their pride against the very power that raised them. She'll hide there to listen to our conversation. This is your job; do it well and leave us alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["I'll make her come, I warrant you, presently."],"id":434,"m":"I'll make her come right away, I promise you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":435},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Now, Ursula, when Beatrice doth come,","As we do trace this alley up and down,","Our talk must only be of Benedick:","When I do name him, let it be thy part","To praise him more than ever man did merit.","My talk to thee must be how Benedick","Is sick in love with Beatrice: of this matter","Is little Cupid's crafty arrow made,","That only wounds by hearsay."],"id":436,"m":"Now, Ursula, when Beatrice comes, as we walk up and down this path, we must talk only about Benedick. Whenever I mention him, your job is to praise him more than any man ever deserved. What I'll tell you is how Benedick is lovesick for Beatrice. That's what little Cupid's crafty arrow is made of, the kind that wounds just by hearsay."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BEATRICE, behind.","id":437},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","cont":1,"lines":["Now begin;","For look where Beatrice, like a lapwing, runs","Close by the ground, to hear our conference."],"id":438,"m":"Now, begin; for look where Beatrice is running close to the ground like a lapwing, to hear what we say."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["The pleasant'st angling is to see the fish","Cut with her golden oars the silver stream,","And greedily devour the treacherous bait:","So angle we for Beatrice; who even now","Is couched in the woodbine coverture.","Fear you not my part of the dialogue."],"id":439,"m":"The most pleasant kind of fishing is watching the fish cut through the silver stream with its golden fins and greedily swallow the treacherous bait. That's how we'll fish for Beatrice, who is already hiding under the honeysuckle. Don't worry about my part of the conversation."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Then go we near her, that her ear lose nothing","Of the false sweet bait that we lay for it."],"id":440,"m":"Then let's go nearer to her, so her ear doesn't miss any of the false sweet bait we're putting out for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"They advance to the bower.","id":441},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","cont":1,"lines":["No, truly, Ursula, she is too disdainful;","I know her spirits are as coy and wild","As haggards of the rock."],"id":442,"m":"No, honestly, Ursula, she's too scornful. I know her spirit is as shy and wild as an untamed hawk from the cliffs."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["But are you sure","That Benedick loves Beatrice so entirely?"],"id":443,"m":"But are you sure that Benedick loves Beatrice so completely?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["So says the prince, and my new-trothed lord."],"id":444,"m":"So says the prince, and my newly engaged lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["And did they bid you tell her of it, madam?"],"id":445,"m":"And did they ask you to tell her about it, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["They did entreat me to acquaint her of it;","But I persuaded them, if they lov'd Benedick,","To wish him wrestle with affection,","And never to let Beatrice know of it."],"id":446,"m":"They begged me to let her know; but I persuaded them, if they loved Benedick, to want him to wrestle with his love and never let Beatrice know about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Why did you so? Doth not the gentleman","Deserve as full as fortunate a bed","As ever Beatrice shall couch upon?"],"id":447,"m":"Why did you do that? Doesn't the gentleman deserve as rich and happy a marriage bed as Beatrice could ever lie in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["O god of love! I know he doth deserve","As much as may be yielded to a man;","But nature never fram'd a woman's heart","Of prouder stuff than that of Beatrice;","Disdain and scorn ride sparkling in her eyes,","Misprising what they look on, and her wit","Values itself so highly, that to her","All matter else seems weak. She cannot love,","Nor take no shape nor project of affection,","She is so self-endear'd."],"id":448,"m":"Oh god of love! I know he deserves as much as any man could be given. But nature never made a woman's heart out of prouder stuff than Beatrice's. Disdain and scorn sparkle in her eyes, despising everything they look at, and she values her own wit so highly that everything else seems feeble to her. She cannot love, or even imagine what love looks like; she's so in love with herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Sure I think so; And therefore certainly it were not good","She knew his love, lest she make sport at it."],"id":449,"m":"I'm sure you're right. So it certainly wouldn't be good for her to know about his love, in case she makes fun of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Why, you speak truth. I never yet saw man,","How wise, how noble, young, how rarely featur'd,","But she would spell him backward: if fair-fac'd,","She would swear the gentleman should be her sister;","If black, why, Nature, drawing of an antick,","Made a foul blot; if tall, a lance ill-headed;","If low, an agate very vilely cut;","If speaking, why, a vane blown with all winds;","If silent, why, a block moved with none.","So turns she every man the wrong side out,","And never gives to truth and virtue that","Which simpleness and merit purchaseth."],"id":450,"m":"Why, you're right. I've never seen a man, however clever, noble, young or handsome, whom she wouldn't turn inside out. If he was fair-skinned, she'd swear the gentleman should be her sister. If he was dark, why, Nature was drawing a clown and made an ugly blot. If he was tall, he was a badly topped lance; if short, a tiny carved stone, very badly cut. If he talked, a weathervane blown by every wind; if he was quiet, a block of wood that nothing moves. That's how she turns every man inside out, and never gives truth and virtue the credit that honesty and merit deserve."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Sure, sure, such carping is not commendable."],"id":451,"m":"Certainly, certainly, such fault-finding isn't admirable."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["No; not to be so odd, and from all fashions,","As Beatrice is, cannot be commendable.","But who dare tell her so? If I should speak,","She would mock me into air: O! she would laugh me","Out of myself, press me to death with wit.","Therefore let Benedick, like cover'd fire,","Consume away in sighs, waste inwardly:","It were a better death than die with mocks,","Which is as bad as die with tickling."],"id":452,"m":"No, being so odd and against all normal ways, as Beatrice is, can't be admirable. But who would dare tell her so? If I spoke up, she'd mock me into thin air. Oh, she'd laugh me out of myself and crush me to death with her wit. So let Benedick, like a smothered fire, burn away in sighs and waste away inside. That would be a better death than dying of mockery, which is as bad as being tickled to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Yet tell her of it: hear what she will say."],"id":453,"m":"Still, tell her about it, and hear what she says."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["No; rather I will go to Benedick,","And counsel him to fight against his passion.","And, truly, I'll devise some honest slanders","To stain my cousin with. One doth not know","How much an ill word may empoison liking."],"id":454,"m":"No, I'd rather go to Benedick and advise him to fight his passion. And honestly, I'll invent some harmless slanders to blacken my cousin's name with. You never know how much a bad word can poison love."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["O! do not do your cousin such a wrong.","She cannot be so much without true judgment,—","Having so swift and excellent a wit","As she is priz'd to have,—as to refuse","So rare a gentleman as Signior Benedick."],"id":455,"m":"Oh, don't do your cousin such an injustice. She can't be so lacking in good judgment, having such a quick and excellent wit as she's famous for, as to refuse such a rare gentleman as Signior Benedick."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["He is the only man of Italy,","Always excepted my dear Claudio."],"id":456,"m":"He's the finest man in Italy, except of course my dear Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["I pray you, be not angry with me, madam,","Speaking my fancy: Signior Benedick,","For shape, for bearing, argument and valour,","Goes foremost in report through Italy."],"id":457,"m":"Please don't be angry with me, madam, for saying what I think: Signior Benedick, for his looks, his manner, his conversation and his courage, has the best reputation in all Italy."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Indeed, he hath an excellent good name."],"id":458,"m":"Yes, he has an excellent reputation."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["His excellence did earn it, ere he had it.","When are you married, madam?"],"id":459,"m":"His excellence earned it before he had it. When are you getting married, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Why, every day, to-morrow. Come, go in:","I'll show thee some attires, and have thy counsel","Which is the best to furnish me to-morrow."],"id":460,"m":"Why, any day now: tomorrow. Come, let's go in. I'll show you some outfits, and you can advise me which is best to wear tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["She's lim'd, I warrant you: we have caught her, madam."],"id":461,"m":"She's trapped, I promise you. We've caught her, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["If it prove so, then loving goes by haps:","Some Cupid kills with arrows, some with traps."],"id":462,"m":"If that's true, then love works by chance. Cupid kills some with arrows, some with traps."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt HERO and URSULA.","id":463},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["[Advancing.] What fire is in mine ears? Can this be true?","~Stand I condemn'd for pride and scorn so much?","Contempt, farewell! and maiden pride, adieu!","~No glory lives behind the back of such.","And, Benedick, love on; I will requite thee,","~Taming my wild heart to thy loving hand:","If thou dost love, my kindness shall incite thee","~To bind our loves up in a holy band;","For others say thou dost deserve, and I","Believe it better than reportingly."],"id":464,"m":"What burning is this in my ears? Can this be true? Am I so condemned for pride and scorn? Contempt, goodbye! And girlish pride, farewell! No one speaks well of such things behind your back. And, Benedick, keep loving; I'll return your love, taming my wild heart to your loving hand. If you love me, my kindness will encourage you to tie our love together with the holy bond of marriage. For other people say you deserve it, and I believe it better than just from what they say."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":465},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"A Room in Leonato's House.","id":466},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON PEDRO, CLAUDIO, BENEDICK, and LEONATO.","id":467},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I do but stay till your marriage be consummate, and then go I","toward Arragon."],"id":468,"m":"I'll only stay until your marriage is complete, and then I'll head for Aragon."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I'll bring you thither, my lord, if you'll vouchsafe me."],"id":469,"m":"I'll go with you there, my lord, if you'll allow me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Nay, that would be as great a soil in the new gloss of your marriage,","as to show a child his new coat and forbid him to wear it. I will only","be bold with Benedick for his company; for, from the crown of his head","to the sole of his foot, he is all mirth; he hath twice or thrice cut","Cupid's bowstring, and the little hangman dare not shoot at him. He","hath a heart as sound as a bell, and his tongue is the clapper; for","what his heart thinks his tongue speaks."],"id":470,"m":"No, that would spoil the fresh shine of your marriage as much as showing a child his new coat and forbidding him to wear it. I'll only ask Benedick to come with me. From the top of his head to the soles of his feet he's all fun. He has cut Cupid's bowstring two or three times, and the little executioner doesn't dare shoot at him. He has a heart as sound as a bell, and his tongue is the clapper; whatever his heart thinks, his tongue says."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Gallants, I am not as I have been."],"id":471,"m":"Gentlemen, I'm not what I used to be."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["So say I: methinks you are sadder."],"id":472,"m":"That's what I think: you seem more serious."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I hope he be in love."],"id":473,"m":"I hope he's in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Hang him, truant! there's no true drop of blood in him, to be truly","touched with love. If he be sad, he wants money."],"id":474,"m":"Hang him, the layabout! There isn't a drop of true blood in him to be truly touched by love. If he's gloomy, he's short of money."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I have the tooth-ache."],"id":475,"m":"I have toothache."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Draw it."],"id":476,"m":"Pull it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Hang it."],"id":477,"m":"Hang it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["You must hang it first, and draw it afterwards."],"id":478,"m":"You have to hang it first and then draw it, like a traitor."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["What! sigh for the tooth-ache?"],"id":479,"m":"What! Sighing over toothache?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Where is but a humour or a worm?"],"id":480,"m":"Which is only caused by a bit of fluid or a worm?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Well, every one can master a grief but he that has it."],"id":481,"m":"Well, everyone can cope with a pain except the man who has it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Yet say I, he is in love."],"id":482,"m":"All the same, I say he's in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["There is no appearance of fancy in him, unless it be a fancy that he","hath to strange disguises; as to be a Dutchman to-day, a Frenchman","to-morrow; or in the shape of two countries at once, as a German from","the waist downward, all slops, and a Spaniard from the hip upward, no","doublet. Unless he have a fancy to this foolery, as it appears he","hath, he is no fool for fancy, as you would have it appear he is."],"id":483,"m":"There's no sign of fancy in him, unless it's a fancy for strange costumes: dressing as a Dutchman today, a Frenchman tomorrow, or as two countries at once, a German from the waist down, all baggy trousers, and a Spaniard from the hips up, with no jacket. Unless he has a fancy for this foolishness, as it seems he has, he's no fool for love, as you want to make out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If he be not in love with some woman, there is no believing old signs:","a' brushes his hat a mornings; what should that bode?"],"id":484,"m":"If he's not in love with some woman, there's no believing the old signs. He brushes his hat every morning; what could that mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Hath any man seen him at the barber's?"],"id":485,"m":"Has anyone seen him at the barber's?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["No, but the barber's man hath been seen with him; and the old","ornament of his cheek hath already stuffed tennis-balls."],"id":486,"m":"No, but the barber's assistant has been seen with him, and the old decoration on his cheeks has already been used to stuff tennis balls."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Indeed he looks younger than he did, by the loss of a beard."],"id":487,"m":"It's true, he looks younger without his beard."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Nay, a' rubs himself with civet: can you smell him out by that?"],"id":488,"m":"And he rubs himself with perfume. Can you sniff him out from that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["That's as much as to say the sweet youth's in love."],"id":489,"m":"That's as good as saying the sweet youth is in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["The greatest note of it is his melancholy."],"id":490,"m":"The biggest sign of it is his gloom."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And when was he wont to wash his face?"],"id":491,"m":"And since when did he wash his face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Yea, or to paint himself? for the which, I hear what they say of him."],"id":492,"m":"Yes, or wear make-up? I hear what people say about that."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Nay, but his jesting spirit; which is now crept into a lute-string,","and new-governed by stops."],"id":493,"m":"No, but look at his joking spirit, which has now crept into a lute-string and is controlled by its frets."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Indeed, that tells a heavy tale for him. Conclude, conclude he is","in love."],"id":494,"m":"Indeed, that tells a sad story about him. Conclude, conclude: he's in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Nay, but I know who loves him."],"id":495,"m":"No, but I know who loves him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["That would I know too: I warrant, one that knows him not."],"id":496,"m":"I'd like to know that too. Someone who doesn't know him, I bet."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Yes, and his ill conditions; and in despite of all, dies for him."],"id":497,"m":"Yes she does, and his bad habits too; and in spite of everything, she's dying for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["She shall be buried with her face upwards."],"id":498,"m":"Then she'll be buried face up, in her lover's arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Yet is this no charm for the tooth-ache. Old signior, walk aside with","me: I have studied eight or nine wise words to speak to you, which","these hobby-horses must not hear."],"id":499,"m":"Still, none of this cures toothache. Old signior, walk aside with me. I've prepared eight or nine wise words to say to you, which these clowns mustn't hear."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt BENEDICK and LEONATO.","id":500},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["For my life, to break with him about Beatrice."],"id":501,"m":"I bet my life he's going to talk to him about Beatrice."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["'Tis even so. Hero and Margaret have by this played their parts with","Beatrice, and then the two bears will not bite one another when they","meet."],"id":502,"m":"That's exactly it. Hero and Margaret will have played their parts with Beatrice by now, and so the two bears won't bite each other when they meet."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON JOHN.","id":503},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["My lord and brother, God save you!"],"id":504,"m":"My lord and brother, God save you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Good den, brother."],"id":505,"m":"Good evening, brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["If your leisure served, I would speak with you."],"id":506,"m":"If you had time, I'd like to speak with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["In private?"],"id":507,"m":"In private?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["If it please you; yet Count Claudio may hear, for what I would","speak of concerns him."],"id":508,"m":"If you like. But Count Claudio can hear, because what I want to say concerns him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":509,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["[To CLAUDIO.] Means your lordship to be married to-morrow?"],"id":510,"m":"Does your lordship mean to be married tomorrow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["You know he does."],"id":511,"m":"You know he does."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I know not that, when he knows what I know."],"id":512,"m":"I don't know that, once he knows what I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If there be any impediment, I pray you discover it."],"id":513,"m":"If there's any obstacle, please reveal it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["You may think I love you not: let that appear hereafter, and aim","better at me by that I now will manifest. For my brother, I think","he holds you well, and in dearness of heart hath holp to effect your","ensuing marriage; surely suit ill-spent and labour ill bestowed!"],"id":514,"m":"You may think I don't like you. Let that be shown later, and judge me better by what I'm about to reveal. As for my brother, I think he's very fond of you, and out of affection he has helped arrange your coming marriage. Surely an effort wasted and work thrown away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Why, what's the matter?"],"id":515,"m":"Why, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I came hither to tell you; and circumstances shortened,—for she","has been too long a talking of,—the lady is disloyal."],"id":516,"m":"I came here to tell you. To cut a long story short, since she's been talked about for too long: the lady is unfaithful."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Who, Hero?"],"id":517,"m":"Who, Hero?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Even she: Leonato's Hero, your Hero, every man's Hero."],"id":518,"m":"That's her: Leonato's Hero, your Hero, every man's Hero."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Disloyal?"],"id":519,"m":"Unfaithful?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["The word's too good to paint out her wickedness; I could say, she were","worse: think you of a worse title, and I will fit her to it. Wonder","not till further warrant: go but with me to-night, you shall see her","chamber-window entered, even the night before her wedding-day: if you","love her then, to-morrow wed her; but it would better fit your honour","to change your mind."],"id":520,"m":"The word is too good to describe her wickedness. I could say she's worse. Think of a worse name, and I'll prove it fits her. Don't be amazed until you have more proof. Just come with me tonight, and you'll see someone climb in at her bedroom window, on the very night before her wedding. If you still love her after that, marry her tomorrow; but it would suit your honour better to change your mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["May this be so?"],"id":521,"m":"Could this be true?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I will not think it."],"id":522,"m":"I won't believe it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["If you dare not trust that you see, confess not that you know. If you","will follow me, I will show you enough; and when you have seen more","and heard more, proceed accordingly."],"id":523,"m":"If you don't dare trust what you see, don't admit to what you know. If you follow me, I'll show you enough; and when you've seen and heard more, act on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If I see anything to-night why I should not marry her to-morrow, in","the congregation, where I should wed, there will I shame her."],"id":524,"m":"If I see anything tonight that means I shouldn't marry her tomorrow, I'll shame her in front of the congregation where I was supposed to marry her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["And, as I wooed for thee to obtain her, I will join with thee to","disgrace her."],"id":525,"m":"And since I wooed her to win her for you, I'll join you in disgracing her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["I will disparage her no farther till you are my witnesses: bear it","coldly but till midnight, and let the issue show itself."],"id":526,"m":"I won't run her down any further until you are my witnesses. Stay calm until midnight, and let the outcome speak for itself."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["O day untowardly turned!"],"id":527,"m":"Oh, what a day gone wrong!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O mischief strangely thwarting!"],"id":528,"m":"Oh, what a strange disaster!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["O plague right well prevented! So will you say when you have seen","the sequel."],"id":529,"m":"Oh, what a plague well prevented! That's what you'll say when you've seen what follows."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":530},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"A Street.","id":531},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DOGBERRY and VERGES, with the Watch.","id":532},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Are you good men and true?"],"id":533,"m":"Are you good men and true?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Yea, or else it were pity but they should suffer salvation, body","and soul."],"id":534,"m":"Yes, or else it would be a pity if they didn't suffer salvation (he means damnation), body and soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Nay, that were a punishment too good for them, if they should have","any allegiance in them, being chosen for the prince's watch."],"id":535,"m":"No, that would be too good a punishment for them, if they had any allegiance (he means disloyalty) in them, having been chosen for the prince's watch."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Well, give them their charge, neighbour Dogberry."],"id":536,"m":"Well, give them their orders, neighbour Dogberry."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["First, who think you the most desartless man to be constable?"],"id":537,"m":"First, who do you think is the most desertless (he means deserving) man to be constable?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCH","lines":["Hugh Oatcake, sir, or George Seacoal; for they can write and read."],"id":538,"m":"Hugh Oatcake, sir, or George Seacoal, because they can read and write."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Come hither, neighbour Seacoal. God hath blessed you with a good name:","to be a well-favoured man is the gift of fortune; but to write and","read comes by nature."],"id":539,"m":"Come here, neighbour Seacoal. God has blessed you with a good name. Being good-looking is a gift of fortune, but reading and writing come by nature."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["Both which, Master Constable,—"],"id":540,"m":"Both of which, Master Constable,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["You have: I knew it would be your answer. Well, for your favour, sir,","why, give God thanks, and make no boast of it; and for your writing","and reading, let that appear when there is no need of such vanity.","You are thought here to be the most senseless and fit man for the","constable of the watch; therefore bear you the lanthorn. This is your","charge: you shall comprehend all vagrom men; you are to bid any man","stand, in the prince's name."],"id":541,"m":"You have: I knew that would be your answer. Well, as for your looks, sir, thank God for them and don't boast about them; and as for your reading and writing, show them when there's no need for such vanity. You're thought to be the most senseless (he means sensible) and suitable man here to be constable of the watch, so you'll carry the lantern. These are your orders: you will comprehend (he means apprehend) all vagrant men. You're to order any man to stand still, in the prince's name."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["How, if a' will not stand?"],"id":542,"m":"What if he won't stand still?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Why, then, take no note of him, but let him go; and presently call","the rest of the watch together, and thank God you are rid of a knave."],"id":543,"m":"Why, then, take no notice of him, but let him go; and at once call the rest of the watch together and thank God you're rid of a villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["If he will not stand when he is bidden, he is none of the prince's","subjects."],"id":544,"m":"If he won't stand when he's told to, he's not one of the prince's subjects."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["True, and they are to meddle with none but the prince's subjects.","You shall also make no noise in the streets: for, for the watch to","babble and to talk is most tolerable and not to be endured."],"id":545,"m":"True, and they're to deal only with the prince's subjects. Also, you must make no noise in the streets; for the watch to babble and chatter is most tolerable (he means intolerable) and not to be put up with."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["We will rather sleep than talk: we know what belongs to a watch."],"id":546,"m":"We'll sleep rather than talk. We know what's proper for a watch."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Why, you speak like an ancient and most quiet watchman, for I","cannot see how sleeping should offend; only have a care that your","bills be not stolen. Well, you are to call at all the alehouses,","and bid those that are drunk get them to bed."],"id":547,"m":"Why, you speak like an experienced and very quiet watchman, for I can't see how sleeping could do any harm. Just take care your weapons aren't stolen. Well, you're to call at all the pubs and tell those who are drunk to go to bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["How if they will not?"],"id":548,"m":"What if they won't?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Why then, let them alone till they are sober: if they make you not","then the better answer, you may say they are not the men you took","them for."],"id":549,"m":"Why then, leave them alone until they're sober. If they don't give you a better answer then, you can say they aren't the men you took them for."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["Well, sir."],"id":550,"m":"Very well, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["If you meet a thief, you may suspect him, by virtue of your office,","to be no true man; and, for such kind of men, the less you meddle","or make with them, why, the more is for your honesty."],"id":551,"m":"If you meet a thief, you may suspect him, because of your job, of being dishonest; and as for men like that, the less you have to do with them, the better for your honesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["If we know him to be a thief, shall we not lay hands on him?"],"id":552,"m":"If we know he's a thief, shouldn't we arrest him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Truly, by your office, you may; but I think they that touch pitch will","be defiled. The most peaceable way for you, if you do take a thief, is","to let him show himself what he is and steal out of your company."],"id":553,"m":"Well, because of your job you may; but I think that whoever touches tar gets dirty. The most peaceful way, if you do catch a thief, is to let him show what he is and steal away from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["You have been always called a merciful man, partner."],"id":554,"m":"You've always been called a merciful man, partner."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Truly, I would not hang a dog by my will, much more a man who hath any","honesty in him."],"id":555,"m":"Truly, I wouldn't willingly hang a dog, let alone a man with any honesty in him."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["If you hear a child cry in the night, you must call to the nurse","and bid her still it."],"id":556,"m":"If you hear a child crying in the night, you must call the nurse and tell her to quieten it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["How if the nurse be asleep and will not hear us?"],"id":557,"m":"What if the nurse is asleep and won't hear us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Why then, depart in peace, and let the child wake her with crying;","for the ewe that will not hear her lamb when it baes, will never","answer a calf when he bleats."],"id":558,"m":"Why then, leave in peace and let the child wake her with its crying; for the ewe that won't hear her own lamb when it baas will never answer a calf when it bleats."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["'Tis very true."],"id":559,"m":"That's very true."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["This is the end of the charge. You constable, are to present the","prince's own person: if you meet the prince in the night, you may","stay him."],"id":560,"m":"That's the end of your orders. You, constable, represent the prince's own person. If you meet the prince in the night, you may stop him."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Nay, by'r lady, that I think, a' cannot."],"id":561,"m":"No, by Our Lady, I don't think he can."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Five shillings to one on't, with any man that knows the statutes, he","may stay him: marry, not without the prince be willing; for, indeed,","the watch ought to offend no man, and it is an offence to stay a man","against his will."],"id":562,"m":"Five shillings to one on it, with any man who knows the laws: he may stop him. But not unless the prince is willing; because the watch really ought to offend nobody, and it's an offence to stop a man against his will."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["By'r lady, I think it be so."],"id":563,"m":"By Our Lady, I think that's right."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Ha, ah, ha! Well, masters, good night: an there be any matter of weight","chances, call up me: keep your fellows' counsels and your own, and good","night. Come, neighbour."],"id":564,"m":"Ha, ha, ha! Well, men, good night. If anything important happens, wake me up. Keep your fellows' secrets and your own, and good night. Come, neighbour."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["Well, masters, we hear our charge: let us go sit here upon the","church-bench till two, and then all to bed."],"id":565,"m":"Well, men, we've heard our orders. Let's go and sit here on the church bench until two, and then all go to bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["One word more, honest neighbours. I pray you, watch about Signior","Leonato's door; for the wedding being there to-morrow, there is a great","coil to-night.","Adieu; be vigitant, I beseech you."],"id":566,"m":"One more word, honest neighbours. Please keep watch around Signior Leonato's door, because the wedding is there tomorrow and there's a great fuss going on tonight. Goodbye; be vigitant (he means vigilant), I beg you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt DOGBERRY and VERGES.","id":567},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BORACHIO and CONRADE.","id":568},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["What, Conrade!"],"id":569,"m":"Hey, Conrade!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WATCH","lines":["[Aside.] Peace! stir not."],"id":570,"m":"Quiet! Don't move."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Conrade, I say!"],"id":571,"m":"Conrade, I say!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Here, man. I am at thy elbow."],"id":572,"m":"Here, man. I'm right at your elbow."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Mass, and my elbow itched; I thought there would a scab follow."],"id":573,"m":"By the Mass, my elbow was itching; I thought a scab would follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["I will owe thee an answer for that; and now forward with thy tale."],"id":574,"m":"I'll answer that insult later. Now get on with your story."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Stand thee close then under this penthouse, for it drizzles rain,","and I will, like a true drunkard, utter all to thee."],"id":575,"m":"Stand close to me under this porch roof, then, because it's drizzling; and like a true drunkard I'll tell you everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"WATCH","lines":["[Aside.] Some treason, masters; yet stand close."],"id":576,"m":"Some treason, men; but keep hidden."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Therefore know, I have earned of Don John a thousand ducats."],"id":577,"m":"Well, then, I've earned a thousand ducats from Don John."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Is it possible that any villany should be so dear?"],"id":578,"m":"Is it possible that any villainy could be so expensive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Thou shouldst rather ask if it were possible any villany should be","so rich; for when rich villains have need of poor ones, poor ones may","make what price they will."],"id":579,"m":"You should rather ask if it's possible for any villain to be so rich; for when rich villains need poor ones, poor ones can name their price."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["I wonder at it."],"id":580,"m":"That amazes me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["That shows thou art unconfirmed. Thou knowest that the fashion of","a doublet, or a hat, or a cloak, is nothing to a man."],"id":581,"m":"That shows you're inexperienced. You know that the style of a jacket or a hat or a cloak means nothing about a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Yes, it is apparel."],"id":582,"m":"Yes, it's just clothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["I mean, the fashion."],"id":583,"m":"I mean the fashion."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Yes, the fashion is the fashion."],"id":584,"m":"Yes, the fashion is the fashion."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Tush! I may as well say the fool's the fool. But seest thou not what","a deformed thief this fashion is?"],"id":585,"m":"Rubbish! I might as well say a fool is a fool. But don't you see what a deformed thief this fashion is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WATCH","lines":["[Aside.] I know that Deformed; a' has been a vile thief this seven","years; a' goes up and down like a gentleman: I remember his name."],"id":586,"m":"I know that Deformed. He's been a vile thief these seven years. He goes around dressed like a gentleman. I remember his name."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Didst thou not hear somebody?"],"id":587,"m":"Didn't you hear somebody?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["No: 'twas the vane on the house."],"id":588,"m":"No, it was the weathervane on the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Seest thou not, I say, what a deformed thief this fashion is? how","giddily he turns about all the hot bloods between fourteen and","five-and-thirty? sometime fashioning them like Pharaoh's soldiers in","the reechy painting; sometime like god Bel's priests in the old","church-window; sometime like the shaven Hercules in the smirched","worm-eaten tapestry, where his codpiece seems as massy as his club?"],"id":589,"m":"Don't you see, I say, what a deformed thief this fashion is? How dizzily he spins round all the hot-blooded young men between fourteen and thirty-five? Sometimes dressing them like Pharaoh's soldiers in a grimy painting, sometimes like the priests of the god Bel in an old church window, sometimes like the clean-shaven Hercules in a dirty, worm-eaten tapestry, where his codpiece looks as massive as his club?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["All this I see, and I see that the fashion wears out more apparel than","the man. But art not thou thyself giddy with the fashion too, that","thou hast shifted out of thy tale into telling me of the fashion?"],"id":590,"m":"I see all that, and I see that fashion wears out more clothes than the men do. But aren't you dizzy with fashion yourself, since you've switched from your story to telling me about fashion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Not so neither; but know, that I have to-night wooed Margaret, the","Lady Hero's gentlewoman, by the name of Hero: she leans me out at her","mistress' chamber-window, bids me a thousand times good night,—I tell","this tale vilely:—I should first tell thee how the prince, Claudio,","and my master, planted and placed and possessed by my master Don John,","saw afar off in the orchard this amiable encounter."],"id":591,"m":"Not at all. But listen: tonight I've wooed Margaret, the Lady Hero's maid, calling her Hero. She leans out of her mistress's bedroom window to me and says good night a thousand times. I'm telling this badly. I should first tell you how the prince, Claudio and my master, set up and placed and convinced by my master Don John, watched this loving meeting from a distance in the orchard."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["And thought they Margaret was Hero?"],"id":592,"m":"And they thought Margaret was Hero?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Two of them did, the prince and Claudio; but the devil my master, knew","she was Margaret; and partly by his oaths, which first possessed them,","partly by the dark night, which did deceive them, but chiefly by my","villany, which did confirm any slander that Don John had made, away","went Claudio enraged; swore he would meet her, as he was appointed,","next morning at the temple, and there, before the whole congregation,","shame her with what he saw o'er night, and send her home again without","a husband."],"id":593,"m":"Two of them did, the prince and Claudio; but the devil my master knew she was Margaret. And partly because of his oaths, which convinced them in the first place, partly because of the dark night, which fooled them, but mainly because of my villainy, which confirmed every slander Don John had told, Claudio went off furious. He swore he'd meet her next morning at the church as arranged and there, in front of the whole congregation, shame her with what he'd seen that night and send her home again without a husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCH","lines":["We charge you in the prince's name, stand!"],"id":594,"m":"We order you in the prince's name: stand!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["Call up the right Master Constable. We have here recovered the most","dangerous piece of lechery that ever was known in the commonwealth."],"id":595,"m":"Call the real Master Constable. We've recovered (he means uncovered) the most dangerous piece of lechery (he means treachery) ever known in the state."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCH","lines":["And one Deformed is one of them: I know him, a' wears a lock."],"id":596,"m":"And one Deformed is one of them. I know him: he wears a lovelock."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Masters, masters!"],"id":597,"m":"Gentlemen, gentlemen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["You'll be made bring Deformed forth, I warrant you."],"id":598,"m":"You'll be made to produce Deformed, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Masters,—"],"id":599,"m":"Gentlemen,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCH","lines":["Never speak: we charge you let us obey you to go with us."],"id":600,"m":"Don't speak. We order you to let us obey you (he means you to obey us) and come with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["We are like to prove a goodly commodity, being taken up of these","men's bills."],"id":601,"m":"We're likely to be a fine bargain, bought on credit by these men's weapons."},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["A commodity in question, I warrant you. Come, we'll obey you."],"id":602,"m":"A bargain that'll be questioned, I'm sure. Come, we'll obey you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":603},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"A Room in Leonato's House.","id":604},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HERO, MARGARET, and URSULA.","id":605},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Good Ursula, wake my cousin Beatrice, and desire her to rise."],"id":606,"m":"Good Ursula, wake my cousin Beatrice and ask her to get up."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["I will, lady."],"id":607,"m":"I will, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["And bid her come hither."],"id":608,"m":"And tell her to come here."},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Well."],"id":609,"m":"All right."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":610},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Troth, I think your other rabato were better."],"id":611,"m":"Honestly, I think your other collar would be better."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["No, pray thee, good Meg, I'll wear this."],"id":612,"m":"No, please, good Meg, I'll wear this one."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["By my troth's not so good; and I warrant your cousin will say so."],"id":613,"m":"Honestly, it's not so good, and I bet your cousin will say so."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["My cousin 's a fool, and thou art another: I'll wear none but this."],"id":614,"m":"My cousin's a fool, and so are you. I'll wear only this one."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["I like the new tire within excellently, if the hair were a thought","browner; and your gown 's a most rare fashion, i' faith. I saw the","Duchess of Milan's gown that they praise so."],"id":615,"m":"I like the new headdress in the next room very much, if the hair were a shade browner; and your gown is a really rare fashion, honestly. I've seen the Duchess of Milan's gown that everyone praises so much."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["O! that exceeds, they say."],"id":616,"m":"Oh, they say that's beyond compare."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["By my troth 's but a night-gown in respect of yours: cloth o' gold,","and cuts, and laced with silver, set with pearls, down sleeves, side","sleeves, and skirts round, underborne with a blush tinsel; but for a","fine, quaint, graceful, and excellent fashion, yours is worth ten on't."],"id":617,"m":"Honestly, it's only a dressing gown compared with yours: cloth of gold, slashed, trimmed with silver lace, set with pearls, with long sleeves and hanging sleeves, and skirts trimmed round, lined with shimmering blue. But for a fine, elegant, graceful and excellent style, yours is worth ten of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["God give me joy to wear it! for my heart is exceeding heavy."],"id":618,"m":"God give me joy in wearing it! For my heart is very heavy."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["'Twill be heavier soon by the weight of a man."],"id":619,"m":"It'll soon be heavier with the weight of a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Fie upon thee! art not ashamed?"],"id":620,"m":"Shame on you! Aren't you ashamed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Of what, lady? of speaking honourably? is not marriage honourable in a","beggar? Is not your lord honourable without marriage? I think you","would have me say, 'saving your reverence, a husband:' an bad thinking","do not wrest true speaking, I'll offend nobody.","Is there any harm in 'the heavier for a husband'? None, I think, an it","be the right husband and the right wife; otherwise 'tis light, and not","heavy: ask my Lady Beatrice else; here she comes."],"id":621,"m":"Of what, lady? Of speaking honourably? Isn't marriage honourable, even for a beggar? Isn't your lord honourable, marriage or not? I think you'd want me to say, 'begging your pardon, a husband'. If dirty minds don't twist honest words, I'll offend nobody. Is there any harm in 'the heavier for a husband'? None, I think, if it's the right husband and the right wife; otherwise it's light, not heavy. Ask my Lady Beatrice if you don't believe me; here she comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BEATRICE.","id":622},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Good morrow, coz."],"id":623,"m":"Good morning, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Good morrow, sweet Hero."],"id":624,"m":"Good morning, sweet Hero."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Why, how now? do you speak in the sick tune?"],"id":625,"m":"Why, what's this? Are you speaking in a sickly tone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I am out of all other tune, methinks."],"id":626,"m":"I seem to be out of tune for anything else."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Clap's into 'Light o' love'; that goes without a burden: do you sing","it, and I'll dance it."],"id":627,"m":"Let's strike up 'Light o' Love'; it has no bass part. You sing it and I'll dance it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Ye, light o' love with your heels! then, if your husband have stables","enough, you'll see he shall lack no barnes."],"id":628,"m":"Yes, 'Light o' Love' with your heels! Then if your husband has stables enough, you'll see he won't be short of babies."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["O illegitimate construction! I scorn that with my heels."],"id":629,"m":"Oh, what a disgraceful interpretation! I kick that away with my heels."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["'Tis almost five o'clock, cousin; 'tis time you were ready. By my","troth, I am exceeding ill. Heigh-ho!"],"id":630,"m":"It's almost five o'clock, cousin; it's time you were ready. Honestly, I feel really ill. Oh dear!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["For a hawk, a horse, or a husband?"],"id":631,"m":"Are you sighing for a hawk, a horse or a husband?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["For the letter that begins them all, H."],"id":632,"m":"For the letter they all begin with: H (an 'ache')."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Well, an you be not turned Turk, there's no more sailing by the star."],"id":633,"m":"Well, if you haven't converted to a new faith, there's no steering by the stars any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["What means the fool, trow?"],"id":634,"m":"What does the fool mean, I wonder?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Nothing I; but God send every one their heart's desire!"],"id":635,"m":"Nothing, me; but God give everyone their heart's desire!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["These gloves the Count sent me; they are an excellent perfume."],"id":636,"m":"The count sent me these gloves. They have an excellent perfume."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I am stuffed, cousin, I cannot smell."],"id":637,"m":"I'm all stuffed up, cousin. I can't smell."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["A maid, and stuffed! there's goodly catching of cold."],"id":638,"m":"A virgin, and stuffed! That's a fine way to catch a cold."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["O, God help me! God help me! how long have you professed","apprehension?"],"id":639,"m":"Oh, God help me, God help me! How long have you been claiming to be witty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Ever since you left it. Doth not my wit become me rarely!"],"id":640,"m":"Ever since you gave it up. Doesn't my wit suit me beautifully?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["It is not seen enough, you should wear it in your cap. By my troth,","I am sick."],"id":641,"m":"It isn't seen enough; you should wear it in your cap, like a fool. Honestly, I'm ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Get you some of this distilled Carduus Benedictus, and lay it to","your heart: it is the only thing for a qualm."],"id":642,"m":"Get some of this distilled holy thistle, Carduus Benedictus, and put it on your heart. It's the only cure for queasiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["There thou prick'st her with a thistle."],"id":643,"m":"There, you've pricked her with a thistle."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Benedictus! why benedictus? you have some moral in this Benedictus."],"id":644,"m":"Benedictus! Why Benedictus? You have some hidden meaning in this Benedictus."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Moral! no, by my troth, I have no moral meaning; I meant, plain","holy-thistle. You may think, perchance, that I think you are in love:","nay, by'r lady, I am not such a fool to think what I list; nor I list","not to think what I can; nor, indeed, I cannot think, if I would think","my heart out of thinking, that you are in love, or that you will be in","love, or that you can be in love.","Yet Benedick was such another, and now is he become a man: he swore he","would never marry; and yet now, in despite of his heart, he eats his","meat without grudging: and how you may be converted, I know not; but","methinks you look with your eyes as other women do."],"id":645,"m":"Hidden meaning! No, honestly, I have no hidden meaning. I meant plain holy thistle. You may perhaps think that I think you're in love. No, by Our Lady, I'm not such a fool as to think whatever I like; and I don't like to think whatever I can; and in fact I couldn't think, even if I thought my heart out, that you're in love, or that you will be in love, or that you can be in love. Still, Benedick was the same, and now he has become a man like any other. He swore he'd never marry, and yet now, in spite of himself, he eats his food without complaining. How you might be converted, I don't know; but it seems to me you look with your eyes the way other women do."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["What pace is this that thy tongue keeps?"],"id":646,"m":"What pace is this your tongue is running at?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Not a false gallop."],"id":647,"m":"Not a false gallop: it's a true one."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter URSULA.","id":648},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Madam, withdraw: the prince, the count, Signior Benedick, Don John,","and all the gallants of the town, are come to fetch you to church."],"id":649,"m":"Madam, come away. The prince, the count, Signior Benedick, Don John and all the young gentlemen of the town have come to take you to church."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Help to dress me, good coz, good Meg, good Ursula."],"id":650,"m":"Help me get dressed, good cousin, good Meg, good Ursula."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":651},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"V","loc":"Another Room in Leonato's House.","id":652},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LEONATO and DOGBERRY and VERGES.","id":653},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["What would you with me, honest neighbour?"],"id":654,"m":"What do you want with me, honest neighbour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Marry, sir, I would have some confidence with you, that decerns","you nearly."],"id":655,"m":"Well, sir, I'd like to have a confidence (he means conference) with you that decerns (concerns) you closely."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Brief, I pray you; for you see it is a busy time with me."],"id":656,"m":"Be brief, please, because as you see, I'm very busy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Marry, this it is, sir."],"id":657,"m":"Well, it's this, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Yes, in truth it is, sir."],"id":658,"m":"Yes, truly it is, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["What is it, my good friends?"],"id":659,"m":"What is it, my good friends?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Goodman Verges, sir, speaks a little off the matter: an old man,","sir, and his wits are not so blunt as, God help, I would desire","they were; but, in faith, honest as the skin between his brows."],"id":660,"m":"Goodman Verges, sir, wanders off the point a bit. He's an old man, sir, and his wits are not as blunt (he means sharp) as I'd like them to be, God help him; but honestly, he's as honest as the skin between his eyebrows."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Yes, I thank God, I am as honest as any man living, that is an","old man and no honester than I."],"id":661,"m":"Yes, thank God, I'm as honest as any man alive who is an old man and no more honest than me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Comparisons are odorous: palabras, neighbour Verges."],"id":662,"m":"Comparisons are odorous (he means odious). Palabras: few words, neighbour Verges."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Neighbours, you are tedious."],"id":663,"m":"Neighbours, you are tedious."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["It pleases your worship to say so, but we are the poor duke's","officers; but truly, for mine own part, if I were as tedious as","a king, I could find in my heart to bestow it all of your worship."],"id":664,"m":"Your worship is kind to say so, but we are only the poor duke's officers. But honestly, for my part, if I were as tedious as a king, I'd gladly give it all to your worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["All thy tediousness on me! ha?"],"id":665,"m":"All your tediousness to me, eh?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Yea, an 't were a thousand pound more than 'tis; for I hear as good","exclamation on your worship, as of any man in the city, and though","I be but a poor man, I am glad to hear it."],"id":666,"m":"Yes, even if it were a thousand pounds more than it is; for I hear as good exclamation (he means acclamation) about your worship as about any man in the city, and though I'm only a poor man, I'm glad to hear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["And so am I."],"id":667,"m":"And so am I."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I would fain know what you have to say."],"id":668,"m":"I'd really like to know what you have to say."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Marry, sir, our watch to-night, excepting your worship's presence,","ha' ta'en a couple of as arrant knaves as any in Messina."],"id":669,"m":"Well, sir, our watch tonight, excepting (he means respecting) your worship's presence, has caught a pair of the biggest villains in Messina."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["A good old man, sir; he will be talking; as they say, 'when the age is","in, the wit is out.' God help us! it is a world to see! Well said, i'","faith, neighbour Verges: well, God's a good man; an two men ride of a","horse, one must ride behind. An honest soul, i' faith, sir; by my","troth he is, as ever broke bread; but God is to be worshipped: all men","are not alike; alas! good neighbour."],"id":670,"m":"A good old man, sir; he will keep talking. As they say, 'When age comes in, wit goes out.' God help us! What a world it is! Well said, really, neighbour Verges. Well, God's a good man. If two men ride one horse, one must ride behind. An honest soul, really, sir; upon my word he is, as honest as anyone who ever broke bread. But God be praised, all men aren't alike. Ah well, good neighbour!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Indeed, neighbour, he comes too short of you."],"id":671,"m":"Indeed, neighbour, he falls a long way short of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Gifts that God gives."],"id":672,"m":"Gifts that God gives."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I must leave you."],"id":673,"m":"I must leave you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["One word, sir: our watch, sir, hath indeed comprehended two","aspicious persons, and we would have them this morning examined","before your worship."],"id":674,"m":"One word, sir. Our watch, sir, has indeed comprehended (apprehended) two aspicious (suspicious) persons, and we'd like them examined in front of your worship this morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Take their examination yourself, and bring it me: I am now in great","haste, as may appear unto you."],"id":675,"m":"Question them yourself and bring me the report. I'm in a great hurry now, as you can see."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["It shall be suffigance."],"id":676,"m":"It will be suffigance (he means sufficient)."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Drink some wine ere you go: fare you well."],"id":677,"m":"Have some wine before you go. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":678},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord, they stay for you to give your daughter to her husband."],"id":679,"m":"My lord, they're waiting for you to give your daughter to her husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I'll wait upon them: I am ready."],"id":680,"m":"I'll come to them. I'm ready."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt LEONATO and Messenger.","id":681},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Go, good partner, go, get you to Francis Seacoal; bid him bring his","pen and inkhorn to the gaol: we are now to examination these men."],"id":682,"m":"Go, good partner, go to Francis Seacoal. Tell him to bring his pen and ink to the jail. We're going to examination these men now."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["And we must do it wisely."],"id":683,"m":"And we must do it wisely."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["We will spare for no wit, I warrant you; here's that shall drive some","of them to a non-come: only get the learned writer to set down our","excommunication, and meet me at the gaol."],"id":684,"m":"We won't spare any wit, I promise you. Here's something that will drive some of them to a non-come (he means non plus, a loss for words). Just get the learned clerk to write down our excommunication (he means communication), and meet me at the jail."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":685},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"The Inside of a Church.","id":686},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON PEDRO, DON JOHN, LEONATO, FRIAR FRANCIS, CLAUDIO, BENEDICK, HERO, BEATRICE, &c.","id":687},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Come, Friar Francis, be brief: only to the plain form of marriage,","and you shall recount their particular duties afterwards."],"id":688,"m":"Come, Friar Francis, be brief. Just the basic marriage service; you can explain their particular duties to them afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["You come hither, my lord, to marry this lady?"],"id":689,"m":"You come here, my lord, to marry this lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["No."],"id":690,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["To be married to her, friar; you come to marry her."],"id":691,"m":"To be married to her, friar; you are the one who marries her."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Lady, you come hither to be married to this count?"],"id":692,"m":"Lady, you come here to be married to this count?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["I do."],"id":693,"m":"I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["If either of you know any inward impediment, why you should not be","conjoined, I charge you, on your souls, to utter it."],"id":694,"m":"If either of you knows any secret obstacle why you should not be joined, I order you, on your souls, to say it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Know you any, Hero?"],"id":695,"m":"Do you know of any, Hero?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["None, my lord."],"id":696,"m":"None, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Know you any, count?"],"id":697,"m":"Do you know of any, count?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I dare make his answer; none."],"id":698,"m":"I'll dare to answer for him: none."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O! what men dare do! what men may do! what men daily do, not","knowing what they do!"],"id":699,"m":"Oh, what men dare to do! What men may do! What men do every day, not knowing what they're doing!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["How now! Interjections? Why then, some be of laughing, as ah! ha!","he!"],"id":700,"m":"Well! Exclamations? Then let's have some laughing ones, like 'ah! ha! he!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Stand thee by, friar. Father, by your leave: Will you with free and","unconstrained soul Give me this maid, your daughter?"],"id":701,"m":"Stand aside, friar. Father, excuse me: will you, freely and without any pressure, give me this girl, your daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["As freely, son, as God did give her me."],"id":702,"m":"As freely, son, as God gave her to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And what have I to give you back whose worth","May counterpoise this rich and precious gift?"],"id":703,"m":"And what can I give you back that is worth as much as this rich and precious gift?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Nothing, unless you render her again."],"id":704,"m":"Nothing, unless you give her back again."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Sweet prince, you learn me noble thankfulness.","There, Leonato, take her back again:","Give not this rotten orange to your friend;","She's but the sign and semblance of her honour.","Behold! how like a maid she blushes here.","O! what authority and show of truth","Can cunning sin cover itself withal.","Comes not that blood as modest evidence","To witness simple virtue? Would you not swear,","All you that see her, that she were a maid,","By these exterior shows? But she is none:","She knows the heat of a luxurious bed;","Her blush is guiltiness, not modesty."],"id":705,"m":"Sweet prince, you teach me noble gratitude. There, Leonato, take her back again. Don't give this rotten orange to your friend; she only looks honourable on the outside. Look how she blushes here like a virgin! Oh, how clever sin can disguise itself with such an air of authority and truth! Doesn't that blush come as modest evidence of simple innocence? Wouldn't all of you who see her swear she was a virgin, judging by these outward signs? But she is not. She knows the heat of a lustful bed. Her blush is guilt, not modesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["What do you mean, my lord?"],"id":706,"m":"What do you mean, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Not to be married,","Not to knit my soul to an approved wanton."],"id":707,"m":"I mean not to be married, not to bind my soul to a proven slut."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Dear my lord, if you, in your own proof,","Have vanquish'd the resistance of her youth,","And made defeat of her virginity,—"],"id":708,"m":"My dear lord, if you yourself have overcome her young resistance and taken her virginity,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I know what you would say: if I have known her,","You'll say she did embrace me as a husband,","And so extenuate the forehand sin: No, Leonato,","I never tempted her with word too large;","But, as a brother to his sister, show'd","Bashful sincerity and comely love."],"id":709,"m":"I know what you're going to say. If I've slept with her, you'll say she accepted me as her husband, and so excuse the sin of doing it early. No, Leonato. I never tempted her with any improper word; but like a brother to his sister, I showed shy sincerity and decent love."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["And seem'd I ever otherwise to you?"],"id":710,"m":"And did I ever seem any different to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Out on thee! Seeming! I will write against it:","You seem to me as Dian in her orb,","As chaste as is the bud ere it be blown;","But you are more intemperate in your blood","Than Venus, or those pamper'd animals","That rage in savage sensuality."],"id":711,"m":"Curse you! 'Seem'! I'll write against it. You seem to me like Diana, goddess of chastity, in her sphere, as chaste as a bud before it opens. But you are more uncontrolled in your lust than Venus, or those overfed animals that rage in wild sensuality."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Is my lord well, that he doth speak so wide?"],"id":712,"m":"Is my lord ill, that he talks so wildly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Sweet prince, why speak not you?"],"id":713,"m":"Sweet prince, why don't you speak?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["What should I speak?","I stand dishonour'd, that have gone about","To link my dear friend to a common stale."],"id":714,"m":"What could I say? I'm dishonoured, having tried to join my dear friend to a common prostitute."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Are these things spoken, or do I but dream?"],"id":715,"m":"Are these things really being said, or am I dreaming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Sir, they are spoken, and these things are true."],"id":716,"m":"Sir, they are being said, and these things are true."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["This looks not like a nuptial."],"id":717,"m":"This doesn't look like a wedding."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["True! O God!"],"id":718,"m":"True! Oh God!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Leonato, stand I here? Is this the prince?","Is this the prince's brother?","Is this face Hero's? Are our eyes our own?"],"id":719,"m":"Leonato, am I standing here? Is this the prince? Is this the prince's brother? Is this Hero's face? Are our eyes our own?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["All this is so; but what of this, my lord?"],"id":720,"m":"All this is so; but what about it, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Let me but move one question to your daughter,","And by that fatherly and kindly power","That you have in her, bid her answer truly."],"id":721,"m":"Let me ask your daughter just one question, and by the natural power you have over her as her father, order her to answer truthfully."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I charge thee do so, as thou art my child."],"id":722,"m":"I order you to do so, as you are my child."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["O, God defend me! how am I beset!","What kind of catechizing call you this?"],"id":723,"m":"Oh, God protect me! How I'm surrounded! What kind of interrogation do you call this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["To make you answer truly to your name."],"id":724,"m":"To make you answer truthfully to your name."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Is it not Hero? Who can blot that name","With any just reproach?"],"id":725,"m":"Isn't it Hero? Who can stain that name with any fair accusation?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Marry, that can Hero:","Hero itself can blot out Hero's virtue.","What man was he talk'd with you yesternight","Out at your window, betwixt twelve and one?","Now, if you are a maid, answer to this."],"id":726,"m":"Why, Hero can. Hero herself can stain Hero's virtue. Who was the man who talked with you last night at your window, between twelve and one? Now, if you are a virgin, answer this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["I talk'd with no man at that hour, my lord."],"id":727,"m":"I talked with no man at that hour, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Why, then are you no maiden.","Leonato, I am sorry you must hear: upon my honour,","Myself, my brother, and this grieved count,","Did see her, hear her, at that hour last night,","Talk with a ruffian at her chamber-window;","Who hath indeed, most like a liberal villain,","Confess'd the vile encounters they have had","A thousand times in secret."],"id":728,"m":"Why, then, you are no virgin. Leonato, I'm sorry you have to hear this. Upon my honour, I, my brother and this grieving count saw her, and heard her, at that hour last night, talking with a ruffian at her bedroom window; and he has, like a foul-mouthed villain, confessed the vile meetings they've had a thousand times in secret."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Fie, fie! they are not to be nam'd, my lord,","Not to be spoke of;","There is not chastity enough in language","Without offence to utter them. Thus, pretty lady,","I am sorry for thy much misgovernment."],"id":729,"m":"Shame, shame! They're not to be named, my lord, not to be spoken of. There aren't enough clean words in the language to describe them without offence. So, pretty lady, I'm sorry for your wicked behaviour."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O Hero! what a Hero hadst thou been,","If half thy outward graces had been plac'd","About thy thoughts and counsels of thy heart!","But fare thee well, most foul, most fair! farewell,","Thou pure impiety, and impious purity!","For thee I'll lock up all the gates of love,","And on my eyelids shall conjecture hang,","To turn all beauty into thoughts of harm,","And never shall it more be gracious."],"id":730,"m":"Oh Hero! What a Hero you would have been, if half your outward beauty had been given to your thoughts and the plans of your heart! But goodbye, most foul and most beautiful! Goodbye, you pure wickedness and wicked purity! Because of you I'll lock up all the gates of love, and suspicion will hang on my eyelids, turning all beauty into thoughts of evil, and it will never seem lovely again."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Hath no man's dagger here a point for me?"],"id":731,"m":"Hasn't any man here got a dagger point for me?"},{"t":"sd","text":"HERO swoons.","id":732},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Why, how now, cousin! wherefore sink you down?"],"id":733,"m":"Why, what's wrong, cousin? Why are you falling down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON JOHN","lines":["Come, let us go. These things, come thus to light,","Smother her spirits up."],"id":734,"m":"Come, let's go. These things, coming to light like this, have overwhelmed her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt DON PEDRO, DON JOHN and CLAUDIO.","id":735},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["How doth the lady?"],"id":736,"m":"How is the lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Dead, I think! help, uncle! Hero! why, Hero! Uncle! Signior","Benedick! Friar!"],"id":737,"m":"Dead, I think! Help, uncle! Hero! Hero! Uncle! Signior Benedick! Friar!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["O Fate! take not away thy heavy hand:","Death is the fairest cover for her shame","That may be wish'd for."],"id":738,"m":"Oh Fate, don't lift your heavy hand! Death is the best covering for her shame that anyone could wish for."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["How now, cousin Hero?"],"id":739,"m":"How are you, cousin Hero?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Have comfort, lady."],"id":740,"m":"Take comfort, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Dost thou look up?"],"id":741,"m":"Are you looking up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Yea; wherefore should she not?"],"id":742,"m":"Yes; why shouldn't she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Wherefore! Why, doth not every earthly thing","Cry shame upon her? Could she here deny","The story that is printed in her blood?","Do not live, Hero; do not ope thine eyes;","For, did I think thou wouldst not quickly die,","Thought I thy spirits were stronger than thy shames,","Myself would, on the rearward of reproaches,","Strike at thy life. Griev'd I, I had but one?","Chid I for that at frugal nature's frame?","O! one too much by thee. Why had I one?","Why ever wast thou lovely in mine eyes?","Why had I not with charitable hand","Took up a beggar's issue at my gates,","Who smirched thus, and mir'd with infamy,","I might have said, 'No part of it is mine;","This shame derives itself from unknown loins?'","But mine, and mine I lov'd, and mine I prais'd,","And mine that I was proud on, mine so much","That I myself was to myself not mine,","Valuing of her; why, she—O! she is fallen","Into a pit of ink, that the wide sea","Hath drops too few to wash her clean again,","And salt too little which may season give","To her foul-tainted flesh."],"id":743,"m":"Why! Doesn't everything on earth cry shame on her? Could she deny the story that's written in her blushes? Don't live, Hero; don't open your eyes. For if I thought you wouldn't die quickly, if I thought your spirit was stronger than your shame, I'd strike at your life myself, after all these reproaches. Did I grieve that I had only one child? Did I blame nature for being so mean? Oh, you were one too many! Why did I have even one? Why were you ever lovely in my eyes? Why didn't I take in some beggar's child at my gates out of charity? Then, if she were stained like this and smeared with disgrace, I could have said, 'None of it is mine; this shame comes from unknown parents.' But mine, and mine I loved, and mine I praised, and mine I was proud of, mine so much that I valued her more than myself: why, she, oh, she has fallen into a pit of ink, so deep that the wide sea hasn't enough drops to wash her clean again, nor enough salt to preserve her rotten flesh."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Sir, sir, be patient.","For my part, I am so attir'd in wonder,","I know not what to say."],"id":744,"m":"Sir, sir, be patient. For my part, I'm so wrapped in amazement I don't know what to say."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["O! on my soul, my cousin is belied!"],"id":745,"m":"Oh, on my soul, my cousin has been lied about!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Lady, were you her bedfellow last night?"],"id":746,"m":"Lady, did you share her bed last night?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["No, truly, not; although, until last night I have this","twelvemonth been her bedfellow."],"id":747,"m":"No, actually, I didn't; although until last night I've shared her bed for the past year."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Confirm'd, confirm'd! O! that is stronger made,","Which was before barr'd up with ribs of iron.","Would the two princes lie? and Claudio lie,","Who lov'd her so, that, speaking of her foulness,","Wash'd it with tears? Hence from her! let her die."],"id":748,"m":"Confirmed, confirmed! Oh, that makes the case even stronger, when it was already locked up with iron bars. Would the two princes lie? And would Claudio lie, who loved her so much that he wept as he spoke of her filth? Get away from her! Let her die."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Hear me a little;","For I have only been silent so long,","And given way unto this course of fortune,","By noting of the lady: I have mark'd","A thousand blushing apparitions","To start into her face; a thousand innocent shames","In angel whiteness bear away those blushes;","And in her eye there hath appear'd a fire,","To burn the errors that these princes hold","Against her maiden truth. Call me a fool;","Trust not my reading nor my observations,","Which with experimental seal doth warrant","The tenure of my book; trust not my age,","My reverence, calling, nor divinity,","If this sweet lady lie not guiltless here","Under some biting error."],"id":749,"m":"Listen to me for a moment. I've only kept quiet so long and let things take their course because I've been watching the lady. I've seen a thousand blushes leap into her face, and a thousand innocent signs of shame drive those blushes away in angelic whiteness. And a fire has appeared in her eye to burn up the mistakes these princes hold against her innocence. Call me a fool. Don't trust my learning or my observations, which experience has confirmed as true. Don't trust my age, my respect, my calling or my holy office, if this sweet lady isn't lying here innocent of some cruel error."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Friar, it cannot be.","Thou seest that all the grace that she hath left","Is that she will not add to her damnation","A sin of perjury: she not denies it.","Why seek'st thou then to cover with excuse","That which appears in proper nakedness?"],"id":750,"m":"Friar, it can't be. You see that the only virtue she has left is that she won't add the sin of lying under oath to her damnation. She doesn't deny it. So why are you trying to cover up with excuses what is plain to see?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Lady, what man is he you are accus'd of?"],"id":751,"m":"Lady, who is the man you are accused of being with?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["They know that do accuse me, I know none;","If I know more of any man alive","Than that which maiden modesty doth warrant,","Let all my sins lack mercy! O, my father!","Prove you that any man with me convers'd","At hours unmeet, or that I yesternight","Maintain'd the change of words with any creature,","Refuse me, hate me, torture me to death."],"id":752,"m":"Those who accuse me know. I know of none. If I know more of any living man than a virgin's modesty allows, let all my sins go without mercy! Oh, my father, if you can prove that any man talked with me at improper hours, or that I exchanged words with anyone last night, disown me, hate me, torture me to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["There is some strange misprision in the princes."],"id":753,"m":"The princes have made some strange mistake."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Two of them have the very bent of honour;","And if their wisdoms be misled in this,","The practice of it lives in John the bastard,","Whose spirits toil in frame of villanies."],"id":754,"m":"Two of them are completely honourable. If their judgment has been misled in this, the plot must come from John the bastard, whose mind is always busy inventing villainy."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I know not. If they speak but truth of her,","These hands shall tear her; if they wrong her honour,","The proudest of them shall well hear of it.","Time hath not yet so dried this blood of mine,","Nor age so eat up my invention,","Nor fortune made such havoc of my means,","Nor my bad life reft me so much of friends,","But they shall find, awak'd in such a kind,","Both strength of limb and policy of mind,","Ability in means and choice of friends,","To quit me of them throughly."],"id":755,"m":"I don't know. If they're telling the truth about her, these hands will tear her apart. If they're wronging her honour, the proudest of them will hear from me. Time hasn't yet dried my blood so much, nor age eaten up my wits, nor bad luck so ruined my wealth, nor a bad life robbed me of so many friends, that they won't find, when I'm roused in this way, strength in my arms and cunning in my mind, enough wealth and well-chosen friends to pay them back in full."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Pause awhile, And let my counsel sway you in this case.","Your daughter here the princes left for dead;","Let her awhile be secretly kept in,","And publish it that she is dead indeed:","Maintain a mourning ostentation;","And on your family's old monument","Hang mournful epitaphs and do all rites","That appertain unto a burial."],"id":756,"m":"Wait a moment, and let my advice guide you in this. The princes left your daughter here for dead. Let her be kept secretly at home for a while, and announce that she really is dead. Put on a show of mourning, hang sad epitaphs on your family's old tomb, and carry out all the rites that belong to a burial."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["What shall become of this? What will this do?"],"id":757,"m":"What will come of this? What will this achieve?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Marry, this well carried shall on her behalf","Change slander to remorse; that is some good.","But not for that dream I on this strange course,","But on this travail look for greater birth.","She dying, as it must be so maintain'd,","Upon the instant that she was accus'd,","Shall be lamented, pitied and excus'd","Of every hearer; for it so falls out","That what we have we prize not to the worth","Whiles we enjoy it, but being lack'd and lost,","Why, then we rack the value, then we find","The virtue that possession would not show us","Whiles it was ours. So will it fare with Claudio:","When he shall hear she died upon his words,","The idea of her life shall sweetly creep","Into his study of imagination,","And every lovely organ of her life","Shall come apparell'd in more precious habit,","More moving-delicate, and full of life","Into the eye and prospect of his soul,","Than when she liv'd indeed: then shall he mourn,—","If ever love had interest in his liver,—","And wish he had not so accused her,","No, though be thought his accusation true.","Let this be so, and doubt not but success","Will fashion the event in better shape","Than I can lay it down in likelihood.","But if all aim but this be levell'd false,","The supposition of the lady's death","Will quench the wonder of her infamy:","And if it sort not well, you may conceal her,—","As best befits her wounded reputation,—","In some reclusive and religious life,","Out of all eyes, tongues, minds, and injuries."],"id":758,"m":"Well, if this is handled well, it will turn slander into regret on her behalf; that's something good. But that isn't why I dream up this strange plan; I hope this labour will give birth to something greater. If it is announced that she died the moment she was accused, every listener will mourn, pity and excuse her. For it happens that we don't value what we have properly while we enjoy it, but once it's missing and lost, why, then we stretch its value to the limit; then we find the virtue that owning it wouldn't show us while it was ours. That's how it will be with Claudio. When he hears she died because of his words, the memory of her life will creep sweetly into his imagination, and every lovely part of her will come back dressed in finer clothes, more moving, more delicate and more full of life in his mind's eye than when she was really alive. Then he'll mourn, if love ever touched his heart, and wish he hadn't accused her, even if he still thought his accusation was true. Let this be done, and have no doubt that the outcome will shape things better than I can predict. And even if everything else misses its aim, the belief that the lady is dead will put an end to talk of her disgrace. And if things don't turn out well, you can hide her away, as best suits her damaged reputation, in some secluded religious life as a nun, far from all eyes, tongues, minds and harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Signior Leonato, let the friar advise you:","And though you know my inwardness and love","Is very much unto the prince and Claudio,","Yet, by mine honour, I will deal in this","As secretly and justly as your soul","Should with your body."],"id":759,"m":"Signior Leonato, let the friar advise you. And though you know my close friendship and love belong to the prince and Claudio, still, on my honour, I'll act in this as secretly and fairly as your soul would with your own body."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Being that I flow in grief, The smallest twine may lead me."],"id":760,"m":"Since I'm drowning in grief, the thinnest thread can lead me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["'Tis well consented: presently away;","For to strange sores strangely they strain the cure.","Come, lady, die to live: this wedding day","Perhaps is but prolong'd: have patience and endure."],"id":761,"m":"That's well agreed. Let's go at once, for strange wounds need strange cures. Come, lady, die so that you may live. This wedding day is perhaps only postponed. Be patient and endure."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt FRIAR, HERO, and LEONATO.","id":762},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Lady Beatrice, have you wept all this while?"],"id":763,"m":"Lady Beatrice, have you been crying all this time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Yea, and I will weep a while longer."],"id":764,"m":"Yes, and I'll cry a while longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I will not desire that."],"id":765,"m":"I don't want that."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["You have no reason; I do it freely."],"id":766,"m":"You've no reason to; I'm doing it of my own accord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Surely I do believe your fair cousin is wronged."],"id":767,"m":"I truly believe your lovely cousin has been wronged."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Ah! how much might the man deserve of me that would right her."],"id":768,"m":"Ah! How much the man who put her right would deserve from me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Is there any way to show such friendship?"],"id":769,"m":"Is there any way to show such friendship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["A very even way, but no such friend."],"id":770,"m":"A very simple way, but no such friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["May a man do it?"],"id":771,"m":"Could a man do it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["It is a man's office, but not yours."],"id":772,"m":"It's a man's job, but not yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I do love nothing in the world so well as you: is not that","strange?"],"id":773,"m":"I love nothing in the world as much as you. Isn't that strange?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["As strange as the thing I know not. It were as possible for me to say","I loved nothing so well as you; but believe me not, and yet I lie not;","I confess nothing, nor I deny nothing. I am sorry for my cousin."],"id":774,"m":"As strange as something I don't know about. It would be just as possible for me to say I loved nothing as much as you. But don't believe me; and yet I'm not lying. I confess nothing, and I deny nothing. I'm sorry for my cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["By my sword, Beatrice, thou lovest me."],"id":775,"m":"By my sword, Beatrice, you love me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Do not swear by it, and eat it."],"id":776,"m":"Don't swear by it and then eat your words."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I will swear by it that you love me; and I will make him eat it that","says I love not you."],"id":777,"m":"I'll swear by it that you love me, and I'll make anyone who says I don't love you eat it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Will you not eat your word?"],"id":778,"m":"Won't you eat your words?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["With no sauce that can be devised to it. I protest I love thee."],"id":779,"m":"Not with any sauce that could be invented for them. I declare I love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Why then, God forgive me!"],"id":780,"m":"Why then, God forgive me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["What offence, sweet Beatrice?"],"id":781,"m":"For what offence, sweet Beatrice?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["You have stayed me in a happy hour: I was about to protest I loved","you."],"id":782,"m":"You interrupted me at a lucky moment. I was about to declare that I loved you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["And do it with all thy heart."],"id":783,"m":"Then do it with all your heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I love you with so much of my heart that none is left to protest."],"id":784,"m":"I love you with so much of my heart that there's none left to declare it with."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Come, bid me do anything for thee."],"id":785,"m":"Come, tell me to do anything for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Kill Claudio."],"id":786,"m":"Kill Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Ha! not for the wide world."],"id":787,"m":"Ha! Not for the whole world."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["You kill me to deny it. Farewell."],"id":788,"m":"You kill me by refusing. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Tarry, sweet Beatrice."],"id":789,"m":"Stay, sweet Beatrice."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I am gone, though I am here: there is no love in you: nay, I pray","you, let me go."],"id":790,"m":"I'm gone, even though I'm still here. There's no love in you. No, please, let me go."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Beatrice,—"],"id":791,"m":"Beatrice,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["In faith, I will go."],"id":792,"m":"Really, I'm going."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["We'll be friends first."],"id":793,"m":"Let's be friends first."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["You dare easier be friends with me than fight with mine enemy."],"id":794,"m":"You find it easier to be friends with me than to fight my enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Is Claudio thine enemy?"],"id":795,"m":"Is Claudio your enemy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Is he not approved in the height a villain, that hath slandered,","scorned, dishonoured my kinswoman? O! that I were a man. What! bear","her in hand until they come to take hands, and then, with public","accusation, uncovered slander, unmitigated rancour,—O God, that I","were a man! I would eat his heart in the market-place."],"id":796,"m":"Hasn't he proved himself the worst kind of villain, who has slandered, scorned and dishonoured my cousin? Oh, if only I were a man! What! Lead her on until the moment they join hands, and then, with a public accusation, open slander, unrelenting hatred... Oh God, if only I were a man! I'd eat his heart in the market place."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Hear me, Beatrice,—"],"id":797,"m":"Listen to me, Beatrice,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Talk with a man out at a window! a proper saying!"],"id":798,"m":"Talking with a man at a window! A likely story!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Nay, but Beatrice,—"],"id":799,"m":"No, but Beatrice,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Sweet Hero! she is wronged, she is slandered, she is undone."],"id":800,"m":"Sweet Hero! She's wronged, she's slandered, she's ruined."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Beat—"],"id":801,"m":"Beat—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Princes and counties! Surely, a princely testimony, a goodly Count","Comfect; a sweet gallant, surely! O! that I were a man for his sake,","or that I had any friend would be a man for my sake!","But manhood is melted into cursies, valour into compliment, and men","are only turned into tongue, and trim ones too: he is now as valiant","as Hercules, that only tells a lie and swears it. I cannot be a man","with wishing, therefore I will die a woman with grieving."],"id":802,"m":"Princes and counts! What princely evidence! What a fine Count Sugar-Sweet, a sweet gentleman, surely! Oh, if only I were a man for his sake, or had a friend who'd be a man for my sake! But manhood has melted into bowing and curtsying, courage into compliments, and men have turned into nothing but tongues, and fancy ones too. A man is now as brave as Hercules if he only tells a lie and swears it's true. I can't become a man by wishing, so I'll die a woman, grieving."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Tarry, good Beatrice. By this hand, I love thee."],"id":803,"m":"Wait, good Beatrice. By this hand, I love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Use it for my love some other way than swearing by it."],"id":804,"m":"Use it for my love in some other way than swearing by it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Think you in your soul the Count Claudio hath wronged Hero?"],"id":805,"m":"Do you believe in your soul that Count Claudio has wronged Hero?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Yea, as sure is I have a thought or a soul."],"id":806,"m":"Yes, as surely as I have a thought or a soul."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Enough! I am engaged, I will challenge him. I will kiss your hand,","and so leave you. By this hand, Claudio shall render me a dear account.","As you hear of me, so think of me. Go, comfort your cousin: I must","say she is dead; and so, farewell."],"id":807,"m":"Enough! I'm committed. I'll challenge him. I'll kiss your hand and leave you. By this hand, Claudio will pay me dearly. Judge me by what you hear of me. Go and comfort your cousin. I must say she is dead. And so, goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":808},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"A Prison.","id":809},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DOGBERRY, VERGES, and SEXTON, in gowns; and the Watch, with CONRADE and BORACHIO.","id":810},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Is our whole dissembly appeared?"],"id":811,"m":"Has our whole dissembly (he means assembly) appeared?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["O! a stool and a cushion for the sexton."],"id":812,"m":"Oh, a stool and a cushion for the clerk."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEXTON","lines":["Which be the malefactors?"],"id":813,"m":"Which are the wrongdoers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Marry, that am I and my partner."],"id":814,"m":"Why, that's me and my partner."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Nay, that's certain: we have the exhibition to examine."],"id":815,"m":"Yes, that's certain: we have the exhibition (he means commission) to examine them."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEXTON","lines":["But which are the offenders that are to be examined? let them","come before Master constable."],"id":816,"m":"But which are the offenders who are to be examined? Bring them before the Master Constable."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Yea, marry, let them come before me. What is your name, friend?"],"id":817,"m":"Yes, indeed, bring them before me. What's your name, friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Borachio."],"id":818,"m":"Borachio."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Pray write down Borachio. Yours, sirrah?"],"id":819,"m":"Please write down 'Borachio'. Yours, fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["I am a gentleman, sir, and my name is Conrade."],"id":820,"m":"I'm a gentleman, sir, and my name is Conrade."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Write down Master gentleman Conrade. Masters, do you serve God?"],"id":821,"m":"Write down 'Master gentleman Conrade'. Gentlemen, do you serve God?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE AND BORACHIO","lines":["Yea, sir, we hope."],"id":822,"m":"Yes, sir, we hope so."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Write down that they hope they serve God: and write God first; for","God defend but God should go before such villains! Masters, it is","proved already that you are little better than false knaves, and it","will go near to be thought so shortly. How answer you for yourselves?"],"id":823,"m":"Write down that they hope they serve God; and write God first, for God forbid that God shouldn't come before such villains! Gentlemen, it's already proved that you're little better than lying villains, and it will soon be close to being thought so. How do you answer for yourselves?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Marry, sir, we say we are none."],"id":824,"m":"Well, sir, we say we're not."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["A marvellous witty fellow, I assure you; but I will go about with him.","Come you hither, sirrah; a word in your ear: sir, I say to you, it is","thought you are false knaves."],"id":825,"m":"A wonderfully clever fellow, I assure you; but I'll get round him. Come here, fellow, a word in your ear. Sir, I tell you, it's thought you are lying villains."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Sir, I say to you we are none."],"id":826,"m":"Sir, I tell you we're not."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Well, stand aside. Fore God, they are both in a tale. Have you writ","down, that they are none?"],"id":827,"m":"Well, stand aside. By God, they've both got the same story. Have you written down that they're not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SEXTON","lines":["Master constable, you go not the way to examine: you must call forth","the watch that are their accusers."],"id":828,"m":"Master Constable, this isn't the way to question them. You must call the watchmen who are their accusers."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Yea, marry, that's the eftest way. Let the watch come forth.","Masters, I charge you, in the prince's name, accuse these men."],"id":829,"m":"Yes, indeed, that's the eftest (he means deftest, quickest) way. Let the watch come forward. Men, I order you in the prince's name, accuse these men."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCH","lines":["This man said, sir, that Don John, the prince's brother, was a","villain."],"id":830,"m":"This man said, sir, that Don John, the prince's brother, was a villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Write down Prince John a villain. Why, this is flat perjury, to","call a prince's brother villain."],"id":831,"m":"Write down 'Prince John a villain'. Why, this is plain perjury, calling a prince's brother a villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Master Constable,—"],"id":832,"m":"Master Constable,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Pray thee, fellow, peace: I do not like thy look, I promise thee."],"id":833,"m":"Please be quiet, fellow. I don't like your looks, I can tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEXTON","lines":["What heard you him say else?"],"id":834,"m":"What else did you hear him say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["Marry, that he had received a thousand ducats of Don John for","accusing the Lady Hero wrongfully."],"id":835,"m":"Why, that he'd received a thousand ducats from Don John for falsely accusing the Lady Hero."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Flat burglary as ever was committed."],"id":836,"m":"As plain a burglary as was ever committed."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Yea, by the mass, that it is."],"id":837,"m":"Yes, by the Mass, that's right."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEXTON","lines":["What else, fellow?"],"id":838,"m":"What else, fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WATCH","lines":["And that Count Claudio did mean, upon his words, to disgrace Hero","before the whole assembly, and not marry her."],"id":839,"m":"And that Count Claudio intended, on his word, to disgrace Hero in front of the whole congregation and not marry her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["O villain! thou wilt be condemned into everlasting redemption for","this."],"id":840,"m":"Oh, villain! You'll be condemned to everlasting redemption (he means damnation) for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEXTON","lines":["What else?"],"id":841,"m":"What else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WATCH","lines":["This is all."],"id":842,"m":"That's all."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEXTON","lines":["And this is more, masters, than you can deny. Prince John is this","morning secretly stolen away: Hero was in this manner accused, in this","manner refused, and, upon the grief of this, suddenly died. Master","Constable, let these men be bound, and brought to Leonato's: I will go","before and show him their examination."],"id":843,"m":"And this is more, gentlemen, than you can deny. Prince John secretly ran away this morning. Hero was accused in this way, rejected in this way, and died suddenly of grief. Master Constable, have these men tied up and brought to Leonato's. I'll go ahead and show him their statements."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":844},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Come, let them be opinioned."],"id":845,"m":"Come on, have them opinioned (he means pinioned, tied up)."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Let them be in the hands—"],"id":846,"m":"Let them be in the hands—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Off, coxcomb!"],"id":847,"m":"Get off, you fool!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["God's my life! where's the sexton? let him write down the prince's","officer coxcomb. Come, bind them. Thou naughty varlet!"],"id":848,"m":"God save my life! Where's the clerk? Let him write down that the prince's officer is a fool. Come, tie them up. You wicked rascal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CONRADE","lines":["Away! you are an ass; you are an ass."],"id":849,"m":"Get away! You're an ass, you're an ass."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Dost thou not suspect my place? Dost thou not suspect my years? O that","he were here to write me down an ass! but, masters, remember that I am","an ass; though it be not written down, yet forget not that I am an","ass. No, thou villain, thou art full of piety, as shall be proved upon","thee by good witness.","I am a wise fellow; and, which is more, an officer; and, which is","more, a householder; and, which is more, as pretty a piece of flesh as","any in Messina; and one that knows the law, go to; and a rich fellow","enough, go to; and a fellow that hath had losses; and one that hath","two gowns, and everything handsome about him. Bring him away. O that I","had been writ down an ass!"],"id":850,"m":"Don't you suspect (he means respect) my position? Don't you suspect my age? Oh, if only he were here to write me down as an ass! But, gentlemen, remember that I am an ass. Though it isn't written down, don't forget that I am an ass. No, you villain, you're full of piety (he means impiety), as good witnesses will prove against you. I'm a wise fellow; and what's more, an officer; and what's more, a householder; and what's more, as fine a piece of flesh as any in Messina; and one who knows the law, so there; and quite a rich fellow, so there; and a fellow who has had losses; and one who has two gowns, and everything handsome about him. Bring him away. Oh, if only I'd been written down as an ass!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":851},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Before Leonato's House.","id":852},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LEONATO and ANTONIO.","id":853},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["If you go on thus, you will kill yourself","And 'tis not wisdom thus to second grief","Against yourself."],"id":854,"m":"If you carry on like this, you'll kill yourself; and it isn't wise to help grief attack you like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I pray thee, cease thy counsel,","Which falls into mine ears as profitless","As water in a sieve: give not me counsel;","Nor let no comforter delight mine ear","But such a one whose wrongs do suit with mine:","Bring me a father that so lov'd his child,","Whose joy of her is overwhelm'd like mine,","And bid him speak to me of patience;","Measure his woe the length and breadth of mine,","And let it answer every strain for strain,","As thus for thus and such a grief for such,","In every lineament, branch, shape, and form:","If such a one will smile, and stroke his beard;","Bid sorrow wag, cry 'hem' when he should groan,","Patch grief with proverbs; make misfortune drunk","With candle-wasters; bring him yet to me,","And I of him will gather patience.","But there is no such man; for, brother, men","Can counsel and speak comfort to that grief","Which they themselves not feel; but, tasting it,","Their counsel turns to passion, which before","Would give preceptial medicine to rage,","Fetter strong madness in a silken thread,","Charm ache with air and agony with words.","No, no; 'tis all men's office to speak patience","To those that wring under the load of sorrow,","But no man's virtue nor sufficiency","To be so moral when he shall endure","The like himself. Therefore give me no counsel:","My griefs cry louder than advertisement."],"id":855,"m":"Please stop your advice, which falls into my ears as uselessly as water into a sieve. Don't give me advice, and don't let any comforter please my ear except one whose wrongs match mine. Bring me a father who loved his child as much, whose joy in her has been crushed like mine, and tell him to speak to me of patience. Measure his sorrow against the length and breadth of mine, and let it match it pang for pang, this for this and grief for grief, in every feature, branch, shape and form. If a man like that can smile and stroke his beard, tell sorrow to be off, say 'ahem' when he ought to groan, patch up his grief with proverbs and drown his misfortune with late-night philosophers, then bring him to me, and I'll learn patience from him. But there is no such man. For, brother, men can advise and comfort a grief they don't feel themselves; but once they taste it, their advice turns into the same passion that they once wanted to cure with wise sayings, as if they could chain raging madness with a silk thread, charm away pain with breath and agony with words. No, no. It's everyone's job to preach patience to those who writhe under the weight of sorrow, but no one has the strength or the ability to be so moral when he has to endure the same himself. So give me no advice. My griefs cry louder than your warnings."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Therein do men from children nothing differ."],"id":856,"m":"In that, men are no different from children."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I pray thee peace! I will be flesh and blood;","For there was never yet philosopher","That could endure the toothache patiently,","However they have writ the style of gods","And made a push at chance and sufferance."],"id":857,"m":"Please, be quiet! I'll be flesh and blood; for there was never yet a philosopher who could bear toothache patiently, however much they've written like gods and scorned bad luck and suffering."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Yet bend not all the harm upon yourself;","Make those that do offend you suffer too."],"id":858,"m":"Still, don't turn all the harm on yourself. Make those who have wronged you suffer too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["There thou speak'st reason: nay, I will do so.","My soul doth tell me Hero is belied;","And that shall Claudio know; so shall the prince,","And all of them that thus dishonour her."],"id":859,"m":"Now you're talking sense. Yes, I'll do that. My soul tells me Hero has been lied about, and Claudio will know it; so will the prince, and all of them who have dishonoured her like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Here comes the prince and Claudio hastily."],"id":860,"m":"Here come the prince and Claudio in a hurry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON PEDRO and CLAUDIO.","id":861},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Good den, good den."],"id":862,"m":"Good evening, good evening."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Good day to both of you."],"id":863,"m":"Good day to you both."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Hear you, my lords,—"],"id":864,"m":"Listen, my lords,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["We have some haste, Leonato."],"id":865,"m":"We're in a hurry, Leonato."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Some haste, my lord! well, fare you well, my lord:","Are you so hasty now?—well, all is one."],"id":866,"m":"In a hurry, my lord! Well, goodbye, my lord. Are you in such a hurry now? Well, it doesn't matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Nay, do not quarrel with us, good old man."],"id":867,"m":"No, don't quarrel with us, good old man."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["If he could right himself with quarrelling,","Some of us would lie low."],"id":868,"m":"If he could put things right by quarrelling, some of us would be lying dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Who wrongs him?"],"id":869,"m":"Who's wronging him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Marry, thou dost wrong me; thou dissembler, thou.","Nay, never lay thy hand upon thy sword; I fear thee not."],"id":870,"m":"Why, you are wronging me, you liar, you! No, don't put your hand on your sword; I'm not afraid of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Marry, beshrew my hand,","If it should give your age such cause of fear.","In faith, my hand meant nothing to my sword."],"id":871,"m":"Curse my hand if it gave your old age any reason to be afraid. Honestly, my hand meant nothing by touching my sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Tush, tush, man! never fleer and jest at me:","I speak not like a dotard nor a fool,","As, under privilege of age, to brag","What I have done being young, or what would do,","Were I not old. Know, Claudio, to thy head,","Thou hast so wrong'd mine innocent child and me","That I am forc'd to lay my reverence by,","And, with grey hairs and bruise of many days,","Do challenge thee to trial of a man.","I say thou hast belied mine innocent child:","Thy slander hath gone through and through her heart,","And she lied buried with her ancestors;","O! in a tomb where never scandal slept,","Save this of hers, fram'd by thy villany!"],"id":872,"m":"Come, come, man! Don't sneer and joke at me. I'm not speaking like a senile old man or a fool, who uses his age as an excuse to boast about what he did when he was young, or what he'd do if he weren't old. Know this, Claudio, to your face: you have so wronged my innocent child and me that I'm forced to put aside my dignity and, with my grey hair and the bruises of many years, challenge you to a duel, man to man. I say you have lied about my innocent child. Your slander has gone right through her heart, and she lies buried with her ancestors; oh, in a tomb where scandal never lay before, except this one about her, invented by your villainy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["My villany?"],"id":873,"m":"My villainy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Thine, Claudio; thine, I say."],"id":874,"m":"Yours, Claudio; yours, I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["You say not right, old man,"],"id":875,"m":"You're wrong, old man."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["My lord, my lord,","I'll prove it on his body, if he dare,","Despite his nice fence and his active practice,","His May of youth and bloom of lustihood."],"id":876,"m":"My lord, my lord, I'll prove it on his body, if he dares, despite his fancy swordsmanship and his constant practice, his youth and his strength in its prime."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Away! I will not have to do with you."],"id":877,"m":"Go away! I won't have anything to do with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Canst thou so daff me? Thou hast kill'd my child;","If thou kill'st me, boy, thou shalt kill a man."],"id":878,"m":"Can you brush me off like that? You've killed my child. If you kill me, boy, you'll kill a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["He shall kill two of us, and men indeed:","But that's no matter; let him kill one first:","Win me and wear me; let him answer me.","Come, follow me, boy; come, sir boy, come, follow me.","Sir boy, I'll whip you from your foining fence;","Nay, as I am a gentleman, I will."],"id":879,"m":"He'll have to kill two of us, and real men too. But that doesn't matter; let him kill one first. Beat me and you can have me; let him answer to me. Come, follow me, boy. Come, sir boy, come, follow me. Sir boy, I'll whip you out of your fancy fencing tricks. Yes, as I'm a gentleman, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Brother,—"],"id":880,"m":"Brother,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Content yourself. God knows I lov'd my niece;","And she is dead, slander'd to death by villains,","That dare as well answer a man indeed","As I dare take a serpent by the tongue.","Boys, apes, braggarts, Jacks, milksops!"],"id":881,"m":"Calm down. God knows I loved my niece, and she's dead, slandered to death by villains who'd no more dare face a real man than I'd dare grab a snake by its tongue. Boys, apes, braggarts, rascals, milksops!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Brother Antony,—"],"id":882,"m":"Brother Antonio,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Hold your content. What, man! I know them, yea,","And what they weigh, even to the utmost scruple,","Scambling, out-facing, fashion-monging boys,","That lie and cog and flout, deprave and slander,","Go antickly, show outward hideousness,","And speak off half a dozen dangerous words,","How they might hurt their enemies, if they durst;","And this is all!"],"id":883,"m":"Be quiet. What, man! I know them, yes, and exactly what they're worth, down to the last ounce. Brawling, bullying, fashion-mad boys, who lie and cheat and mock, insult and slander, dress up like clowns, put on a fierce look and spout half a dozen threatening words about how they'd hurt their enemies if they dared; and that's all they are!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["But, brother Antony,—"],"id":884,"m":"But, brother Antonio,—"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Come, 'tis no matter:","Do not you meddle, let me deal in this."],"id":885,"m":"Come, it doesn't matter. Don't you interfere; let me deal with this."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Gentlemen both, we will not wake your patience.","My heart is sorry for your daughter's death;","But, on my honour, she was charg'd with nothing","But what was true and very full of proof."],"id":886,"m":"Gentlemen, we won't try your patience any further. I'm sorry with all my heart for your daughter's death; but, on my honour, she was accused of nothing that wasn't true and fully proved."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["My lord, my lord—"],"id":887,"m":"My lord, my lord—"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I will not hear you."],"id":888,"m":"I won't listen to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["No? Come, brother, away. I will be heard.—"],"id":889,"m":"No? Come, brother, let's go. I will be heard.—"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["And shall, or some of us will smart for it."],"id":890,"m":"And you will, or some of us will suffer for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt LEONATO and ANTONIO.","id":891},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BENEDICK.","id":892},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["See, see; here comes the man we went to seek."],"id":893,"m":"Look, look, here comes the man we went to find."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Now, signior, what news?"],"id":894,"m":"Well, signior, what's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Good day, my lord."],"id":895,"m":"Good day, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Welcome, signior: you are almost come to part almost a fray."],"id":896,"m":"Welcome, signior. You've almost arrived in time to break up almost a fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["We had like to have had our two noses snapped off with two old","men without teeth."],"id":897,"m":"We nearly had our two noses bitten off by two old men without any teeth."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Leonato and his brother. What think'st thou? Had we fought, I","doubt we should have been too young for them."],"id":898,"m":"Leonato and his brother. What do you think? If we'd fought, I suspect we'd have been too young for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["In a false quarrel there is no true valour. I came to seek you","both."],"id":899,"m":"There's no real courage in an unjust quarrel. I came to find you both."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["We have been up and down to seek thee; for we are high-proof","melancholy, and would fain have it beaten away. Wilt thou use","thy wit?"],"id":900,"m":"We've been looking everywhere for you, because we're terribly depressed and would love to have it chased away. Will you use your wit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["It is in my scabbard; shall I draw it?"],"id":901,"m":"It's in my scabbard. Shall I draw it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Dost thou wear thy wit by thy side?"],"id":902,"m":"Do you wear your wit at your side?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Never any did so, though very many have been beside their wit. I","will bid thee draw, as we do the minstrels; draw, to pleasure us."],"id":903,"m":"No one ever did that, though plenty have been beside themselves. I'll ask you to draw, the way we ask musicians to draw their bows: draw, to entertain us."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["As I am an honest man, he looks pale. Art thou sick, or angry?"],"id":904,"m":"As I'm an honest man, he looks pale. Are you ill, or angry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["What, courage, man! What though care killed a cat, thou hast","mettle enough in thee to kill care."],"id":905,"m":"What, cheer up, man! Even though worry killed the cat, you've got enough spirit in you to kill worry."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Sir, I shall meet your wit in the career, an you charge it","against me. I pray you choose another subject."],"id":906,"m":"Sir, I'll meet your wit head on, like a jousting knight, if you charge it at me. Please choose another subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Nay then, give him another staff: this last was broke cross."],"id":907,"m":"No, then, give him another lance; that last one broke sideways."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["By this light, he changes more and more: I think he be angry","indeed."],"id":908,"m":"By this light, he's changing colour more and more. I think he really is angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["If he be, he knows how to turn his girdle."],"id":909,"m":"If he is, he knows what to do about it: he can turn his belt round and get his sword ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Shall I speak a word in your ear?"],"id":910,"m":"May I have a word in your ear?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["God bless me from a challenge!"],"id":911,"m":"God save me from a challenge!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["[Aside to CLAUDIO.]","You are a villain, I jest not: I will make it good how you dare,","with what you dare, and when you dare. Do me right, or I will protest","your cowardice. You have killed a sweet lady, and her death shall","fall heavy on you. Let me hear from you."],"id":912,"m":"You're a villain; I'm not joking. I'll prove it however you dare, with whatever weapon you dare, and whenever you dare. Give me satisfaction, or I'll declare you a coward. You've killed a sweet lady, and her death will fall heavily on you. Let me hear from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Well I will meet you, so I may have good cheer."],"id":913,"m":"Well, I'll meet you, as long as I get a good meal out of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["What, a feast, a feast?"],"id":914,"m":"What, a feast, a feast?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I' faith, I thank him; he hath bid me to a calf's-head and a capon,","the which if I do not carve most curiously, say my knife's naught.","Shall I not find a woodcock too?"],"id":915,"m":"Yes, really, I thank him. He has invited me to a calf's head and a castrated cockerel, and if I don't carve them very skilfully, say my knife's no good. Won't I find a woodcock, a stupid bird, too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Sir, your wit ambles well; it goes easily."],"id":916,"m":"Sir, your wit trots along nicely; it moves at an easy pace."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["I'll tell thee how Beatrice praised thy wit the other day. I said,","thou hadst a fine wit. 'True,' says she, 'a fine little one.'","'No,' said I, 'a great wit.'","'Right,' said she, 'a great gross one.'","'Nay,' said I, 'a good wit.'","'Just,' said she, 'it hurts nobody.'","'Nay,' said I, 'the gentleman is wise.'","'Certain,' said she, a wise gentleman.'","'Nay,' said I, 'he hath the tongues.'","'That I believe' said she, 'for he swore a thing to me on Monday","night, which he forswore on Tuesday morning: there's a double tongue;","there's two tongues.'","Thus did she, an hour together, trans-shape thy particular virtues;","yet at last she concluded with a sigh, thou wast the properest man in","Italy."],"id":917,"m":"I'll tell you how Beatrice praised your wit the other day. I said you had a fine wit. 'True,' she said, 'a fine little one.' 'No,' I said, 'a great wit.' 'Right,' she said, 'a great fat one.' 'No,' I said, 'a good wit.' 'Exactly,' she said, 'it doesn't hurt anybody.' 'No,' I said, 'the gentleman is wise.' 'Certainly,' she said, 'a wise gentleman.' 'No,' I said, 'he speaks several languages.' 'That I believe,' she said, 'for he swore something to me on Monday night and took it back on Tuesday morning: there's a double tongue, there's two tongues.' Like that, for a whole hour, she twisted every one of your virtues out of shape; and yet at last she concluded, with a sigh, that you were the most handsome man in Italy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["For the which she wept heartily and said she cared not."],"id":918,"m":"And for that she cried her heart out and said she didn't care."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Yea, that she did; but yet, for all that, an if she did not hate him","deadly, she would love him dearly. The old man's daughter told us all."],"id":919,"m":"Yes, she did. But all the same, if she didn't hate him to death, she would love him dearly. The old man's daughter told us everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["All, all; and moreover, God saw him when he was hid in the garden."],"id":920,"m":"Everything, everything; and what's more, God saw him when he was hiding in the garden, like Adam."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["But when shall we set the savage bull's horns on the sensible","Benedick's head?"],"id":921,"m":"But when shall we put the wild bull's horns on the sensible Benedick's head?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Yea, and text underneath, 'Here dwells Benedick the married man!'"],"id":922,"m":"Yes, with the words underneath: 'Here lives Benedick the married man!'"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Fare you well, boy: you know my mind. I will leave you now to your","gossip-like humour; you break jests as braggarts do their blades,","which, God be thanked, hurt not. My lord, for your many courtesies I","thank you: I must discontinue your company.","Your brother the bastard is fled from Messina: you have, among you,","killed a sweet and innocent lady. For my Lord Lack-beard there, he and","I shall meet; and till then, peace be with him."],"id":923,"m":"Goodbye, boy. You know what I mean. I'll leave you now to your gossiping mood. You crack jokes the way braggarts break swords, which, thank God, hurt nobody. My lord, I thank you for your many kindnesses, but I must leave your company. Your brother the bastard has fled from Messina. Between you, you have killed a sweet and innocent lady. As for my Lord No-beard there, he and I will meet; and until then, peace be with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":924},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["He is in earnest."],"id":925,"m":"He's serious."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["In most profound earnest; and, I'll warrant you, for the love of","Beatrice."],"id":926,"m":"Deadly serious; and, I promise you, it's for the love of Beatrice."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["And hath challenged thee?"],"id":927,"m":"And he's challenged you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Most sincerely."],"id":928,"m":"Most sincerely."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["What a pretty thing man is when he goes in his doublet and hose","and leaves off his wit!"],"id":929,"m":"What a fine thing a man is when he goes out in his jacket and trousers and leaves his brains behind!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["He is then a giant to an ape; but then is an ape a doctor to such","a man."],"id":930,"m":"Then he's a giant to an ape; but then an ape is a learned doctor compared with such a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["But, soft you; let me be: pluck up, my heart, and be sad! Did he","not say my brother was fled?"],"id":931,"m":"But wait, let me think. Rouse yourself, my heart, and be serious! Didn't he say my brother had fled?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DOGBERRY, VERGES, and the Watch, with CONRADE and BORACHIO.","id":932},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Come you, sir: if justice cannot tame you, she shall ne'er weigh more","reasons in her balance. Nay, an you be a cursing hypocrite once, you","must be looked to."],"id":933,"m":"Come along, sir. If justice can't tame you, she'll never weigh any more reasons in her scales. No, if you're once a cursing hypocrite, you must be watched."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["How now! two of my brother's men bound! Borachio, one!"],"id":934,"m":"What's this! Two of my brother's men tied up! Borachio is one of them!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Hearken after their offence, my lord."],"id":935,"m":"Ask what they've done, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Officers, what offence have these men done?"],"id":936,"m":"Officers, what crime have these men committed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Marry, sir, they have committed false report; moreover, they have","spoken untruths; secondarily, they are slanders; sixth and lastly,","they have belied a lady; thirdly, they have verified unjust things;","and to conclude, they are lying knaves."],"id":937,"m":"Why, sir, they have committed false report; moreover, they have spoken untruths; secondarily, they are slanders; sixth and lastly, they have lied about a lady; thirdly, they have verified unjust things; and to conclude, they are lying villains."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["First, I ask thee what they have done; thirdly, I ask thee what's","their offence; sixth and lastly, why they are committed; and, to","conclude, what you lay to their charge?"],"id":938,"m":"First, I ask you what they've done; thirdly, I ask you what their crime is; sixth and lastly, why they've been arrested; and to conclude, what are you charging them with?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Rightly reasoned, and in his own division; and, by my troth, there's","one meaning well suited."],"id":939,"m":"Well argued, and in his own order; and upon my word, there's one meaning dressed up in several suits."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Who have you offended, masters, that you are thus bound to your","answer? this learned constable is too cunning to be understood.","What's your offence?"],"id":940,"m":"Whom have you offended, gentlemen, that you're tied up to answer for it? This learned constable is too clever to be understood. What's your crime?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Sweet prince, let me go no further to mine answer: do you hear me, and","let this count kill me. I have deceived even your very eyes: what your","wisdoms could not discover, these shallow fools have brought to light;","who, in the night overheard me confessing to this man how Don John","your brother incensed me to slander the Lady Hero; how you were","brought into the orchard and saw me court Margaret in Hero's garments;","how you disgraced her, when you should marry her.","My villany they have upon record; which I had rather seal with my","death than repeat over to my shame. The lady is dead upon mine and my","master's false accusation; and, briefly, I desire nothing but the","reward of a villain."],"id":941,"m":"Sweet prince, let me go no further before answering. Listen to me, and let this count kill me. I have deceived your very eyes. What your wisdom couldn't discover, these simple fools have brought to light. They overheard me in the night confessing to this man how Don John your brother urged me on to slander the Lady Hero; how you were brought into the orchard and saw me court Margaret dressed in Hero's clothes; how you disgraced her when you should have married her. They have recorded my villainy, which I'd rather seal with my death than repeat to my shame. The lady is dead because of my false accusation and my master's; and, in short, I want nothing but a villain's reward."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Runs not this speech like iron through your blood?"],"id":942,"m":"Doesn't this speech run through your blood like iron?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I have drunk poison whiles he utter'd it."],"id":943,"m":"I've drunk poison while he spoke it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["But did my brother set thee on to this?"],"id":944,"m":"But did my brother put you up to this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Yea; and paid me richly for the practice of it."],"id":945,"m":"Yes, and paid me richly for doing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["He is compos'd and fram'd of treachery:","And fled he is upon this villany."],"id":946,"m":"He's made and shaped entirely out of treachery, and he has fled because of this villainy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Sweet Hero! now thy image doth appear In the rare semblance that","I lov'd it first."],"id":947,"m":"Sweet Hero! Now your image appears in the rare beauty I loved at first."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Come, bring away the plaintiffs: by this time our sexton hath","reformed Signior Leonato of the matter. And masters, do not forget","to specify, when time and place shall serve, that I am an ass."],"id":948,"m":"Come, bring away the plaintiffs (he means defendants). By now our clerk has reformed (informed) Signior Leonato about the matter. And gentlemen, don't forget to state, when the time and place are right, that I am an ass."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERGES","lines":["Here, here comes Master Signior Leonato, and the sexton too."],"id":949,"m":"Here, here comes Master Signior Leonato, and the clerk too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter LEONATO, ANTONIO, and the Sexton.","id":950},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Which is the villain? Let me see his eyes,","That, when I note another man like him,","I may avoid him. Which of these is he?"],"id":951,"m":"Which is the villain? Let me see his eyes, so that when I notice another man like him, I can avoid him. Which of these is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["If you would know your wronger, look on me."],"id":952,"m":"If you want to know who wronged you, look at me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Art thou the slave that with thy breath hast kill'd","Mine innocent child?"],"id":953,"m":"Are you the wretch who killed my innocent child with your breath?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["Yea, even I alone."],"id":954,"m":"Yes, me alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["No, not so, villain; thou beliest thyself:","Here stand a pair of honourable men;","A third is fled, that had a hand in it.","I thank you, princes, for my daughter's death:","Record it with your high and worthy deeds.","'Twas bravely done, if you bethink you of it."],"id":955,"m":"No, not so, villain; you're lying about yourself. Here stand a pair of honourable men; a third, who had a hand in it, has fled. I thank you, princes, for my daughter's death. Record it with your noble and worthy deeds. It was bravely done, if you think about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I know not how to pray your patience;","Yet I must speak. Choose your revenge yourself;","Impose me to what penance your invention","Can lay upon my sin: yet sinn'd I not","But in mistaking."],"id":956,"m":"I don't know how to ask for your patience, yet I must speak. Choose your revenge yourself. Give me whatever penance you can think of for my sin; and yet I didn't sin, except by being mistaken."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["By my soul, nor I:","And yet, to satisfy this good old man,","I would bend under any heavy weight","That he'll enjoin me to."],"id":957,"m":"On my soul, nor did I. And yet, to satisfy this good old man, I'd bend under any heavy burden he orders me to carry."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I cannot bid you bid my daughter live;","That were impossible; but, I pray you both,","Possess the people in Messina here","How innocent she died; and if your love","Can labour aught in sad invention,","Hang her an epitaph upon her tomb,","And sing it to her bones: sing it to-night.","To-morrow morning come you to my house,","And since you could not be my son-in-law,","Be yet my nephew. My brother hath a daughter,","Almost the copy of my child that's dead,","And she alone is heir to both of us:","Give her the right you should have given her cousin,","And so dies my revenge."],"id":958,"m":"I can't order you to tell my daughter to live; that would be impossible. But I beg you both, let the people here in Messina know how innocent she was when she died. And if your love can produce anything in a sad poem, hang an epitaph on her tomb and sing it to her bones; sing it tonight. Tomorrow morning come to my house, and since you couldn't be my son-in-law, be my nephew instead. My brother has a daughter who is almost a copy of my child who's dead, and she alone is heir to both of us. Give her the rights you should have given her cousin, and so my revenge dies."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["O noble sir,","Your over-kindness doth wring tears from me!","I do embrace your offer; and dispose","For henceforth of poor Claudio."],"id":959,"m":"Oh, noble sir, your great kindness wrings tears from me! I accept your offer, and from now on poor Claudio is yours to command."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["To-morrow then I will expect your coming;","To-night I take my leave. This naughty man","Shall face to face be brought to Margaret,","Who, I believe, was pack'd in all this wrong,","Hir'd to it by your brother."],"id":960,"m":"Then I'll expect you tomorrow. Tonight I'll say goodbye. This wicked man will be brought face to face with Margaret, who I believe was in on all this wrong, hired to do it by your brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"BORACHIO","lines":["No, by my soul she was not;","Nor knew not what she did when she spoke to me;","But always hath been just and virtuous","In anything that I do know by her."],"id":961,"m":"No, on my soul, she wasn't. She didn't know what she was doing when she spoke to me, and she has always been honest and good in everything I know of her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Moreover, sir,—which, indeed, is not under white and black,— this","plaintiff here, the offender, did call me ass: I beseech you, let it","be remembered in his punishment.","And also, the watch heard them talk of one Deformed: they say he wears","a key in his ear and a lock hanging by it, and borrows money in God's","name, the which he hath used so long and never paid, that now men grow","hard-hearted, and will lend nothing for God's sake. Pray you, examine","him upon that point."],"id":962,"m":"Moreover, sir, which indeed isn't written down in black and white, this plaintiff here, the offender, called me an ass. I beg you, let it be remembered in his punishment. And also, the watch heard them talk of one Deformed. They say he wears a key in his ear and a lock hanging by it, and borrows money in God's name, which he has done for so long without ever paying it back that men are growing hard-hearted and won't lend anything for God's sake. Please question him on that point."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["I thank thee for thy care and honest pains."],"id":963,"m":"Thank you for your care and honest efforts."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["Your worship speaks like a most thankful and reverent youth, and","I praise God for you."],"id":964,"m":"Your worship speaks like a most thankful and respectful youth, and I praise God for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["There's for thy pains."],"id":965,"m":"Here's something for your trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["God save the foundation!"],"id":966,"m":"God save the foundation! (He thanks him as if he were a charity.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Go, I discharge thee of thy prisoner, and I thank thee."],"id":967,"m":"Go. I release you from your prisoner, and I thank you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOGBERRY","lines":["I leave an arrant knave with your worship; which I beseech your","worship to correct yourself, for the example of others. God keep","your worship! I wish your worship well; God restore you to health!","I humbly give you leave to depart, and if a merry meeting may be","wished, God prohibit it! Come, neighbour."],"id":968,"m":"I leave a complete villain with your worship, whom I beg your worship to correct yourself, as an example to others. God keep your worship! I wish your worship well; God restore you to health! I humbly give you permission to leave; and if a happy meeting may be wished for, God prohibit (he means permit) it! Come, neighbour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt DOGBERRY and VERGES.","id":969},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Until to-morrow morning, lords, farewell."],"id":970,"m":"Until tomorrow morning, lords, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Farewell, my lords: we look for you to-morrow."],"id":971,"m":"Goodbye, my lords. We expect you tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["We will not fail."],"id":972,"m":"We won't fail."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["To-night I'll mourn with Hero."],"id":973,"m":"Tonight I'll mourn with Hero."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt DON PEDRO and CLAUDIO.","id":974},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["[To the Watch.] Bring you these fellows on. We'll talk with","Margaret, How her acquaintance grew with this lewd fellow."],"id":975,"m":"Bring these fellows along. We'll talk with Margaret about how she came to know this wicked fellow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":976},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Leonato's Garden.","id":977},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BENEDICK and MARGARET, meeting.","id":978},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Pray thee, sweet Mistress Margaret, deserve well at my hands by","helping me to the speech of Beatrice."],"id":979,"m":"Please, sweet Mistress Margaret, earn my gratitude by helping me to speak with Beatrice."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Will you then write me a sonnet in praise of my beauty?"],"id":980,"m":"Will you write me a sonnet praising my beauty, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["In so high a style, Margaret, that no man living shall come over","it; for, in most comely truth, thou deservest it."],"id":981,"m":"In such a high style, Margaret, that no living man will come over it, for in all honest truth, you deserve it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["To have no man come over me! why, shall I always keep below stairs?"],"id":982,"m":"No man come over me! Why, am I always to stay downstairs with the servants?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Thy wit is as quick as the greyhound's mouth; it catches."],"id":983,"m":"Your wit is as quick as a greyhound's mouth: it catches."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["And yours as blunt as the fencer's foils, which hit, but hurt not."],"id":984,"m":"And yours is as blunt as a fencer's practice swords, which hit but don't hurt."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["A most manly wit, Margaret; it will not hurt a woman: and so, I","pray thee, call Beatrice. I give thee the bucklers."],"id":985,"m":"A very manly wit, Margaret: it won't hurt a woman. And so, please, call Beatrice. I surrender; I give you the shields."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Give us the swords, we have bucklers of our own."],"id":986,"m":"Give us the swords. We have shields of our own."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["If you use them, Margaret, you must put in the pikes with a vice;","and they are dangerous weapons for maids."],"id":987,"m":"If you use them, Margaret, you must screw in the spikes with a vice; and they're dangerous weapons for virgins."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Well, I will call Beatrice to you, who I think hath legs."],"id":988,"m":"Well, I'll call Beatrice for you, who I think has legs."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["And therefore will come."],"id":989,"m":"And so she'll come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit MARGARET.","id":990},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","cont":1,"lines":["~The god of love, ~That sits above, And knows me, and knows me, ~How","pitiful I deserve,— I mean, in singing: but in loving, Leander the","good swimmer, Troilus the first employer of panders, and a whole book","full of these quondam carpet-mongers, whose names yet run smoothly in","the even road of a blank verse, why, they were never so truly turned","over and over as my poor self in love.","Marry, I cannot show it in rime; I have tried: I can find out no rime","to 'lady' but 'baby', an innocent rhyme; for 'scorn,' 'horn', a hard","rime; for 'school', 'fool', a babbling rhyme; very ominous endings:","no, I was not born under a riming planet, nor I cannot woo in festival","terms."],"id":991,"m":"'The god of love, who sits above, and knows me, and knows me, how pitiful I deserve...' I mean at singing. But at loving: Leander the good swimmer, Troilus the first man to use a go-between, and a whole book full of these old-time ladies' men, whose names still run smoothly along the even road of blank verse, why, they were never so truly turned head over heels as my poor self in love. But I can't show it in rhyme. I've tried. I can't find a rhyme for 'lady' except 'baby', a childish rhyme; for 'scorn', 'horn', a harsh rhyme; for 'school', 'fool', a silly rhyme. Very unlucky endings. No, I wasn't born under a rhyming planet, and I can't woo in fancy holiday language."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BEATRICE.","id":992},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","cont":1,"lines":["Sweet Beatrice, wouldst thou come when I called thee?"],"id":993,"m":"Sweet Beatrice, would you come when I called you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Yea, signior; and depart when you bid me."],"id":994,"m":"Yes, signior, and leave when you tell me to."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["O, stay but till then!"],"id":995,"m":"Oh, stay just until then!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["'Then' is spoken; fare you well now: and yet, ere I go, let me go with","that I came for; which is, with knowing what hath passed between you","and Claudio."],"id":996,"m":"'Then' has been said, so goodbye now. And yet before I go, let me go with what I came for, which is knowing what has passed between you and Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Only foul words; and thereupon I will kiss thee."],"id":997,"m":"Only foul words; and for that I'll kiss you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Foul words is but foul wind, and foul wind is but foul breath, and","foul breath is noisome; therefore I will depart unkissed."],"id":998,"m":"Foul words are only foul wind, and foul wind is only foul breath, and foul breath is disgusting, so I'll leave unkissed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Thou hast frighted the word out of his right sense, so forcible is thy","wit. But I must tell thee plainly, Claudio undergoes my challenge, and","either I must shortly hear from him, or I will subscribe him a coward.","And, I pray thee now, tell me, for which of my bad parts didst thou","first fall in love with me?"],"id":999,"m":"You've frightened the word out of its proper meaning, your wit is so forceful. But I must tell you plainly: Claudio has received my challenge, and either I'll hear from him soon or I'll publicly declare him a coward. And now, please tell me, for which of my bad qualities did you first fall in love with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["For them all together; which maintained so politic a state of evil","that they will not admit any good part to intermingle with them.","But for which of my good parts did you first suffer love for me?"],"id":1000,"m":"For all of them together, which run such a well-organised state of evil that they won't let any good quality mix with them. But for which of my good qualities did you first suffer love for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["'Suffer love,' a good epithet! I do suffer love indeed, for I love","thee against my will."],"id":1001,"m":"'Suffer love': a good phrase! I do suffer love indeed, because I love you against my will."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["In spite of your heart, I think. Alas, poor heart! If you spite it","for my sake, I will spite it for yours; for I will never love that","which my friend hates."],"id":1002,"m":"In spite of your heart, I think. Ah, poor heart! If you spite it for my sake, I'll spite it for yours; because I'll never love something my friend hates."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Thou and I are too wise to woo peaceably."],"id":1003,"m":"You and I are too clever to woo peacefully."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["It appears not in this confession: there's not one wise man among","twenty that will praise himself."],"id":1004,"m":"That doesn't show in this confession. Not one wise man in twenty will praise himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["An old, an old instance, Beatrice, that lived in the time of good","neighbours. If a man do not erect in this age his own tomb ere he","dies, he shall live no longer in monument than the bell rings and","the widow weeps."],"id":1005,"m":"An old, old saying, Beatrice, from the days of good neighbours who praised each other. In this age, if a man doesn't put up his own monument before he dies, he'll be remembered no longer than the funeral bell rings and the widow weeps."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["And how long is that think you?"],"id":1006,"m":"And how long is that, do you think?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Question: why, an hour in clamour and a quarter in rheum: therefore is","it most expedient for the wise,—if Don Worm, his conscience, find no","impediment to the contrary,—to be the trumpet of his own virtues, as","I am to myself. So much for praising myself, who, I myself will bear","witness, is praiseworthy. And now tell me, how doth your cousin?"],"id":1007,"m":"Good question. Why, an hour of bell-ringing and a quarter of an hour of tears. So it's best for the wise man, if Sir Worm, his conscience, has no objection, to blow the trumpet for his own virtues, as I do for myself. So much for praising myself, and I myself will testify that I'm praiseworthy. And now tell me, how is your cousin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Very ill."],"id":1008,"m":"Very ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["And how do you?"],"id":1009,"m":"And how are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Very ill too."],"id":1010,"m":"Very ill too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Serve God, love me, and mend. There will I leave you too, for here","comes one in haste."],"id":1011,"m":"Serve God, love me, and get better. I'll leave you there too, because here comes someone in a hurry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter URSULA.","id":1012},{"t":"sp","who":"URSULA","lines":["Madam, you must come to your uncle. Yonder's old coil at home: it is","proved, my Lady Hero hath been falsely accused, the prince and Claudio","mightily abused; and Don John is the author of all, who is fled and","gone. Will you come presently?"],"id":1013,"m":"Madam, you must come to your uncle. There's a great commotion at home. It's been proved that my Lady Hero was falsely accused, the prince and Claudio badly deceived, and Don John is behind it all, and he has fled. Will you come at once?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Will you go hear this news, signior?"],"id":1014,"m":"Will you come and hear this news, signior?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I will live in thy heart, die in thy lap, and be buried in thy eyes;","and moreover I will go with thee to thy uncle's."],"id":1015,"m":"I'll live in your heart, die in your lap and be buried in your eyes; and what's more, I'll go with you to your uncle's."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1016},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"The Inside of a Church.","id":1017},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON PEDRO, CLAUDIO, and Attendants, with music and tapers","id":1018},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Is this the monument of Leonato?"],"id":1019,"m":"Is this Leonato's family tomb?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["It is, my lord."],"id":1020,"m":"It is, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["[Reads from a scroll.]","~Done to death by slanderous tongues","~Was the Hero that here lies:","~Death, in guerdon of her wrongs,","~Gives her fame which never dies.","~So the life that died with shame","~Lives in death with glorious fame.","~Hang thou there upon the tomb,","~Praising her when I am dumb.","Now, music, sound, and sing your solemn hymn."],"id":1021,"m":"'Done to death by slandering tongues was the Hero who lies here. Death, as a reward for her wrongs, gives her fame that never dies. So the life that died in shame lives on in death with glorious fame.' Hang there on the tomb, praising her when I am silent. Now, music, play and sing your solemn hymn."},{"t":"sp","who":"BALTHAZAR","lines":["~Pardon, goddess of the night,","~Those that slew thy virgin knight;","~For the which, with songs of woe,","~Round about her tomb they go.","~Midnight, assist our moan;","~Help us to sigh and groan,","~Heavily, heavily:","~Graves, yawn and yield your dead,","~Till death be uttered,","~Heavily, heavily."],"id":1022,"m":"Forgive, goddess of the night, those who killed your virgin follower; for which, with songs of sorrow, they walk around her tomb. Midnight, help our mourning; help us to sigh and groan, heavily, heavily. Graves, open and give up your dead, until death is fully spoken of, heavily, heavily."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Now, unto thy bones good night!","Yearly will I do this rite."],"id":1023,"m":"Now, good night to your bones! I'll perform this ritual every year."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Good morrow, masters: put your torches out.","The wolves have prey'd; and look, the gentle day,","Before the wheels of Phoebus, round about","~Dapples the drowsy east with spots of grey.","Thanks to you all, and leave us: fare you well."],"id":1024,"m":"Good morning, gentlemen. Put your torches out. The wolves have finished hunting; and look, the gentle dawn, ahead of the sun god's chariot, is dappling the sleepy east with spots of grey. Thank you all, and leave us. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Good morrow, masters: each his several way."],"id":1025,"m":"Good morning, gentlemen. Each go your own way."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Come, let us hence, and put on other weeds; And then to Leonato's","we will go."],"id":1026,"m":"Come, let's go and change our clothes, and then we'll go to Leonato's."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And Hymen now with luckier issue speed's,","Than this for whom we rend'red up this woe!"],"id":1027,"m":"And may Hymen, god of marriage, bring us a happier outcome than the one for whom we've performed this mourning!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1028},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"A Room in Leonato's House.","id":1029},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LEONATO, ANTONIO, BENEDICK, BEATRICE, MARGARET, URSULA, FRIAR FRANCIS, and HERO.","id":1030},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["Did I not tell you she was innocent?"],"id":1031,"m":"Didn't I tell you she was innocent?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["So are the prince and Claudio, who accus'd her","Upon the error that you heard debated:","But Margaret was in some fault for this,","Although against her will, as it appears","In the true course of all the question."],"id":1032,"m":"So are the prince and Claudio, who accused her because of the mistake you heard discussed. But Margaret was partly to blame for this, although it was against her will, as appears from the whole investigation."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Well, I am glad that all things sort so well."],"id":1033,"m":"Well, I'm glad everything has turned out so well."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["And so am I, being else by faith enforc'd","To call young Claudio to a reckoning for it."],"id":1034,"m":"And so am I, since otherwise I'd be bound by my promise to call young Claudio to account for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Well, daughter, and you gentlewomen all,","Withdraw into a chamber by yourselves,","And when I send for you, come hither mask'd:","The prince and Claudio promis'd by this hour","To visit me."],"id":1035,"m":"Well, daughter, and all you ladies, go into a room by yourselves, and when I send for you, come here masked. The prince and Claudio promised to visit me at this hour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Ladies.","id":1036},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","cont":1,"lines":["You know your office, brother;","You must be father to your brother's daughter,","And give her to young Claudio."],"id":1037,"m":"You know your part, brother. You must act as father to your brother's daughter and give her to young Claudio."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["Which I will do with confirm'd countenance."],"id":1038,"m":"Which I'll do with a straight face."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Friar, I must entreat your pains, I think."],"id":1039,"m":"Friar, I think I must ask for your help."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["To do what, signior?"],"id":1040,"m":"To do what, signior?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["To bind me, or undo me; one of them.","Signior Leonato, truth it is, good signior,","Your niece regards me with an eye of favour."],"id":1041,"m":"To bind me or undo me, one or the other. Signior Leonato, the truth is, good signior, your niece looks at me with favour."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["That eye my daughter lent her: 'tis most true."],"id":1042,"m":"That eye was lent to her by my daughter; that's very true."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["And I do with an eye of love requite her."],"id":1043,"m":"And I return her love with a loving eye."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["The sight whereof I think, you had from me,","From Claudio, and the prince. But what's your will?"],"id":1044,"m":"I think you got that view of her from me, from Claudio and from the prince. But what is it you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Your answer, sir, is enigmatical:","But, for my will, my will is your good will","May stand with ours, this day to be conjoin'd","In the state of honourable marriage:","In which, good friar, I shall desire your help."],"id":1045,"m":"Your answer, sir, is a riddle. But as for what I want, I want your goodwill to agree with ours, so that we can be joined today in the state of honourable marriage; and for that, good friar, I'll need your help."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["My heart is with your liking."],"id":1046,"m":"My heart agrees with your wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["And my help. Here comes the prince and Claudio."],"id":1047,"m":"And my help with them. Here come the prince and Claudio."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DON PEDRO and CLAUDIO, with Attendants.","id":1048},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Good morrow to this fair assembly."],"id":1049,"m":"Good morning to this fine company."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Good morrow, prince; good morrow, Claudio:","We here attend you. Are you yet determin'd","To-day to marry with my brother's daughter?"],"id":1050,"m":"Good morning, prince; good morning, Claudio. We're waiting for you here. Are you still determined to marry my brother's daughter today?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I'll hold my mind, were she an Ethiope."],"id":1051,"m":"I'll stick to my decision even if she were an Ethiopian."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Call her forth, brother: here's the friar ready."],"id":1052,"m":"Call her in, brother. The friar is ready."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit ANTONIO.","id":1053},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["Good morrow, Benedick. Why, what's the matter,","That you have such a February face,","So full of frost, of storm and cloudiness?"],"id":1054,"m":"Good morning, Benedick. Why, what's the matter, that you have such a February face, so full of frost, storms and clouds?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I think he thinks upon the savage bull.","Tush! fear not, man, we'll tip thy horns with gold,","And all Europa shall rejoice at thee,","As once Europa did at lusty Jove,","When he would play the noble beast in love."],"id":1055,"m":"I think he's thinking about the wild bull. Come on, don't be afraid, man; we'll tip your horns with gold, and all Europe will delight in you, as Europa once delighted in lusty Jove when he played the noble bull in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Bull Jove, sir, had an amiable low:","And some such strange bull leap'd your father's cow,","And got a calf in that same noble feat,","Much like to you, for you have just his bleat."],"id":1056,"m":"Jove the bull, sir, had a lovely moo; and some strange bull like that jumped on your father's cow and fathered a calf in that same noble act, very much like you, because you have exactly his bleat."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["For this I owe you: here comes other reckonings."],"id":1057,"m":"I'll pay you back for this. Here come other matters to settle."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter ANTONIO, with the ladies masked.","id":1058},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","cont":1,"lines":["Which is the lady I must seize upon?"],"id":1059,"m":"Which is the lady I must take?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONIO","lines":["This same is she, and I do give you her."],"id":1060,"m":"This is the one, and I give her to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Why then, she's mine. Sweet, let me see your face."],"id":1061,"m":"Why then, she's mine. Sweetheart, let me see your face."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["No, that you shall not, till you take her hand","Before this friar, and swear to marry her."],"id":1062,"m":"No, you shall not, until you take her hand in front of this friar and swear to marry her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Give me your hand: before this holy friar,","I am your husband, if you like of me."],"id":1063,"m":"Give me your hand. In front of this holy friar, I am your husband, if you'll have me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["And when I liv'd, I was your other wife:","[Unmasking.] And when you lov'd, you were my other husband."],"id":1064,"m":"And when I was alive, I was your other wife; and when you loved me, you were my other husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["Another Hero!"],"id":1065,"m":"Another Hero!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["Nothing certainer:","One Hero died defil'd, but I do live,","And surely as I live, I am a maid."],"id":1066,"m":"Nothing is more certain. One Hero died disgraced, but I am alive, and as surely as I live, I am a virgin."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["The former Hero! Hero that is dead!"],"id":1067,"m":"The former Hero! Hero who is dead!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["She died, my lord, but whiles her slander liv'd."],"id":1068,"m":"She was dead, my lord, only while the slander about her lived."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","lines":["All this amazement can I qualify:","When after that the holy rites are ended,","I'll tell you largely of fair Hero's death:","Meantime, let wonder seem familiar,","And to the chapel let us presently."],"id":1069,"m":"I can explain all this amazement. When the holy ceremony is over, I'll tell you all about lovely Hero's death. Meanwhile, treat these wonders as ordinary, and let's go to the chapel at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Soft and fair, friar. Which is Beatrice?"],"id":1070,"m":"Wait a moment, friar. Which one is Beatrice?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["[Unmasking.] I answer to that name. What is your will?"],"id":1071,"m":"I answer to that name. What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Do not you love me?"],"id":1072,"m":"Don't you love me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Why, no; no more than reason."],"id":1073,"m":"Why, no, no more than is reasonable."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Why, then, your uncle and the prince and Claudio","Have been deceived; for they swore you did."],"id":1074,"m":"Why, then, your uncle and the prince and Claudio have been deceived, because they swore you did."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Do not you love me?"],"id":1075,"m":"Don't you love me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Troth, no; no more than reason."],"id":1076,"m":"Honestly, no, no more than is reasonable."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["Why, then my cousin, Margaret, and Ursula,","Are much deceiv'd; for they did swear you did."],"id":1077,"m":"Why, then my cousin, Margaret and Ursula are very much deceived, because they swore you did."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["They swore that you were almost sick for me."],"id":1078,"m":"They swore you were almost sick with love for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["They swore that you were well-nigh dead for me."],"id":1079,"m":"They swore you were nearly dead with love for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Tis no such matter. Then you do not love me?"],"id":1080,"m":"That's not the case. So you don't love me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["No, truly, but in friendly recompense."],"id":1081,"m":"No, really, only as a friend returns kindness."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["Come, cousin, I am sure you love the gentleman."],"id":1082,"m":"Come, niece, I'm sure you love the gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["And I'll be sworn upon 't that he loves her;","For here's a paper written in his hand,","A halting sonnet of his own pure brain,","Fashion'd to Beatrice."],"id":1083,"m":"And I'll swear that he loves her; for here's a paper in his handwriting, a limping sonnet straight out of his own brain, written to Beatrice."},{"t":"sp","who":"HERO","lines":["And here's another,","Writ in my cousin's hand, stolen from her pocket,","Containing her affection unto Benedick."],"id":1084,"m":"And here's another, in my cousin's handwriting, stolen from her pocket, declaring her love for Benedick."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["A miracle! here's our own hands against our hearts. Come, I will","have thee; but, by this light, I take thee for pity."],"id":1085,"m":"A miracle! Here are our own hands testifying against our hearts. Come, I'll have you; but, by this light, I'm taking you out of pity."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEATRICE","lines":["I would not deny you; but, by this good day, I yield upon great","persuasion, and partly to save your life, for I was told you were","in a consumption."],"id":1086,"m":"I won't refuse you; but, by this good day, I give in only after a lot of persuasion, and partly to save your life, because I was told you were wasting away."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Peace! I will stop your mouth. [Kisses her.]"],"id":1087,"m":"Quiet! I'll stop your mouth with a kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"DON PEDRO","lines":["How dost thou, Benedick, the married man?"],"id":1088,"m":"How are you, Benedick, the married man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["I'll tell thee what, prince; a college of witcrackers cannot flout me","out of my humour. Dost thou think I care for a satire or an epigram?","No; if man will be beaten with brains, a' shall wear nothing handsome","about him.","In brief, since I do purpose to marry, I will think nothing to any","purpose that the world can say against it; and therefore never flout","at me for what I have said against it, for man is a giddy thing, and","this is my conclusion. For thy part, Claudio, I did think to have","beaten thee; but, in that thou art like to be my kinsman, live","unbruised, and love my cousin."],"id":1089,"m":"I'll tell you what, prince: a whole university of wisecrackers couldn't mock me out of my good mood. Do you think I care about a satire or a witty poem? No. If a man is going to be beaten down by clever remarks, he won't dare wear anything fine about him. In short, since I intend to marry, I'll take no notice of anything the world can say against it. So never mock me for what I've said against marriage, because man is a fickle creature, and that's my conclusion. As for you, Claudio, I was planning to beat you; but since you're going to be my relative, live unbruised, and love my cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIO","lines":["I had well hoped thou wouldst have denied Beatrice, that I might have","cudgelled thee out of thy single life, to make thee a double-dealer;","which, out of question, thou wilt be, if my cousin do not look","exceeding narrowly to thee."],"id":1090,"m":"I was hoping you'd refuse Beatrice, so I could have beaten you out of your single life and made you a double-dealer; which you certainly will be, if my cousin doesn't keep a very close eye on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Come, come, we are friends. Let's have a dance ere we are married,","that we may lighten our own hearts and our wives' heels."],"id":1091,"m":"Come, come, we're friends. Let's have a dance before we're married, to lighten our own hearts and our wives' feet."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEONATO","lines":["We'll have dancing afterward."],"id":1092,"m":"We'll have dancing afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["First, of my word; therefore play, music! Prince, thou art sad; get","thee a wife, get thee a wife: there is no staff more reverent than one","tipped with horn."],"id":1093,"m":"First, upon my word! So strike up, music! Prince, you're sad. Get yourself a wife, get yourself a wife. There's no walking stick more respectable than one tipped with horn."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Messenger.","id":1094},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord, your brother John is ta'en in flight,","And brought with armed men back to Messina."],"id":1095,"m":"My lord, your brother John has been caught running away and brought back to Messina by armed men."},{"t":"sp","who":"BENEDICK","lines":["Think not on him till to-morrow: I'll devise thee brave","punishments for him.","Strike up, pipers!"],"id":1096,"m":"Don't think about him till tomorrow. I'll think up some fine punishments for him. Strike up, pipers!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Dance. Exeunt.","id":1097}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Benedick","group":"The soldiers","desc":"A witty young lord of Padua, back from the wars with Don Pedro. Swears he will die a bachelor and trades insults with Beatrice. Tricked into believing she loves him, he decides to love her back, takes her side after Hero is shamed, challenges his friend Claudio, and marries Beatrice."},{"name":"Beatrice","group":"Leonato's household","desc":"Leonato's niece. Quick, scornful and proud of her independence; mocks Benedick and marriage. Tricked into believing Benedick loves her, she drops her pride. When Hero is slandered she is furious ('O that I were a man!') and asks Benedick to kill Claudio. Ends by marrying him."},{"name":"Claudio","group":"The soldiers","desc":"A young lord of Florence who won honour in the war. Falls for Hero at first sight but lets others do his wooing. Easily convinced she is unfaithful, he shames her at the altar. When the truth comes out he mourns at her tomb and agrees to marry Leonato's 'niece', who turns out to be Hero."},{"name":"Hero","group":"Leonato's household","desc":"Leonato's only daughter and heir. Gentle, obedient and mostly quiet in public, though witty with her women. Publicly accused of unchastity at her wedding, she faints and is reported dead on the Friar's advice. Restored and cleared, she marries Claudio."},{"name":"Don Pedro","group":"The soldiers","desc":"Prince of Aragon, a gracious commander and matchmaker. Woos Hero for Claudio and plans the trick to make Benedick and Beatrice fall in love. Deceived by his brother, he joins Claudio in shaming Hero. Ends the play alone; Benedick tells him to 'get thee a wife'."},{"name":"Don John","group":"The villains","desc":"Don Pedro's illegitimate half-brother, recently defeated in a rebellion and reconciled with him. Sullen and spiteful: 'a plain-dealing villain'. Plots to wreck Claudio's marriage, then flees. He is captured at the end."},{"name":"Leonato","group":"Leonato's household","desc":"Governor of Messina, Hero's father and Beatrice's uncle. A warm host who joins in the gulling of Benedick. When Hero is accused he believes the princes and wishes her dead, then turns to rage and challenges Claudio. Sets the penance that leads to the second wedding."},{"name":"Antonio","group":"Leonato's household","desc":"Leonato's elderly brother. Tells Leonato the garbled news about Don Pedro's wooing, offers to fight Claudio in Act 5, and gives away the masked 'niece' at the end."},{"name":"Borachio","group":"The villains","desc":"Don John's follower; his name means 'drunkard' in Spanish. Invents the plot of wooing Margaret at Hero's window. Overheard boasting by the Watch, he confesses and clears Hero."},{"name":"Conrade","group":"The villains","desc":"Another of Don John's men. Listens to Borachio's boast and is arrested with him; calls Dogberry an ass."},{"name":"Margaret","group":"Leonato's household","desc":"One of Hero's gentlewomen; bawdy and quick. Unknowingly helps the plot by dressing in Hero's clothes and talking with Borachio at the window. Later spars with Benedick."},{"name":"Ursula","group":"Leonato's household","desc":"Hero's other gentlewoman. Helps Hero trick Beatrice in the orchard and brings news that Hero has been cleared."},{"name":"Dogberry","group":"The Watch","desc":"Master Constable of Messina. Pompous and kind, he mangles long words. His Watch uncovers the plot, but his rambling stops Leonato hearing it before the wedding. Outraged at being called an ass."},{"name":"Verges","group":"The Watch","desc":"The headborough, Dogberry's elderly partner. Agrees with everything and is constantly corrected by him."},{"name":"The Watch","group":"The Watch","desc":"Seacoal and his fellow watchmen. Simple men who, by sheer luck, overhear Borachio and arrest the villains."},{"name":"Friar Francis","group":"Others","desc":"The priest at the failed wedding. Watches Hero's face, judges her innocent, and devises the plan to report her dead so that slander will turn to grief."},{"name":"Balthazar","group":"Others","desc":"Don Pedro's musician. Sings 'Sigh no more, ladies' while Benedick hides in the arbour."},{"name":"The Sexton","group":"Others","desc":"The town clerk who records the examination of Borachio and Conrade and takes the news to Leonato."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"A messenger tells Leonato that Don Pedro has won his war and is coming to Messina, and that young Claudio fought bravely. Beatrice mocks Benedick before he even arrives, and the two clash as soon as they meet. Don Pedro accepts a month's stay. Alone with Benedick, Claudio confesses he loves Hero; Benedick scoffs at marriage. Don Pedro offers to woo Hero for Claudio in disguise at the night's masked party.","key":["The 'merry war' of wit between Beatrice and Benedick is set up at once.","Claudio falls for Hero at first sight but wants others to do his wooing.","Don Pedro's plan to woo in disguise starts a chain of overhearing and misunderstanding."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Don Pedro offer to do for Claudio?","o":["Lend him money","Woo Hero for him in disguise","Fight Benedick","Ask Beatrice for advice"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Benedick swear?","o":["To marry within a month","To die a bachelor","To leave Messina","To fight Don John"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Leonato call the quarrel between Beatrice and Benedick?","o":["A cold war","A merry war","A lovers' tiff","A dangerous feud"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Antonio tells Leonato that a servant overheard Don Pedro say he loves Hero and will propose at the dance. Leonato decides to warn Hero so that she can prepare her answer.","key":["The first of many overheard conversations, and it is garbled.","Everyone is ready to act on secondhand news.","Hero's answer is expected to follow her father's wishes."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Antonio's servant think he overheard?","o":["Claudio loves Beatrice","Don Pedro loves Hero","Don John plans a crime","Benedick loves Hero"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Leonato decide to do?","o":["Ignore it","Warn Hero so she can prepare an answer","Ask Don Pedro directly","Cancel the party"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is mistaken here?","o":["Leonato","Antonio's servant","Claudio","Hero"],"a":1}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"Don John broods with Conrade and refuses to hide his sour mood: he is 'a plain-dealing villain'. Borachio brings news that Claudio is to marry Hero, which he overheard while hiding behind a tapestry. Don John, who hates Claudio for winning glory from his defeat, resolves to spoil it.","key":["Don John's villainy has no grand motive beyond spite and envy.","Borachio's eavesdropping gives the villains their opening.","The comedy has a dark edge from the start."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Don John hate Claudio?","o":["Claudio insulted him","Claudio gained glory from Don John's defeat","Claudio loves Beatrice","Claudio owes him money"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Borachio learn of the marriage plan?","o":["From a letter","By hiding behind a tapestry","From Hero","From Leonato"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Don John describe himself?","o":["An honest soldier","A plain-dealing villain","A loyal brother","A lover"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Beatrice jokes that no man is good enough for her. At the masked dance, Don Pedro woos Hero, and Beatrice, pretending not to know Benedick, calls him 'the prince's jester'. Don John tells the masked Claudio that Don Pedro wants Hero for himself, and Claudio believes it. The misunderstanding is cleared, Hero and Claudio are engaged, and Don Pedro plans to make Benedick and Beatrice fall in love.","key":["Masks let characters speak freely and be misled.","Claudio believes the first lie he hears: a warning of what is to come.","Don Pedro becomes the play's matchmaker: 'we are the only love-gods'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Beatrice call Benedick at the dance?","o":["A brave soldier","The prince's jester","Her true love","A villain"],"a":1},{"q":"What lie does Don John tell Claudio?","o":["That Hero is dead","That Don Pedro wants Hero for himself","That Benedick loves Hero","That Leonato refuses"],"a":1},{"q":"What 'labour of Hercules' does Don Pedro plan?","o":["Capturing Don John","Making Benedick and Beatrice fall in love","Building a church","Winning a war"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Borachio offers Don John a way to break the match: he will court Margaret at Hero's bedroom window by night, and Don John will bring Don Pedro and Claudio to watch, so that they think Hero is unfaithful. Don John promises him a thousand ducats.","key":["The villains' plot mirrors the friends' plot: both rely on staged scenes and overhearing.","The trick works on the eyes: what seems true is taken as proof.","Margaret will be used without knowing it."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom will Borachio court at the window?","o":["Hero","Margaret","Beatrice","Ursula"],"a":1},{"q":"How much does Don John promise him?","o":["A hundred pounds","A thousand ducats","A horse","A knighthood"],"a":1},{"q":"When is the scene to be staged?","o":["At the masked ball","The night before the wedding","At the wedding","A month later"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Benedick mocks Claudio for becoming a lover and lists the impossible virtues his own wife would need. Hiding in the arbour, he overhears Don Pedro, Claudio and Leonato say that Beatrice is desperately in love with him but will never admit it. Convinced, he decides to love her back: 'the world must be peopled'. Beatrice calls him in to dinner, and he finds 'double meanings' in her rude words.","key":["The gulling of Benedick: a deliberately staged overhearing.","His reasons for changing his mind are comic and self-justifying.","He reads love into Beatrice's scornful words."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Benedick hide?","o":["Behind a tapestry","In the arbour","Under a table","In a church"],"a":1},{"q":"Who does the gulling party say loves Benedick?","o":["Hero","Margaret","Beatrice","Ursula"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Benedick say to justify marrying?","o":["'I am too old'","'The world must be peopled'","'I need money'","'The prince commands it'"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Hero and Ursula trick Beatrice the same way. While she hides in the bower, they say that Benedick is lovesick for her but that she is too proud and scornful to be told. Alone, Beatrice resolves to give up her contempt and return his love, speaking in verse.","key":["The gulling of Beatrice mirrors the gulling of Benedick.","Hero's criticism of Beatrice's pride is partly true and hits home.","Beatrice's reply is in rhymed verse, a sign of her changed feeling."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Beatrice hide?","o":["In the woodbine bower","Behind a door","In the church","In her bedroom"],"a":0},{"q":"What fault do Hero and Ursula find in Beatrice?","o":["Laziness","Pride and scorn","Greed","Cowardice"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Beatrice react?","o":["She laughs it off","She resolves to love Benedick","She tells Leonato","She leaves Messina"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"The friends tease Benedick, who has shaved and perfumed himself and claims toothache. He takes Leonato aside. Don John tells Don Pedro and Claudio that Hero is disloyal and offers to show them proof that night. Claudio vows that if he sees it he will shame her in church; Don Pedro agrees to join him.","key":["Benedick's outward changes show he is in love.","Don John's accusation comes immediately after a scene of comedy.","Claudio plans public humiliation, not a private talk."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Benedick claim is wrong with him?","o":["A broken heart","Toothache","A fever","A wound"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Don John promise to show them?","o":["A letter","A man entering Hero's window","Hero's ring","A witness"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Claudio vow to do?","o":["Forgive Hero","Shame her in church","Leave quietly","Fight Don John"],"a":1}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Dogberry gives the Watch absurd orders full of mistaken words. Under a porch, Borachio boasts to Conrade that he wooed Margaret as Hero while Don Pedro and Claudio watched, and that Claudio now means to shame Hero. The watchmen overhear and arrest them, convinced that one 'Deformed' is a thief.","key":["The truth is uncovered by the most foolish characters in the play.","Borachio's talk about 'fashion' is about appearances deceiving.","Dramatic irony: the audience now knows Hero is innocent."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Dogberry tell the Watch to do if a man will not stand?","o":["Arrest him","Let him go","Chase him","Call the prince"],"a":1},{"q":"Who overhears Borachio's boast?","o":["Don Pedro","The Watch","Leonato","Benedick"],"a":1},{"q":"Who do the watchmen think 'Deformed' is?","o":["A thief","The prince","A lord","A dog"],"a":0}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"On the wedding morning Hero dresses with Margaret, who jokes bawdily. Beatrice arrives with a cold and is teased about 'Benedictus', the holy thistle, which she suspects is a dig at her love.","key":["Light comedy just before the disaster.","Hero says her heart is 'exceeding heavy': a hint of what is to come.","Beatrice is now on the receiving end of the teasing."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Margaret recommend for Beatrice's illness?","o":["Wine","Carduus Benedictus","Sleep","A song"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Hero feel?","o":["Overjoyed","Her heart is very heavy","Angry","Bored"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Beatrice irritated by the remedy's name?","o":["It is too expensive","It sounds like Benedick","It tastes bad","It is a poison"],"a":1}]},"a3s5":{"sum":"Dogberry and Verges try to tell Leonato about the arrests, but they ramble so much that Leonato, busy with the wedding, tells them to examine the prisoners themselves.","key":["The truth nearly comes out in time.","Comic delay causes serious harm.","Leonato's impatience is a turning point."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why doesn't Leonato listen properly?","o":["He is ill","He is in a hurry for the wedding","He dislikes Dogberry","He is asleep"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Leonato tell them to do?","o":["Release the men","Examine the men themselves","Bring them to church","Arrest Margaret"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Dogberry say comparisons are?","o":["Odious","Odorous","Obvious","Ominous"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"At the altar Claudio rejects Hero as a 'rotten orange' and accuses her of unchastity; Don Pedro and Don John back him. Hero faints and they leave. Leonato believes the accusation and wishes her dead. The Friar, who has watched Hero's face, believes her innocent and advises them to pretend she has died. Left alone, Benedick and Beatrice admit their love. She asks him to 'Kill Claudio'; after hesitating, he agrees to challenge him.","key":["The comedy turns almost tragic: public shame, fainting, a father's curses.","The Friar's plan: let slander turn to pity.","Benedick chooses Beatrice and Hero over his friendship with Claudio."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Claudio call Hero?","o":["A rose","A rotten orange","An angel","A jewel"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the Friar's plan?","o":["Send Hero to a convent at once","Report that Hero has died","Challenge Claudio","Write to Don Pedro"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Beatrice ask Benedick to do?","o":["Marry her at once","Kill Claudio","Leave Messina","Forgive Claudio"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"The Sexton questions Borachio and Conrade, while Dogberry gets everything muddled. The Watch report Borachio's confession. The Sexton reveals that Don John has fled and Hero has 'died', and takes the news to Leonato. Conrade calls Dogberry an ass, and Dogberry is outraged.","key":["The Sexton, not Dogberry, makes sense of the evidence.","Don John's flight confirms his guilt.","Dogberry's 'write me down an ass' is comic and touching."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who actually sorts out the evidence?","o":["Dogberry","The Sexton","Verges","Leonato"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Don John done?","o":["Confessed","Fled secretly","Married","Died"],"a":1},{"q":"What insult upsets Dogberry most?","o":["Fool","Ass","Knave","Villain"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Leonato refuses comfort. He and Antonio confront Don Pedro and Claudio and challenge them; the young men brush them off. Benedick arrives, ignores their jokes and challenges Claudio. The Watch bring in Borachio, who confesses. Claudio is horrified. Leonato tells him to mourn at Hero's tomb tonight and to marry his 'niece' tomorrow.","key":["Old men challenge young ones: grief makes Leonato fierce.","Benedick's challenge shows he has changed sides.","Borachio's confession ends the deception, but Claudio claims he only sinned 'in mistaking'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who challenges Claudio in this scene?","o":["Only Benedick","Leonato, Antonio and Benedick","Don John","The Friar"],"a":1},{"q":"What penance does Leonato set?","o":["Exile","Mourning at the tomb and marrying his 'niece'","A fine","Prison"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is accused of helping the plot unknowingly?","o":["Ursula","Margaret","Beatrice","Hero"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Benedick struggles to write a love poem and admits he cannot rhyme. Beatrice comes and they spar affectionately about which faults made them fall in love. Ursula brings news that Hero has been cleared and Don John exposed.","key":["Love has not stopped the wit, but now it is gentle.","Benedick mocks his own bad poetry.","They agree they are 'too wise to woo peaceably'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What can't Benedick find a rhyme for except 'baby'?","o":["Love","Lady","Hero","Scorn"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Benedick say he will do in Beatrice's heart?","o":["Rule","Live","Hide","Sing"],"a":1},{"q":"What news does Ursula bring?","o":["Hero is cleared and Don John exposed","Claudio has fled","Leonato is ill","The wedding is off"],"a":0}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"At night Claudio and Don Pedro visit Leonato's family tomb. Claudio reads an epitaph declaring Hero was 'done to death by slanderous tongues', a hymn is sung, and he vows to repeat the rite every year. At dawn they leave to prepare for the new wedding.","key":["A solemn ritual of mourning and penance.","The epitaph clears Hero's name in public.","Dawn signals the turn from darkness back to comedy."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does this scene take place?","o":["The orchard","Leonato's family tomb","The prison","A street"],"a":1},{"q":"According to the epitaph, what killed Hero?","o":["Grief","Slanderous tongues","Poison","Illness"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Claudio vow?","o":["To leave Italy","To perform the rite every year","To become a priest","To kill Don John"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Leonato's household prepares the masked brides. Benedick asks to marry Beatrice. Claudio swears to marry the masked 'niece', who unmasks as Hero, alive and cleared. Benedick and Beatrice both deny loving each other until their own love sonnets are produced; Benedick stops her mouth with a kiss. He tells Don Pedro to get a wife. News comes that Don John is captured, and the play ends in a dance.","key":["Hero's 'rebirth' completes the Friar's plan.","Beatrice and Benedick are exposed by their own written words.","The ending is festive but leaves Don Pedro alone."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is the masked 'niece'?","o":["Beatrice","Margaret","Hero","Ursula"],"a":2},{"q":"What proves Beatrice and Benedick love each other?","o":["A ring","Their own love sonnets","Hero's word","A song"],"a":1},{"q":"What news arrives at the very end?","o":["Don John is captured","Don Pedro is married","Claudio is banished","Leonato is dead"],"a":0}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play opens with returning soldiers and news by letter. Messages, reports and overheard words drive the whole plot.","7":"Claudio is presented as young and untested in peace, however brave in war.","13":"Beatrice's first words are about Benedick. She mocks him before anyone else mentions him, which suggests she cares more than she admits.","26":"Leonato's phrase sums up the relationship: real wit, real rivalry, but with affection underneath.","29":"Beatrice accuses Benedick of changing loyalties like fashions. Fashion and changeable appearance become a motif.","49":"The lovers greet each other with insults. Their 'skirmish of wit' is a way of paying attention to each other.","52":"Beatrice rejects romantic wooing. Her independence and wit are unusual for a woman on the Elizabethan stage.","58":"'I know you of old' hints at some earlier relationship between them that went wrong.","73":"Claudio talks about Hero as a precious object. Benedick turns it straight into a joke about buying her.","97":"Benedick's vow is the set-up for his comic downfall. The audience expects him to break it.","102":"Don Pedro's proverb: even the wildest man is tamed by marriage. Benedick will hear it again at the end.","115":"Claudio's first question about his beloved is whether she is the heir. Romance and money are linked.","120":"The first deception in the play is well meant. Disguise and staged wooing will be copied by the villains.","141":"Don John refuses to pretend. Ironically the honest-sounding villain will build his plot entirely on pretence.","143":"Don John is a malcontent: sullen, envious and resentful. His evil has little motive beyond spite.","177":"Beatrice's wit turns logic against marriage. No man fits her requirements.","185":"Beatrice compares marriage to three dances, ending in a stumbling 'repentance'. A cynical view of courtship.","198":"Masks allow freedom and confusion. Nobody can be sure who is speaking.","225":"Beatrice pretends not to recognise Benedick so she can insult him to his face. It hurts him more than he admits.","241":"Claudio instantly believes Don John and turns cynical about love. He trusts what he is told rather than the people he loves.","265":"Benedick's furious complaint shows how much Beatrice's words got to him. Violent images of wit as weapons recur.","284":"Claudio speaks little to Hero. Their love is conducted mostly through other people.","295":"Beatrice's most charming line. It shows the joy behind her sharp wit.","313":"Don Pedro enjoys controlling others' lives. The friends' benign plot runs alongside the villains' malicious one.","324":"Borachio's plot depends on what can be seen at a window in the dark. Appearance will be mistaken for truth.","344":"Benedick thinks he is secretly watching, but he is the one being watched. A comic reversal of eavesdropping.","356":"Balthazar puns on musical notes and 'noting'. Don Pedro answers 'Notes, notes, forsooth, and nothing!': the title's pun in miniature.","361":"The song warns that men are 'deceivers ever'. It foreshadows Claudio's faithlessness.","379":"Fishing and hunting images present the trick as a game, with Benedick as the prey.","421":"Benedick's comic excuse for changing his mind. He talks himself into love.","429":"Benedick now reads love into everything. People see what they want to see, a lesson the play repeats darkly.","462":"Hero's couplet sums up both gulling scenes: love here is caught by a trick.","464":"Beatrice gives up the pride Hero criticised. She changes because she hears how others see her.","520":"Don John offers no evidence except a staged scene. Claudio does not question him.","524":"Claudio plans public humiliation. His concern is for his own honour, not Hero's feelings.","541":"Dogberry's malapropisms (wrong words for right ones) are a joke about language and authority.","585":"Borachio's speech about fashion is really about appearance deceiving people, which is exactly how his trick worked.","586":"The Watch misunderstand Borachio's speech about fashion. They get the details wrong but the truth right.","705":"Claudio now misreads every sign. The same blush the Friar will read as innocence.","730":"Claudio puns on her name, showing he still thinks in terms of appearances and reputation.","743":"Leonato wants his daughter dead rather than dishonoured. A shocking view of family honour.","749":"The Friar 'notes' (observes) what others miss. 'Nothing' sounded like 'noting' in Shakespeare's time: the title's pun.","761":"The Friar's paradox: Hero must seem to die to be reborn with her name restored.","773":"Benedick finally declares his love, in plain prose, at the darkest moment of the play.","786":"Two words that test Benedick's love. Beatrice demands action, not compliments.","796":"Beatrice's fury shows the limits placed on women. She cannot fight for her cousin's honour herself.","802":"Beatrice attacks men who talk and swear but do nothing.","850":"Dogberry's wounded pride is comic, but he is also the man whose Watch saved Hero.","855":"Leonato rejects comfort. Grief makes him more passionate and more human than in the first acts.","941":"Borachio's confession. The princes trusted their eyes, and their eyes were fooled.","956":"Claudio's defence. Was his only fault a mistake, or was his readiness to believe the worst a sin?","1021":"The epitaph names slander as the killer. It publicly restores Hero's reputation.","1066":"Hero's rebirth: her reputation dies and returns. Her speech is brief, and she does not reproach Claudio.","1087":"Benedick ends the argument with a kiss. Some find it romantic; some note that Beatrice is silenced.","1089":"Benedick accepts that people change their minds. Inconsistency is part of being human.","1093":"Benedick's advice to Don Pedro, who remains unmarried at the end, a slightly lonely note in the festivity."},"gloss":{"leagues":"about three miles each","action":"battle","sort":"rank","name":"note, reputation","bettered":"surpassed","badge":"sign","Mountanto":"a showy fencing thrust (a mocking nickname)","bills":"posters; also pikes, long weapons","flight":"long-distance archery","subscribed":"signed up","bird-bolt":"blunt arrow for shooting birds","tax":"criticise","meet":"even, quits; suitable","victual":"food","holp":"helped","trencher-man":"eater","stuffed":"filled (also: a stuffed dummy)","wits":"mental powers","halting":"limping","difference":"heraldic mark of distinction","block":"wooden mould for hats","squarer":"brawler","presently":"at once","ere":"before","Grace":"title for a prince or duke","charge":"burden, expense; orders","fathers":"resembles her father","Disdain":"scorn","convert":"change","turncoat":"traitor","humour":"mood, temperament","predestinate":"fated","jade":"worn-out horse","noted":"observed","sad":"serious","flouting":"mocking","Jack":"rascal","hare-finder":"good at spotting hares","Vulcan":"god of smiths","yoke":"wooden collar for oxen; marriage","constrain":"force","allegiance":"loyalty","heretic":"unbeliever","recheat":"horn call (with a joke about cuckold's horns)","baldrick":"belt for a horn","bottle":"wicker basket","Adam":"Adam Bell, a famous archer","temporize":"soften with time","embassage":"errand, mission","guarded":"trimmed","basted":"loosely stitched","flout":"mock","liege":"lord","affect":"love","salv'd":"explained","treatise":"account, story","pleached":"interwoven","accordant":"agreeing","withal":"with it","measure":"limit; a stately dance","sufferance":"endurance","Saturn":"planet thought to cause gloom","mortifying":"deadly","claw":"flatter","controlment":"restraint","canker":"wild dog-rose","carriage":"manner","muzzle":"device to stop an animal biting","enfranchised":"freed","clog":"heavy block tied to an animal","intelligence":"information","start-up":"upstart","arras":"tapestry wall-hanging","perfumer":"one hired to sweeten rooms with smoke","cross":"thwart","prove":"try, test","tartly":"sourly","melancholy":"gloomy","curst":"bad-tempered","woollen":"rough woollen blankets","bear-ward":"bear-keeper","apes":"monkeys (old maids were said to lead apes in hell)","cuckold":"man whose wife is unfaithful (said to grow horns)","marl":"clay","kinred":"relatives","important":"pressing, insistent","cinque-pace":"a lively five-step dance","ancientry":"old-fashioned dignity","apprehend":"understand","passing":"very","visor":"mask","Philemon":"poor man who welcomed Jove to his cottage","favour":"face; goodwill","clerk":"parish clerk who said Amen","counterfeit":"imitate; pretend","libertines":"loose-living men","villany":"wickedness","partridge wing":"a delicacy","willow":"symbol of a rejected lover","usurer":"moneylender","drovier":"cattle dealer","sedges":"reeds","Lady Fame":"Rumour","lodge in a warren":"lonely gamekeeper's hut","transgression":"wrongdoing","poniards":"daggers","terminations":"words","Ate":"goddess of discord","Antipodes":"opposite side of the world","Prester John":"legendary Christian king in Asia","Cham":"Khan, emperor of the Mongols","harpy":"monster, half woman half bird","civil":"polite (with a pun on Seville oranges)","blazon":"description","conceit":"idea","herald":"messenger","alliance":"marriage, family tie","sunburnt":"unattractive (pale skin was fashionable)","heigh-ho":"sigh","Hercules":"Greek hero who did twelve great labours","watchings":"sleepless nights","strain":"family, descent","practise on":"plot against","queasy":"squeamish","cross it":"block it","medicinable":"healing","covertly":"secretly","temper":"mix","stale":"prostitute","misuse":"deceive","cozened":"cheated","semblance":"appearance","instances":"proofs","ducats":"gold coins","doublet":"close-fitting jacket","orthography":"one who uses fancy words","cheapen":"bargain for","arbour":"shady garden shelter","kid-fox":"young fox","crotchets":"quarter notes; whims","ravished":"carried away","hale":"drag","night-raven":"bird of ill omen","gull":"trick","reverence":"respectability","halfpence":"tiny pieces","daffed":"brushed aside","contemptible":"scornful","proper":"handsome","Hector":"Trojan hero","requited":"returned","censured":"judged","detractions":"faults pointed out","quirks":"jokes","sentences":"wise sayings","career":"course, gallop","daw":"jackdaw (a foolish bird)","pleached bower":"arbour of woven branches","propose":"conversation","lapwing":"bird that runs low along the ground","woodbine":"honeysuckle","coverture":"shelter","haggards":"wild hawks","coy":"shy, disdainful","self-endear'd":"in love with herself","antick":"clown","agate":"tiny carved stone","vane":"weathervane","carping":"fault-finding","haps":"chance","requite":"return","reportingly":"by report","consummate":"completed","vouchsafe":"permit","truant":"idler","slops":"baggy breeches","civet":"perfume from a civet cat","stops":"frets on a lute","hobby-horses":"buffoons","good den":"good evening","impediment":"obstacle","disloyal":"unfaithful","warrant":"proof","untowardly":"unluckily","desartless":"(he means deserving)","senseless":"(he means sensible)","lanthorn":"lantern","comprehend":"(he means apprehend)","vagrom":"vagrant","pitch":"tar","vigitant":"(he means vigilant)","penthouse":"porch roof","drizzles":"rains lightly","reechy":"grimy","Bel":"a Babylonian god","codpiece":"pouch at the front of men's breeches","lechery":"(he means treachery)","rabato":"stiff collar","tire":"headdress","Carduus Benedictus":"holy thistle, a medicinal herb","qualm":"sudden sickness","moral":"hidden meaning","barnes":"children (pun on barns)","Turk":"convert, change one's faith","trow":"I wonder","confidence":"(he means conference)","decerns":"(he means concerns)","odorous":"(he means odious)","palabras":"few words (Spanish)","tedious":"boring (Dogberry takes it as a compliment)","aspicious":"(he means suspicious)","suffigance":"(he means sufficient)","excommunication":"(he means communication)","non-come":"(he means non plus, at a loss)","gaol":"jail","counterpoise":"balance","luxurious":"lustful","extenuate":"excuse","Dian":"Diana, goddess of chastity","Venus":"goddess of love","catechizing":"questioning","liberal":"crude, unrestrained","misgovernment":"misconduct","conjecture":"suspicion","smirched":"stained","mir'd":"muddied","attir'd":"dressed","misprision":"mistake","throughly":"thoroughly","ostentation":"show","epitaphs":"inscriptions for the dead","travail":"labour","liver":"seat of passion","levell'd":"aimed","reclusive":"secluded","inwardness":"close friendship","twine":"thread","Comfect":"sweet, sugar-plum","cursies":"curtsies, bows","dissembly":"(he means assembly)","exhibition":"(he means commission)","malefactors":"wrongdoers","eftest":"(he means deftest, quickest)","perjury":"(he means slander)","burglary":"(he means perjury)","redemption":"(he means damnation)","opinioned":"(he means pinioned, tied)","coxcomb":"fool","varlet":"rogue","suspect":"(he means respect)","piety":"(he means impiety)","sieve":"strainer","candle-wasters":"scholars who study late","preceptial":"made of wise sayings","advertisement":"advice","dissembler":"liar","beshrew":"curse","fleer":"sneer","dotard":"senile old man","fence":"fencing skill","lustihood":"vigour","daff":"brush aside","foining":"thrusting","milksops":"weaklings","scruple":"tiny weight","scambling":"brawling","fashion-monging":"fashion-mad","cog":"cheat","antickly":"like clowns","high-proof":"extremely","mettle":"spirit","girdle":"belt","calf's-head":"fool","capon":"castrated cockerel; fool","woodcock":"stupid bird; fool","Lack-beard":"beardless boy","plaintiffs":"(he means defendants)","reformed":"(he means informed)","foundation":"charitable institution","arrant":"complete","bucklers":"small shields","pikes":"spikes in a shield","Leander":"lover who swam the Hellespont","Troilus":"Trojan lover","panders":"go-betweens","carpet-mongers":"ladies' men","politic":"well-organised","epithet":"descriptive phrase","rheum":"tears","coil":"fuss, commotion","guerdon":"reward","Phoebus":"the sun god","weeds":"clothes","Hymen":"god of marriage","enigmatical":"puzzling","conjoin'd":"joined","Ethiope":"Ethiopian","Europa":"princess carried off by Jove as a bull","qualify":"explain, moderate","consumption":"wasting disease","witcrackers":"jokers","epigram":"short witty poem","cudgelled":"beaten","double-dealer":"unfaithful husband","staff":"walking stick","tipped with horn":"(a cuckold joke)"}};
const PARTS={"117":[[0,4,"Oh, my lord, when you set off on this war that's now over, I looked at her with a soldier's eye. I liked her, but I had a tougher job in hand than to turn liking into love."],[5,9,"But now I'm back, and thoughts of war have left their places empty, and soft, delicate desires come crowding in, all telling me how lovely young Hero is and saying I liked her before I went to war."]],"120":[[0,3,"Why build the bridge any wider than the river? The best gift is what's needed. Look, whatever works is right. In short, you love her, and I'll give you the cure."],[4,12,"I know there'll be a party tonight. I'll take your place in a disguise and tell lovely Hero I am Claudio. I'll open my heart to her and take her ears prisoner with the force and power of my loving words. Then I'll speak to her father, and the result will be that she's yours. Let's put it into action right away."]],"143":[[0,4,"I'd rather be a wild rose in a hedge than a cultivated rose in his favour. It suits my nature better to be despised by everyone than to put on an act to steal anyone's love. In this, though no one can call me a flattering honest man, no one can deny I'm a plain, honest villain."],[5,8,"I'm trusted only with a muzzle on and freed only with a weight on my leg, so I've decided not to sing in my cage. If I had my mouth free, I would bite; if I had my freedom, I'd do as I like. Meanwhile, let me be what I am, and don't try to change me."]],"185":[[0,2,"It'll be the music's fault, cousin, if you're not wooed in good time. If the prince is too pushy, tell him there's a proper rhythm to everything, and dance your answer out."],[3,8,"Because, listen, Hero: wooing, wedding and regretting are like a Scottish jig, a stately dance and a five-step galliard. The proposal is hot and hasty, like a Scottish jig, and just as wild. The wedding is polite and modest, like a stately dance, all ceremony and tradition. And then along comes Regret, and on his bad legs he stumbles into the galliard, faster and faster, until he sinks into his grave."]],"241":[[0,2,"I answered like that pretending to be Benedick, but I hear this bad news with Claudio's ears. It's definitely true: the prince is wooing for himself."],[3,10,"Friendship is loyal in everything except the business of love. So all lovers should use their own tongues. Let every eye negotiate for itself and trust no go-between; for beauty is a witch whose spells melt loyalty into lust. This happens every hour of the day, and I never suspected it. So goodbye, Hero!"]],"265":[[0,6,"Oh, she abused me more than a block of wood could stand! An oak tree with only one green leaf left would have answered her back. Even my mask began to come alive and scold her. Not realising it was me, she told me I was the prince's jester and duller than a muddy thaw, piling joke on joke on me so fast and so unbelievably that I stood like a man at a target with a whole army shooting at me."],[7,12,"She speaks daggers, and every word stabs. If her breath were as terrible as her words, nobody could live near her; she'd poison everything as far as the North Star. I wouldn't marry her even if she came with everything Adam owned before he sinned. She'd have made Hercules turn the roasting spit in her kitchen, yes, and split his club for firewood too."],[13,17,"Come on, don't talk about her. You'll find she's Ate, the goddess of discord, in fine clothes. I wish to God some scholar would exorcise her; for while she's here, a man could live as peacefully in hell as in a church, and people sin on purpose so as to go there. So really, all trouble, horror and chaos follow her."]],"313":[[0,2,"And Benedick is not the most hopeless husband I know. I can praise him this far: he's of noble family, of proven courage and of tested honesty."],[3,8,"I'll teach you how to handle your cousin so that she falls in love with Benedick; and I, with the help of you two, will work on Benedick so that, despite his quick wit and his weak stomach for marriage, he'll fall in love with Beatrice. If we can do this, Cupid is no longer the archer. His glory will be ours, for we'll be the only gods of love. Come in with me, and I'll tell you my plan."]],"330":[[0,5,"Go then. Find me a suitable time to get Don Pedro and Count Claudio alone. Tell them you know that Hero loves me. Pretend to be full of concern for both the prince and Claudio, as if, out of love for your brother's honour, since he made this match, and for his friend's reputation, since he's about to be cheated with something that only looks like a virgin, you've found this out."],[6,13,"They'll hardly believe it without proof. Offer them evidence, no less convincing than seeing me at her bedroom window, hearing me call Margaret 'Hero' and hearing Margaret call me 'Claudio'. Bring them to see this on the very night before the wedding. In the meantime I'll arrange things so that Hero is away, and there'll be such convincing evidence of Hero's unfaithfulness that suspicion will be taken for certainty, and all the wedding plans overturned."]],"344":[[0,3,"It amazes me that one man, seeing what a fool another man is when he devotes himself to love, will laugh at such silly behaviour in others and then become the object of his own scorn by falling in love. And Claudio is just such a man."],[4,11,"I've known a time when the only music he liked was the military drum and fife; now he'd rather hear the little drum and pipe of a village dance. I've known a time when he'd have walked ten miles to see a good suit of armour; now he'll lie awake ten nights designing the style of a new jacket. He used to speak plainly and to the point, like an honest man and a soldier; now he's turned into a dictionary. His words are a very fancy banquet, so many exotic dishes. Could I ever be changed like that and see with these same eyes?"],[12,17,"I can't say; I don't think so. I won't swear that love couldn't turn me into an oyster; but I'll swear this: until he's made an oyster of me, he'll never make such a fool of me. One woman is beautiful, yet I'm fine; another is clever, yet I'm fine; another is good, yet I'm fine. But until all the virtues are in one woman, no woman will win my favour."],[18,22,"She'll be rich, that's certain; clever, or I won't have her; good, or I won't even bargain for her; beautiful, or I won't look at her; gentle, or she'd better not come near me; noble, or I wouldn't take her for a gold coin; a good talker, an excellent musician, and her hair can be whatever colour God likes. Ha! The prince and Monsieur Love! I'll hide in the arbour."]],"361":[[0,3,"Sigh no more, ladies, sigh no more; men were always deceivers. With one foot in the sea and one on the shore, they're never faithful to one thing."],[4,8,"So don't sigh like that, but let them go, and be happy and pretty, turning all your sounds of sadness into 'Hey nonny, nonny."],[9,12,"Sing no more songs, sing no more, of gloomy, heavy moods. Men have always cheated like this, ever since the first summer had leaves."],[13,17,"So don't sigh like that, but let them go, and be happy and pretty, turning all your sounds of sadness into 'Hey nonny, nonny.'"]],"421":[[0,3,"This can't be a trick. They talked seriously. They have the truth of it from Hero. They seem to pity the lady. It seems her love is stretched to the limit. Love me! Why, it must be returned."],[4,8,"I hear how I'm criticised. They say I'll be proud if I realise the love comes from her. They also say she would rather die than show any sign of love. I never thought I'd marry. I mustn't seem proud. Happy are those who hear their faults and can correct them."],[9,16,"They say the lady is beautiful; that's true, I can vouch for it. And good; that's so, I can't deny it. And clever, except for loving me. Honestly, that doesn't add to her cleverness, but it's no great proof of her foolishness either, because I'm going to be terribly in love with her. Some odd jokes and scraps of wit may be cracked at my expense, because I've attacked marriage for so long. But don't tastes change? A man loves food in his youth that he can't stand in old age."],[17,21,"Shall clever remarks and sayings and these paper bullets of the brain scare a man off following his desires? No, the world must be populated. When I said I'd die a bachelor, I didn't think I'd live until I got married. Here comes Beatrice. By heaven! She's a beautiful lady. I do see some signs of love in her."]],"433":[[0,2,"Good Margaret, run to the drawing room. You'll find my cousin Beatrice there talking with the prince and Claudio."],[3,12,"Whisper in her ear and tell her that Ursula and I are walking in the orchard and that all our talk is about her. Say you overheard us, and tell her to slip into the arbour of woven branches, where honeysuckle, ripened by the sun, won't let the sun come in, like royal favourites who are made proud by princes and then turn their pride against the very power that raised them. She'll hide there to listen to our conversation. This is your job; do it well and leave us alone."]],"436":[[0,4,"Now, Ursula, when Beatrice comes, as we walk up and down this path, we must talk only about Benedick. Whenever I mention him, your job is to praise him more than any man ever deserved."],[5,8,"What I'll tell you is how Benedick is lovesick for Beatrice. That's what little Cupid's crafty arrow is made of, the kind that wounds just by hearsay."]],"448":[[0,3,"Oh god of love! I know he deserves as much as any man could be given. But nature never made a woman's heart out of prouder stuff than Beatrice's."],[4,9,"Disdain and scorn sparkle in her eyes, despising everything they look at, and she values her own wit so highly that everything else seems feeble to her. She cannot love, or even imagine what love looks like; she's so in love with herself."]],"450":[[0,8,"Why, you're right. I've never seen a man, however clever, noble, young or handsome, whom she wouldn't turn inside out. If he was fair-skinned, she'd swear the gentleman should be her sister. If he was dark, why, Nature was drawing a clown and made an ugly blot. If he was tall, he was a badly topped lance; if short, a tiny carved stone, very badly cut. If he talked, a weathervane blown by every wind; if he was quiet, a block of wood that nothing moves."],[9,11,"That's how she turns every man inside out, and never gives truth and virtue the credit that honesty and merit deserve."]],"452":[[0,4,"No, being so odd and against all normal ways, as Beatrice is, can't be admirable. But who would dare tell her so? If I spoke up, she'd mock me into thin air. Oh, she'd laugh me out of myself and crush me to death with her wit."],[5,8,"So let Benedick, like a smothered fire, burn away in sighs and waste away inside. That would be a better death than dying of mockery, which is as bad as being tickled to death."]],"464":[[0,3,"What burning is this in my ears? Can this be true? Am I so condemned for pride and scorn? Contempt, goodbye! And girlish pride, farewell! No one speaks well of such things behind your back."],[4,9,"And, Benedick, keep loving; I'll return your love, taming my wild heart to your loving hand. If you love me, my kindness will encourage you to tie our love together with the holy bond of marriage. For other people say you deserve it, and I believe it better than just from what they say."]],"645":[[0,5,"Hidden meaning! No, honestly, I have no hidden meaning. I meant plain holy thistle. You may perhaps think that I think you're in love. No, by Our Lady, I'm not such a fool as to think whatever I like; and I don't like to think whatever I can; and in fact I couldn't think, even if I thought my heart out, that you're in love, or that you will be in love, or that you can be in love."],[6,9,"Still, Benedick was the same, and now he has become a man like any other. He swore he'd never marry, and yet now, in spite of himself, he eats his food without complaining. How you might be converted, I don't know; but it seems to me you look with your eyes the way other women do."]],"705":[[0,6,"Sweet prince, you teach me noble gratitude. There, Leonato, take her back again. Don't give this rotten orange to your friend; she only looks honourable on the outside. Look how she blushes here like a virgin! Oh, how clever sin can disguise itself with such an air of authority and truth!"],[7,12,"Doesn't that blush come as modest evidence of simple innocence? Wouldn't all of you who see her swear she was a virgin, judging by these outward signs? But she is not. She knows the heat of a lustful bed. Her blush is guilt, not modesty."]],"730":[[0,4,"Oh Hero! What a Hero you would have been, if half your outward beauty had been given to your thoughts and the plans of your heart! But goodbye, most foul and most beautiful! Goodbye, you pure wickedness and wicked purity!"],[5,8,"Because of you I'll lock up all the gates of love, and suspicion will hang on my eyelids, turning all beauty into thoughts of evil, and it will never seem lovely again."]],"743":[[0,7,"Why! Doesn't everything on earth cry shame on her? Could she deny the story that's written in her blushes? Don't live, Hero; don't open your eyes. For if I thought you wouldn't die quickly, if I thought your spirit was stronger than your shame, I'd strike at your life myself, after all these reproaches. Did I grieve that I had only one child?"],[8,15,"Did I blame nature for being so mean? Oh, you were one too many! Why did I have even one? Why were you ever lovely in my eyes? Why didn't I take in some beggar's child at my gates out of charity? Then, if she were stained like this and smeared with disgrace, I could have said, 'None of it is mine; this shame comes from unknown parents."],[16,23,"But mine, and mine I loved, and mine I praised, and mine I was proud of, mine so much that I valued her more than myself: why, she, oh, she has fallen into a pit of ink, so deep that the wide sea hasn't enough drops to wash her clean again, nor enough salt to preserve her rotten flesh."]],"749":[[0,6,"Listen to me for a moment. I've only kept quiet so long and let things take their course because I've been watching the lady. I've seen a thousand blushes leap into her face, and a thousand innocent signs of shame drive those blushes away in angelic whiteness."],[7,9,"And a fire has appeared in her eye to burn up the mistakes these princes hold against her innocence. Call me a fool."],[10,15,"Don't trust my learning or my observations, which experience has confirmed as true. Don't trust my age, my respect, my calling or my holy office, if this sweet lady isn't lying here innocent of some cruel error."]],"755":[[0,2,"I don't know. If they're telling the truth about her, these hands will tear her apart. If they're wronging her honour, the proudest of them will hear from me."],[3,10,"Time hasn't yet dried my blood so much, nor age eaten up my wits, nor bad luck so ruined my wealth, nor a bad life robbed me of so many friends, that they won't find, when I'm roused in this way, strength in my arms and cunning in my mind, enough wealth and well-chosen friends to pay them back in full."]],"758":[[0,3,"Well, if this is handled well, it will turn slander into regret on her behalf; that's something good. But that isn't why I dream up this strange plan; I hope this labour will give birth to something greater."],[4,23,"If it is announced that she died the moment she was accused, every listener will mourn, pity and excuse her. For it happens that we don't value what we have properly while we enjoy it, but once it's missing and lost, why, then we stretch its value to the limit; then we find the virtue that owning it wouldn't show us while it was ours. That's how it will be with Claudio. When he hears she died because of his words, the memory of her life will creep sweetly into his imagination, and every lovely part of her will come back dressed in finer clothes, more moving, more delicate and more full of life in his mind's eye than when she was really alive. Then he'll mourn, if love ever touched his heart, and wish he hadn't accused her, even if he still thought his accusation was true."],[24,26,"Let this be done, and have no doubt that the outcome will shape things better than I can predict."],[27,33,"And even if everything else misses its aim, the belief that the lady is dead will put an end to talk of her disgrace. And if things don't turn out well, you can hide her away, as best suits her damaged reputation, in some secluded religious life as a nun, far from all eyes, tongues, minds and harm."]],"850":[[0,4,"Don't you suspect (he means respect) my position? Don't you suspect my age? Oh, if only he were here to write me down as an ass! But, gentlemen, remember that I am an ass. Though it isn't written down, don't forget that I am an ass. No, you villain, you're full of piety (he means impiety), as good witnesses will prove against you."],[5,10,"I'm a wise fellow; and what's more, an officer; and what's more, a householder; and what's more, as fine a piece of flesh as any in Messina; and one who knows the law, so there; and quite a rich fellow, so there; and a fellow who has had losses; and one who has two gowns, and everything handsome about him. Bring him away. Oh, if only I'd been written down as an ass!"]],"855":[[0,16,"Please stop your advice, which falls into my ears as uselessly as water into a sieve. Don't give me advice, and don't let any comforter please my ear except one whose wrongs match mine. Bring me a father who loved his child as much, whose joy in her has been crushed like mine, and tell him to speak to me of patience. Measure his sorrow against the length and breadth of mine, and let it match it pang for pang, this for this and grief for grief, in every feature, branch, shape and form. If a man like that can smile and stroke his beard, tell sorrow to be off, say 'ahem' when he ought to groan, patch up his grief with proverbs and drown his misfortune with late-night philosophers, then bring him to me, and I'll learn patience from him."],[17,23,"But there is no such man. For, brother, men can advise and comfort a grief they don't feel themselves; but once they taste it, their advice turns into the same passion that they once wanted to cure with wise sayings, as if they could chain raging madness with a silk thread, charm away pain with breath and agony with words."],[24,29,"No, no. It's everyone's job to preach patience to those who writhe under the weight of sorrow, but no one has the strength or the ability to be so moral when he has to endure the same himself. So give me no advice. My griefs cry louder than your warnings."]],"872":[[0,8,"Come, come, man! Don't sneer and joke at me. I'm not speaking like a senile old man or a fool, who uses his age as an excuse to boast about what he did when he was young, or what he'd do if he weren't old. Know this, Claudio, to your face: you have so wronged my innocent child and me that I'm forced to put aside my dignity and, with my grey hair and the bruises of many years, challenge you to a duel, man to man."],[9,13,"I say you have lied about my innocent child. Your slander has gone right through her heart, and she lies buried with her ancestors; oh, in a tomb where scandal never lay before, except this one about her, invented by your villainy!"]],"917":[[0,6,"I'll tell you how Beatrice praised your wit the other day. I said you had a fine wit. 'True,' she said, 'a fine little one. 'No,' I said, 'a great wit. 'Right,' she said, 'a great fat one. 'No,' I said, 'a good wit. 'Exactly,' she said, 'it doesn't hurt anybody. 'No,' I said, 'the gentleman is wise."],[7,14,"'Certainly,' she said, 'a wise gentleman. 'No,' I said, 'he speaks several languages. 'That I believe,' she said, 'for he swore something to me on Monday night and took it back on Tuesday morning: there's a double tongue, there's two tongues. Like that, for a whole hour, she twisted every one of your virtues out of shape; and yet at last she concluded, with a sigh, that you were the most handsome man in Italy."]],"941":[[0,6,"Sweet prince, let me go no further before answering. Listen to me, and let this count kill me. I have deceived your very eyes. What your wisdom couldn't discover, these simple fools have brought to light. They overheard me in the night confessing to this man how Don John your brother urged me on to slander the Lady Hero; how you were brought into the orchard and saw me court Margaret dressed in Hero's clothes; how you disgraced her when you should have married her."],[7,10,"They have recorded my villainy, which I'd rather seal with my death than repeat to my shame. The lady is dead because of my false accusation and my master's; and, in short, I want nothing but a villain's reward."]],"958":[[0,6,"I can't order you to tell my daughter to live; that would be impossible. But I beg you both, let the people here in Messina know how innocent she was when she died. And if your love can produce anything in a sad poem, hang an epitaph on her tomb and sing it to her bones; sing it tonight."],[7,13,"Tomorrow morning come to my house, and since you couldn't be my son-in-law, be my nephew instead. My brother has a daughter who is almost a copy of my child who's dead, and she alone is heir to both of us. Give her the rights you should have given her cousin, and so my revenge dies."]],"991":[[0,5,"'The god of love, who sits above, and knows me, and knows me, how pitiful I deserve... I mean at singing. But at loving: Leander the good swimmer, Troilus the first man to use a go-between, and a whole book full of these old-time ladies' men, whose names still run smoothly along the even road of blank verse, why, they were never so truly turned head over heels as my poor self in love."],[6,10,"But I can't show it in rhyme. I've tried. I can't find a rhyme for 'lady' except 'baby', a childish rhyme; for 'scorn', 'horn', a harsh rhyme; for 'school', 'fool', a silly rhyme. Very unlucky endings. No, I wasn't born under a rhyming planet, and I can't woo in fancy holiday language."]],"1021":[[0,4,"'Done to death by slandering tongues was the Hero who lies here. Death, as a reward for her wrongs, gives her fame that never dies."],[5,9,"So the life that died in shame lives on in death with glorious fame. Hang there on the tomb, praising her when I am silent. Now, music, play and sing your solemn hymn."]],"1022":[[0,3,"Forgive, goddess of the night, those who killed your virgin follower; for which, with songs of sorrow, they walk around her tomb."],[4,9,"Midnight, help our mourning; help us to sigh and groan, heavily, heavily. Graves, open and give up your dead, until death is fully spoken of, heavily, heavily."]],"1089":[[0,3,"I'll tell you what, prince: a whole university of wisecrackers couldn't mock me out of my good mood. Do you think I care about a satire or a witty poem? No. If a man is going to be beaten down by clever remarks, he won't dare wear anything fine about him."],[4,9,"In short, since I intend to marry, I'll take no notice of anything the world can say against it. So never mock me for what I've said against marriage, because man is a fickle creature, and that's my conclusion. As for you, Claudio, I was planning to beat you; but since you're going to be my relative, live unbruised, and love my cousin."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"love":["Love and courtship","Two very different couples: Claudio and Hero's quick, arranged romance and Beatrice and Benedick's slow, witty one."],"deception":["Deception and tricks","Good tricks and bad ones: the friends' matchmaking and Don John's slander both work by staged scenes."],"noting":["Noting and seeing","'Nothing' was pronounced 'noting': overhearing, watching and misreading what you see."],"honour":["Honour and reputation","A woman's chastity and a man's good name, and how quickly gossip can destroy both."],"wit":["Wit and language","Insults, puns and the 'merry war' of words; also Dogberry's mangled words."],"gender":["Men and women","Male friendship, female obedience, and Beatrice's protest against what women are allowed to do."],"marriage":["Marriage","Mockery of marriage, fear of cuckoldry, and the festive weddings that end the play."],"appearance":["Appearance and reality","Masks, fashion, blushes and disguises: what looks true often is not."],"change":["Change and constancy","'Men were deceivers ever': who stays true, and who changes their mind?"]},"quotes":[{"id":"26","who":"LEONATO","text":"You must not, sir, mistake my niece. There is a kind of merry war betwixt Signior Benedick and her; they never meet but there's a","themes":["wit","love"],"note":"Leonato sums up the lovers' battle of wits."},{"id":"48","who":"BEATRICE","text":"I wonder that you will still be talking, Signior Benedick: nobody marks you.","themes":["wit","noting"],"note":"Beatrice's opening attack: she notices him even as she says no one does."},{"id":"49","who":"BENEDICK","text":"What! my dear Lady Disdain, are you yet living?","themes":["wit","love"],"note":"Benedick's first words to Beatrice."},{"id":"52","who":"BEATRICE","text":"for that. I had rather hear my dog bark at a crow than a man swear he loves me.","themes":["love","gender"],"note":"Beatrice scorns romantic vows."},{"id":"29","who":"BEATRICE","text":"Very easily possible: he wears his faith but as the fashion of his hat; it ever changes with the next block.","themes":["appearance","change"],"note":"Beatrice on Benedick's changing loyalties."},{"id":"58","who":"BEATRICE","text":"You always end with a jade's trick: I know you of old.","themes":["love","wit"],"note":"A hint of past history between them."},{"id":"73","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Can the world buy such a jewel?","themes":["love","marriage"],"note":"Claudio sees Hero as a precious possession."},{"id":"75","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"In mine eye she is the sweetest lady that ever I looked on.","themes":["love","appearance","noting"],"note":"Claudio's love is based on looks."},{"id":"115","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Hath Leonato any son, my lord?","themes":["marriage","honour"],"note":"Claudio checks whether Hero is the heir."},{"id":"97","who":"BENEDICK","text":"is,—for the which I may go the finer,—I will live a bachelor.","themes":["marriage","change"],"note":"Benedick's vow, which he will break."},{"id":"102","who":"DON PEDRO","text":"Well, as time shall try: 'In time the savage bull doth bear the yoke.'","themes":["marriage","change"],"note":"Don Pedro predicts that even Benedick will be tamed."},{"id":"120","who":"DON PEDRO","text":"I will assume thy part in some disguise,","themes":["deception","appearance"],"note":"Don Pedro's well-meant plan to woo for Claudio."},{"id":"141","who":"DON JOHN","text":"mischief. I cannot hide what I am: I must be sad when I have cause,","themes":["deception","appearance"],"note":"Don John claims to be honest about his bad nature."},{"id":"143","who":"DON JOHN","text":"flattering honest man, it must not be denied but I am a plain-dealing villain.","themes":["deception"],"note":"Don John defines himself."},{"id":"145","who":"DON JOHN","text":"I make all use of it, for I use it only. Who comes here?","themes":["deception"],"note":"Don John feeds on his own discontent."},{"id":"177","who":"BEATRICE","text":"waiting-gentlewoman? He that hath a beard is more than a youth, and he","themes":["gender","marriage","wit"],"note":"Beatrice's witty proof that no man will suit her."},{"id":"183","who":"BEATRICE","text":"Not till God make men of some other metal than earth. Would it not grieve a woman to be over-mastered with a piece of valiant dust? to","themes":["gender","marriage"],"note":"Beatrice refuses to be ruled by a man made of earth."},{"id":"185","who":"BEATRICE","text":"For, hear me, Hero: wooing, wedding, and repenting is as a Scotch jig, a measure, and a cinque-pace: the first suit is hot and hasty, like a","themes":["marriage","wit"],"note":"Beatrice's cynical dance of marriage."},{"id":"198","who":"DON PEDRO","text":"Speak low, if you speak love.","themes":["love","deception","appearance"],"note":"Masked wooing at the dance."},{"id":"225","who":"BEATRICE","text":"Why, he is the prince's jester: a very dull fool; only his gift is in","themes":["wit","deception"],"note":"Beatrice insults Benedick to his masked face."},{"id":"241","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Let every eye negotiate for itself And trust no agent; for beauty is a witch","themes":["love","deception"],"note":"Claudio's bitter lesson, which he soon forgets."},{"id":"241","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"And trust no agent; for beauty is a witch Against whose charms faith melteth into blood.","themes":["love","change"],"note":"Claudio believes love destroys loyalty."},{"id":"265","who":"BENEDICK","text":"She speaks poniards, and every word stabs: if her breath were as","themes":["wit","gender"],"note":"Benedick wounded by Beatrice's words."},{"id":"284","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Silence is the perfectest herald of joy: I were but little happy, if I","themes":["love"],"note":"Claudio says little to Hero."},{"id":"295","who":"BEATRICE","text":"No, sure, my lord, my mother cried; but then there was a star danced, and under that was I born. Cousins, God give you joy!","themes":["wit","love"],"note":"Beatrice's joyful spirit."},{"id":"313","who":"DON PEDRO","text":"longer an archer: his glory shall be ours, for we are the only love-gods. Go in with me, and I will tell you my drift.","themes":["love","deception"],"note":"Don Pedro's matchmaking plot."},{"id":"324","who":"BORACHIO","text":"I can, at any unseasonable instant of the night, appoint her to look out at her lady's chamber window.","themes":["deception","noting"],"note":"Borachio plans the window trick."},{"id":"344","who":"BENEDICK","text":"am well; but till all graces be in one woman, one woman shall not come in my grace.","themes":["love","marriage","gender"],"note":"Benedick's impossible standards."},{"id":"361","who":"BALTHAZAR","text":"Sigh no more, ladies, sigh no more,","themes":["change","love","gender"],"note":"The song's warning about faithless men."},{"id":"361","who":"BALTHAZAR","text":"Men were deceivers ever;","themes":["change","deception","gender"],"note":"Balthazar's song foreshadows Claudio's betrayal."},{"id":"379","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Bait the hook well: this fish will bite.","themes":["deception"],"note":"Claudio on the gulling of Benedick."},{"id":"421","who":"BENEDICK","text":"man from the career of his humour? No; the world must be peopled. When","themes":["marriage","change"],"note":"Benedick's comic excuse for falling in love."},{"id":"421","who":"BENEDICK","text":"man from the career of his humour? No; the world must be peopled. When I said I would die a bachelor, I did not think I should live till I were married. Here comes Beatrice. By this day! she's a fair lady: I","themes":["change","marriage","wit"],"note":"Benedick justifies his change of heart."},{"id":"429","who":"BENEDICK","text":"Ha! 'Against my will I am sent to bid you come in to dinner,' there's a double meaning in that. 'I took no more pains for those thanks than","themes":["noting","love"],"note":"Benedick reads love into Beatrice's insults."},{"id":"462","who":"HERO","text":"Some Cupid kills with arrows, some with traps.","themes":["love","deception"],"note":"Hero on the trick played on Beatrice."},{"id":"448","who":"HERO","text":"Disdain and scorn ride sparkling in her eyes,","themes":["gender","wit"],"note":"Hero's portrait of Beatrice's pride."},{"id":"464","who":"BEATRICE","text":"Contempt, farewell! and maiden pride, adieu!","themes":["love","change"],"note":"Beatrice decides to love Benedick."},{"id":"516","who":"DON JOHN","text":"has been too long a talking of,—the lady is disloyal.","themes":["honour","deception"],"note":"Don John's slander."},{"id":"518","who":"DON JOHN","text":"Even she: Leonato's Hero, your Hero, every man's Hero.","themes":["honour","deception"],"note":"Don John's cruel pun."},{"id":"524","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"the congregation, where I should wed, there will I shame her.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Claudio plans public humiliation."},{"id":"585","who":"BORACHIO","text":"Tush! I may as well say the fool's the fool. But seest thou not what a deformed thief this fashion is?","themes":["appearance"],"note":"Borachio on how appearances mislead."},{"id":"662","who":"DOGBERRY","text":"Comparisons are odorous: palabras, neighbour Verges.","themes":["wit"],"note":"Dogberry's malapropism."},{"id":"705","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Give not this rotten orange to your friend;","themes":["honour","appearance"],"note":"Claudio rejects Hero at the altar."},{"id":"705","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Her blush is guiltiness, not modesty.","themes":["honour","appearance","noting"],"note":"Claudio misreads Hero's blush."},{"id":"730","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"O Hero! what a Hero hadst thou been,","themes":["honour","appearance"],"note":"Claudio's grief and cruelty."},{"id":"743","who":"LEONATO","text":"Do not live, Hero; do not ope thine eyes;","themes":["honour","gender"],"note":"Leonato would rather his daughter died than lived dishonoured."},{"id":"749","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","text":"By noting of the lady: I have mark'd","themes":["noting"],"note":"The Friar sees Hero's innocence by watching her."},{"id":"761","who":"FRIAR FRANCIS","text":"Come, lady, die to live: this wedding day","themes":["deception","honour"],"note":"The Friar's paradoxical plan."},{"id":"773","who":"BENEDICK","text":"I do love nothing in the world so well as you: is not that strange?","themes":["love","noting"],"note":"Benedick's declaration."},{"id":"784","who":"BEATRICE","text":"I love you with so much of my heart that none is left to protest.","themes":["love"],"note":"Beatrice's declaration."},{"id":"786","who":"BEATRICE","text":"Kill Claudio.","themes":["love","honour","gender"],"note":"Beatrice's test of Benedick."},{"id":"796","who":"BEATRICE","text":"accusation, uncovered slander, unmitigated rancour,—O God, that I were a man! I would eat his heart in the market-place.","themes":["gender","honour"],"note":"Beatrice's rage at her powerlessness."},{"id":"802","who":"BEATRICE","text":"But manhood is melted into cursies, valour into compliment, and men","themes":["gender"],"note":"Beatrice despises men who only talk."},{"id":"850","who":"DOGBERRY","text":"two gowns, and everything handsome about him. Bring him away. O that I had been writ down an ass!","themes":["wit"],"note":"Dogberry's wounded dignity."},{"id":"857","who":"LEONATO","text":"For there was never yet philosopher That could endure the toothache patiently,","themes":["change"],"note":"Leonato on the uselessness of advice in grief."},{"id":"941","who":"BORACHIO","text":"let this count kill me. I have deceived even your very eyes: what your","themes":["deception","noting"],"note":"Borachio's confession."},{"id":"956","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Can lay upon my sin: yet sinn'd I not But in mistaking.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Claudio's defence of himself."},{"id":"1003","who":"BENEDICK","text":"Thou and I are too wise to woo peaceably.","themes":["love","wit"],"note":"Benedick on their kind of love."},{"id":"1015","who":"BENEDICK","text":"I will live in thy heart, die in thy lap, and be buried in thy eyes;","themes":["love"],"note":"Benedick's playful devotion."},{"id":"1021","who":"CLAUDIO","text":"Done to death by slanderous tongues","themes":["honour"],"note":"Hero's epitaph."},{"id":"1066","who":"HERO","text":"One Hero died defil'd, but I do live,","themes":["honour","change"],"note":"Hero's 'rebirth'."},{"id":"1087","who":"BENEDICK","text":"Peace! I will stop your mouth.","themes":["love","gender"],"note":"Benedick's kiss ends the argument."},{"id":"1089","who":"BENEDICK","text":"at me for what I have said against it, for man is a giddy thing, and this is my conclusion. For thy part, Claudio, I did think to have","themes":["change","marriage"],"note":"Benedick accepts that people change."},{"id":"1093","who":"BENEDICK","text":"First, of my word; therefore play, music! Prince, thou art sad; get thee a wife, get thee a wife: there is no staff more reverent than one","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Benedick's advice to the lonely Don Pedro."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Messina, Sicily. Don Pedro, Prince of Aragon, returns from a short war with his friends Claudio and Benedick, and stays with Leonato, the governor.","Young Claudio falls for Leonato's daughter Hero. Leonato's niece Beatrice and Benedick only trade insults, and both swear they will never marry.","Don Pedro's sulky half-brother, Don John, is with them too. Watch how much of the plot depends on overhearing, spying and 'noting'."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The 'merry war': Beatrice and Benedick clash","id":49},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Claudio loves Hero; Don Pedro offers to woo her for him","id":120},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Don John learns of the match and vows to spoil it","id":157},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The masked dance: Beatrice mocks Benedick, Claudio is fooled","id":225},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Hero and Claudio engaged; Don Pedro plans to matchmake","id":313},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Borachio's plan: woo Margaret at Hero's window","id":324},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Benedick is tricked into loving Beatrice","id":379},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Beatrice is tricked into loving Benedick","id":464},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Don John accuses Hero","id":516},{"s":"a3s3","t":"The Watch overhear Borachio and arrest him","id":585},{"s":"a3s5","t":"Dogberry fails to warn Leonato","id":662},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Claudio shames Hero at the altar","id":705},{"s":"a4s1","t":"The Friar's plan: Hero 'dies'","id":761},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Beatrice and Benedick confess: 'Kill Claudio'","id":786},{"s":"a4s2","t":"The villains are examined; Don John has fled","id":843},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Benedick challenges Claudio; Borachio confesses","id":941},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Claudio mourns at Hero's tomb","id":1021},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Hero unmasked; two weddings and a dance","id":1066}],"irony":{"77":"Claudio's lack of trust in himself becomes a lack of trust in Hero.","97":"We know a romantic comedy rarely lets a vow like this survive.","120":"Don Pedro's harmless disguise will be overheard and twisted. Disguise soon becomes Don John's weapon too.","241":"Claudio decides love makes friends false. He is wrong about Don Pedro, and will be just as quick to believe the next lie.","313":"The 'love-gods' cannot see the plot being hatched against Hero at the same time.","384":"Benedick trusts Leonato because he is old and respectable, which is exactly why the trick works.","618":"We know the wedding will be wrecked; Hero's heavy heart is ominous.","675":"Leonato sends away the men who could save his daughter's wedding.","695":"The audience knows Hero is innocent; Claudio thinks he has caught her lying.","705":"We know the blush is innocence. Claudio reads every sign the wrong way.","716":"The liar swears to the truth.","958":"Leonato's 'revenge' is a marriage to Hero herself, which only he and the audience know.","1051":"Claudio promises to marry a bride he cannot see; the bride is the woman he rejected.","1085":"The lovers were tricked into love, but their own sonnets prove the love is real."},"devices":{"wit":"Puns and wordplay","hunt":"Hunting and fishing","war":"War and weapons","fashion":"Fashion and appearance","horns":"Horns and cuckolds","malaprop":"Malapropisms"},"dev":{"18":[["war","Beatrice turns Benedick into a boastful archer.","challenged Cupid at the flight"]],"25":[["wit","A pun: 'stuffed with virtues' becomes a stuffed dummy.","he is no less than a stuffed man"]],"27":[["war","Wit as a battle with wounded soldiers.","In our last conflict four of his five wits went halting off"]],"29":[["fashion","Loyalty changing like hat styles.","he wears his faith but as the fashion of his hat"]],"101":[["hunt","Benedick imagines himself as a target.","hang me in a bottle like a cat"]],"103":[["horns","Cuckold jokes: a married man fears horns.","pluck off the bull's horns and set them in my forehead"]],"173":[["horns","Beatrice twists a proverb into a joke about husbands.","God sends a curst cow short horns"]],"177":[["wit","The fate supposed to await old maids.","lead his apes into hell"]],"185":[["wit","An extended comparison between dances and stages of marriage.","Scotch jig, a measure, and a cinque-pace"]],"196":[["wit","A classical joke about masks.","My visor is Philemon's roof"]],"265":[["war","Words as weapons.","She speaks poniards, and every word stabs"]],"280":[["wit","A pun on Seville oranges and 'civil'.","civil as an orange"]],"371":[["hunt","The trick on Benedick as bird-hunting.","Stalk on, stalk on; the fowl sits"]],"379":[["hunt","The trick as fishing.","Bait the hook well: this fish will bite"]],"421":[["war","Jokes as gunfire made of paper.","these paper bullets of the brain"]],"439":[["hunt","The gulling of Beatrice as fishing.","The pleasant'st angling is to see the fish"]],"442":[["hunt","Beatrice as an untamed hawk.","haggards of the rock"]],"462":[["hunt","Love as hunting.","Some Cupid kills with arrows, some with traps"]],"464":[["hunt","Beatrice speaks of herself as a hawk being tamed.","Taming my wild heart to thy loving hand"]],"541":[["malaprop","'Comprehend' for 'apprehend'.","you shall comprehend all vagrom men"]],"585":[["fashion","Fashion as a thief that changes men's appearance.","what a deformed thief this fashion is"]],"662":[["malaprop","'Odorous' for 'odious'.","Comparisons are odorous"]],"674":[["malaprop","'Aspicious' for 'suspicious'.","our watch, sir, hath indeed comprehended two aspicious persons"]],"705":[["fashion","Hero as fruit that looks good but is rotten inside.","Give not this rotten orange to your friend"]],"840":[["malaprop","'Redemption' for 'damnation': the opposite of what he means.","thou wilt be condemned into everlasting redemption"]],"1055":[["horns","Claudio returns to the cuckold joke at the wedding.","we'll tip thy horns with gold"]],"1093":[["horns","Benedick ends the play with a cuckold joke at his own expense.","there is no staff more reverent than one tipped with horn"]]},"stage":{"house":"Leonato's house","garden":"The garden","street":"A street","church":"The church","prison":"The prison"},"why":{"0":"Sets up the central relationship as a battle of equals.","1":"Beatrice's first words to Benedick: she has been watching him.","3":"Shows Beatrice's independence and scorn for empty romance.","4":"Introduces fashion as a symbol of inconstancy.","6":"Shows how Claudio thinks of love: as acquiring something valuable.","8":"Love and money: Claudio checks the inheritance.","9":"The vow the comedy exists to break.","11":"The first deception, meant kindly, sets the pattern of the plot.","13":"Don John's self-description: open about being bad, secret in his methods.","16":"Beatrice's protest against male authority in marriage.","18":"Masks and wooing by proxy create the first misunderstanding.","20":"Claudio's distrust of others, which he applies to Hero later.","22":"Benedick hurt: wit wounds and he takes it seriously.","25":"Don Pedro's matchmaking, a 'good' deception.","26":"The 'bad' deception: the window plot.","28":"The song warns that men are faithless, foreshadowing Claudio.","31":"Benedick's comic rationalising of his change of heart.","36":"Beatrice's change is sincere and in verse.","37":"Don John's slander, short and brutal.","42":"The play's darkest public scene: Hero shamed.","43":"Claudio misreads Hero; seeing is not knowing.","45":"Shows the power of honour culture over a father's love.","46":"The Friar 'notes' Hero properly: the title's pun at work.","48":"Love declared at the darkest point.","50":"The turning point: Benedick must choose between friend and lover.","51":"Beatrice's rage shows what women are denied.","53":"Comic relief from Dogberry, the unlikely hero.","55":"Truth comes out through the Watch's 'noting'.","56":"Is Claudio truly guilty, or just mistaken?","59":"Public restoration of Hero's honour.","60":"Hero's 'rebirth' closes the plot.","61":"The witty lovers' war ends in a kiss.","62":"Benedick accepts change as human."},"arc":{"Beatrice and Benedick's love":[1,1,1,1,1,5,7,7,7,7,7,9,9,9,10,10,10],"Claudio's trust in Hero":[8,8,8,6,9,9,9,4,1,1,1,0,0,7,7,8,10]},"arcnote":"As Beatrice and Benedick slowly move from scorn to love, Claudio's quick trust in Hero collapses at the wedding, then is restored only when the truth comes out.","context":[["Date and first performance","Written about 1598–99. The 1600 Quarto names the famous clown Will Kemp and Richard Cowley in some of Dogberry's and Verges' speech prefixes, so we know who first played them."],["Sources","The Hero–Claudio story comes from an Italian tale by Matteo Bandello and from an episode in Ariosto's poem Orlando Furioso, where a maid is dressed in her mistress's clothes to fool a lover. Beatrice, Benedick and Dogberry are Shakespeare's own additions."],["Nothing and noting","In Elizabethan English 'nothing' sounded close to 'noting', which meant observing, overhearing and musical notes. 'Nothing' was also bawdy slang for a woman's sexuality, which links the title to the slander of Hero. The title is a pun: a fuss about nothing, and a play about noting."],["Messina and Aragon","Sicily was ruled by the Spanish crown of Aragon, which is why a Spanish prince is the guest of honour. The soldiers are returning from a short, unnamed war in which Don John rebelled against his brother."],["Women and chastity","A woman's value in marriage was bound up with her virginity and her father's honour. An accusation, even unproved, could ruin her and shame her family, which is why Leonato reacts so violently."],["Honour and duelling","A gentleman's honour demanded he defend himself or his family. Leonato, Antonio and Benedick all issue challenges. Benedick's challenge to his friend shows how serious Beatrice's demand is."],["Illegitimacy","Don John is a 'bastard'. Illegitimate sons could not inherit and were often portrayed on stage as resentful outsiders and villains."],["Prose and verse","Much Ado is mostly prose, unusual for Shakespeare. Verse appears in moments of formal speech, romance and crisis, such as the gulling of Beatrice and the church scene."],["The perfect courtier","Elizabethan gentlemen admired the ideal described in Castiglione's The Courtier (English translation 1561): grace, wit and effortless good manners. Don Pedro's circle prizes clever talk and polished behaviour, which makes Claudio's loyalty to his prince, and his concern with how things look, easier to understand."],["Masked dances","Masked revels were fashionable entertainments at court and in great houses. The masks allow free talk, flirting and mistaken identity."],["On screen","Kenneth Branagh's 1993 film, set in a sunlit Tuscan villa with Branagh and Emma Thompson as Benedick and Beatrice, made the play widely known again. Productions often show the window scene, which Shakespeare only reports."],["Cuckoldry jokes","A cuckold was a man whose wife was unfaithful, said to grow horns. Fear of it runs through the men's jokes and helps explain why they believe Don John so quickly."]],"critics":{"deception":[["Samuel Taylor Coleridge (lectures, 1818)","Paraphrased: the plot's interest does not depend on the villain; Don John sets things in motion and is then barely seen."],["Jean E. Howard, 'Antitheatricality Staged' (1987)","Paraphrased: the play is full of staged scenes and directors, good and bad, and shows how such theatre can shape what people believe."]],"noting":[["A. P. Rossiter, Angel with Horns (1961)","Paraphrased: the play is about 'misprision', mistaking, as characters misread what they see and hear."]],"gender":[["Anna Jameson, Characteristics of Women (1832)","Paraphrased: Beatrice's sharp tongue hides a warm and generous heart, shown in her loyalty to Hero."],["Carol Thomas Neely, Broken Nuptials in Shakespeare's Plays (1985)","Paraphrased: the broken wedding exposes men's fear of women's sexuality; the women's loyalty to each other matters as much as the marriages."]],"love":[["King Charles I (annotated copy of the Second Folio)","Not a critic: the king wrote 'Benedik and Betrice' as an alternative title in his copy, showing who audiences cared about even then."]]},"insults":[{"id":21,"f":"he is a very valiant trencher-man","who":"Beatrice","n":"On Benedick: brave only at the dinner table."},{"id":25,"f":"he is no less than a stuffed man","who":"Beatrice","n":"Benedick as a stuffed dummy."},{"id":49,"f":"my dear Lady Disdain","who":"Benedick","n":"To Beatrice."},{"id":55,"f":"you are a rare parrot-teacher","who":"Benedick","n":"Beatrice repeats herself like someone training a parrot."},{"id":56,"f":"A bird of my tongue is better than a beast of yours","who":"Beatrice","n":"Her reply: better a talking bird than a beast."},{"id":270,"f":"I cannot endure my Lady Tongue","who":"Benedick","n":"About Beatrice."},{"id":268,"f":"rather than hold three words' conference with this harpy","who":"Benedick","n":"A harpy is a monster, half woman and half bird."},{"id":225,"f":"he is the prince's jester: a very dull fool","who":"Beatrice","n":"To Benedick's masked face."},{"id":155,"f":"A very forward March-chick","who":"Don John","n":"On Hero: a chick hatched too early."},{"id":157,"f":"That young start-up hath all the glory of my overthrow","who":"Don John","n":"On Claudio."},{"id":326,"f":"to a contaminated stale, such a one as Hero","who":"Borachio","n":"Slander of Hero: 'stale' means prostitute."},{"id":705,"f":"Give not this rotten orange to your friend","who":"Claudio","n":"On Hero at the altar."},{"id":802,"f":"a goodly Count Comfect","who":"Beatrice","n":"Mocking Claudio as a sugary sweet."},{"id":847,"f":"Off, coxcomb","who":"Conrade","n":"To Dogberry."},{"id":849,"f":"you are an ass; you are an ass","who":"Conrade","n":"The insult Dogberry cannot forget."},{"id":881,"f":"Boys, apes, braggarts, Jacks, milksops","who":"Antonio","n":"On Claudio and Don Pedro."},{"id":923,"f":"For my Lord Lack-beard there","who":"Benedick","n":"On Claudio, mocking his youth."}],"pronouns":[{"id":65,"f":"Benedick, didst thou note the daughter","n":"Claudio and Benedick use 'thou' as close friends."},{"id":711,"f":"Out on thee! Seeming","n":"Claudio switches to contemptuous 'thee' to Hero in church."},{"id":870,"f":"Marry, thou dost wrong me; thou dissembler","n":"Leonato uses 'thou' to Claudio in anger, though Claudio outranks his patience."},{"id":775,"f":"By my sword, Beatrice, thou lovest me","n":"Benedick moves from 'you' to the intimate 'thou' with Beatrice once love is declared."},{"id":923,"f":"Fare you well, boy: you know my mind","n":"Benedick uses 'you' and 'boy' to Claudio: cold and dismissive after his challenge."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Don Pedro woos Hero for Claudio","Claudio proposes himself","Benedick woos Hero"],"a1s3":["Don John helps the match","Don John tries to wreck it","Don John leaves Messina"],"a2s1":["Benedick hates Beatrice forever","Benedick is tricked into loving Beatrice","Benedick leaves Messina"],"a2s2":["The plan is discovered at once","Don Pedro and Claudio are fooled","Margaret refuses"],"a2s3":["Beatrice laughs at the trick","Beatrice is tricked too","Beatrice tells Benedick"],"a3s2":["Claudio ignores Don John","Claudio believes Don John","Claudio fights Don John"],"a3s3":["The Watch let the men go","The Watch arrest them","Borachio escapes"],"a3s5":["The wedding goes ahead happily","Claudio shames Hero","The wedding is cancelled quietly"],"a4s1":["Benedick refuses Beatrice","Benedick challenges Claudio","Benedick leaves with Claudio"],"a5s1":["Claudio refuses Leonato's terms","Claudio agrees to marry the 'niece'","Claudio leaves Messina"],"a5s3":["Claudio marries a stranger","Claudio marries Hero","The wedding is called off"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Beatrice and Hero","pts":["Beatrice is outspoken and witty; Hero is quiet and obedient in public.","Beatrice refuses to marry to please her uncle; Hero accepts the match her father arranges.","When Hero is shamed she faints; Beatrice demands revenge."],"ids":[177,743]},{"t":"Benedick and Claudio","pts":["Benedick mocks love; Claudio falls in love at first sight.","Claudio lets others woo for him; Benedick (eventually) speaks for himself.","Claudio believes the slander; Benedick believes Beatrice and challenges his friend."],"ids":[97,705]},{"t":"Don Pedro and Don John","pts":["Both brothers stage scenes to deceive others.","Don Pedro deceives to make love; Don John to destroy it.","Don Pedro is fooled by his brother's trick."],"ids":[313,141]},{"t":"The two gullings","pts":["Benedick is tricked by men in prose; Beatrice by women in verse.","Both are told they are proud and the other is dying of love.","Both decide to change: Benedick with jokes, Beatrice with sincerity."],"ids":[379,464]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","Much Ado About Nothing"],["Written","About 1598–99; printed in a Quarto in 1600 and in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Romantic comedy, with a plot that turns almost tragic in Act 4"],["Setting","Messina, Sicily, in Leonato's house, orchard and nearby streets, church and prison, over about a week"],["Language","Mostly prose, with verse for formal, romantic and serious moments"],["Main plot","Claudio and Hero's engagement, wrecked by Don John's slander and restored at the end"],["Second plot","Beatrice and Benedick, tricked by their friends into admitting love"],["Comic plot","Dogberry and the Watch, who stumble on the truth"],["Antagonist","Don John, Don Pedro's resentful illegitimate brother"],["Turning point","The church scene (4.1): Hero is shamed and Beatrice asks Benedick to 'Kill Claudio'"],["Resolution","Borachio confesses, Hero is 'reborn', and two couples marry"],["Key themes","Love and courtship, deception, noting and misreading, honour, men and women"],["Title pun","'Nothing' sounded like 'noting': observing, overhearing and misjudging"]],"analysis":{"BENEDICK":["Benedick begins as the play's self-declared enemy of marriage. His jokes about horns and bachelors are a performance of independence, and, like Beatrice's, they show he cannot stop thinking about the other sex. He is stung far more than he admits when she calls him 'the prince's jester'.","The orchard trick works because it flatters him and because he hears himself criticised as proud. His reasoning ('the world must be peopled') is funny precisely because he is talking himself into what he already wants.","His real test comes in the church. Unlike Claudio and Don Pedro he trusts Beatrice's judgment of Hero, and when she says 'Kill Claudio' he chooses love and justice over male friendship. His challenge in Act 5 is serious; the joker has become a man of action.","He ends the play still witty but humbled: 'man is a giddy thing'. He accepts that people change, and that being mocked for it is a small price."],"BEATRICE":["Beatrice is the sharpest speaker in the play. She mocks marriage and men because she refuses to be 'over-mastered with a piece of valiant dust'. Her jokes are also defensive: hints like 'I know you of old' suggest an earlier hurt involving Benedick.","When she overhears Hero and Ursula calling her proud and scornful, she changes at once, and in verse. Her change is quieter and more sincere than Benedick's.","In Act 4 she is the only one apart from the Friar who never doubts Hero. Her fury, 'O that I were a man!', exposes how little power women have: she cannot fight for her cousin, so she must ask a man to do it.","She keeps her independence to the end, agreeing to marry 'upon great persuasion' and 'to save your life'. The final kiss silences her, which some audiences find romantic and others uneasy."],"CLAUDIO":["Claudio is a brave young soldier but an inexperienced lover. He falls for Hero's looks, checks she is Leonato's heir, and lets Don Pedro do his wooing. He trusts reports more than people.","At the masked dance he instantly believes Don John's lie about Don Pedro, which shows his insecurity. The same weakness makes him believe the window scene without asking Hero a single question.","His public rejection of Hero is cruel and calculated: he plans it in advance to protect his own honour. His puns on her name show he is thinking about reputation rather than about her.","He repents and accepts Leonato's penance, but he claims he sinned only 'in mistaking'. Whether he has really learned anything, or deserves Hero, is one of the play's open questions."],"HERO":["Hero says little in public and obeys her father, which makes her the conventional opposite of Beatrice. In private, with her women, she is lively and witty, and she leads the trick on Beatrice with skill.","Her near-silence at the wedding is part of the shock: she can barely defend herself against three powerful men and her own father. She faints rather than argues.","The Friar's plan makes her a symbol: her reputation 'dies' and is reborn. When she returns she says only a few lines and does not blame Claudio, which modern audiences often find troubling."],"DON PEDRO":["Don Pedro is the highest-ranking character and enjoys arranging other people's lives: wooing Hero for Claudio and setting up the gulling of Benedick and Beatrice ('we are the only love-gods').","His readiness to believe his brother, whom he has just forgiven for rebellion, is surprising, and he joins Claudio in shaming Hero, saying he 'stands dishonoured'.","He ends unmarried. Beatrice jokingly turns down his half-serious proposal in Act 2, and Benedick's final advice, 'get thee a wife', leaves him slightly outside the celebration."],"DON JOHN":["Don John is a malcontent: sullen, envious and resentful of his brother and of Claudio, who gained glory from his defeat. He calls himself 'a plain-dealing villain' and refuses to pretend to be happy.","His evil has almost no motive beyond spite; he says that if he can cross Claudio 'any way' he will be happy. He relies on Borachio's cleverness rather than his own.","He disappears after the church scene and is captured off stage. His thin role lets the play stay a comedy: the danger is real, but the villain is never allowed to dominate."],"LEONATO":["Leonato is a generous host and a playful one, joining in the trick on Benedick. He is proud of his daughter and assumes she will obey him.","In the church he believes the princes over his own daughter and wishes her dead. His speech shows how completely family honour depends on a daughter's chastity.","Grief transforms him. He rejects comforting advice, challenges Claudio despite his age, and then designs the penance that brings Hero back. He ends as the play's moral authority."],"DOGBERRY":["Dogberry, the Master Constable, loves long words and gets them wrong: 'comprehend' for 'apprehend', 'odorous' for 'odious'. His malapropisms make fun of petty officials who love their own authority.","His Watch are fools, yet they uncover the plot that the clever lords miss. The play suggests that truth can be found by accident, and that wit is not the same as wisdom.","His outrage at being called an ass is comic but also touching: he has dignity, two gowns and 'everything handsome about him', and he wants to be respected."]},"motifs":[["Noting and overhearing","Almost every turn of the plot comes from someone noting, overhearing or watching: Antonio's servant, Borachio behind the arras, Benedick in the arbour, Beatrice in the bower, the princes at the window, the Watch under the porch. Some overhearing leads to truth, some to disaster.",[1,30,34,46,55]],["Masks, disguise and staged scenes","The masked dance, Margaret dressed as Hero, the gulling scenes and the masked bride all depend on people performing. Good and bad characters use the same theatrical tricks.",[11,18,25,26,60]],["Hunting and taming","Lovers are hunted, fished and caught: 'Bait the hook well', 'Some Cupid kills with arrows, some with traps'. Wild creatures, from the 'savage bull' to the 'haggard' hawk, are tamed by marriage.",[10,30,34,36]],["Horns and cuckoldry","Jokes about horns run from Benedick's first scene to his last line. They show the men's fear of being betrayed by women, which helps make Don John's slander believable.",[9,10,63]],["Fashion and inconstancy","Beatrice says Benedick changes his faith like his hat; Borachio calls fashion a 'deformed thief'. Clothes and appearances change, and so do people.",[4,29,40,62]],["Public shaming","Claudio plans to shame Hero 'in the congregation', Leonato fears his family's public disgrace, Dogberry is outraged at being called an ass in front of witnesses, and the epitaph clears Hero's name in public. In this society what others say about you can matter more than what is true.",[39,42,45,53,59]],["Wit as warfare","The 'merry war', words as 'poniards', 'paper bullets of the brain'. Language is a weapon, until the church scene shows how much real damage words can do.",[0,22,31,57]]],"symbols2":[["Hero's window","The bedroom window is where appearance replaces truth: Margaret in Hero's clothes, seen at night, is taken as proof of Hero's guilt."],["The masks","Masks at the dance and at the second wedding hide identity. The second masking reverses the first: this time it brings a joyful revelation."],["The rotten orange","Claudio's image for Hero: beautiful outside, corrupt inside. It is really an image of how his own judgment works on surfaces."],["Hero's 'death'","Hero's pretended death stands for the death of her reputation. When the truth comes out she is, in effect, reborn, and the comedy can end in marriage."],["Hero's tomb and epitaph","The empty tomb and Claudio's epitaph mark the death of the slander and the restoration of Hero's name."],["The sonnets","Benedick's and Beatrice's clumsy love poems, stolen from their pockets, finally expose the truth they deny in public."]],"bigq":[["Does Claudio deserve Hero at the end?",["He shames her in public instead of asking her","He believes Don John on very little evidence","He grieves and accepts Leonato's penance","But he says he sinned only 'in mistaking'","The genre demands a happy ending; how far should we accept it?"],[20,42,43,56]],["Is Beatrice a feminist voice, or tamed by the end?",["She mocks marriage and male authority","She demands justice for Hero when men do not","She asks a man to act for her because she cannot","She agrees to marry; Benedick stops her mouth with a kiss"],[16,50,51,61]],["Are the 'good' tricks very different from the bad one?",["Both use staged overhearing","Don Pedro's trick reveals real feelings","Don John's trick invents false ones","Both show how easily people believe what they hear"],[11,25,26,30]],["Why do the men believe Don John so easily?",["Fear of cuckoldry runs through their jokes","Honour matters more than evidence","They trust their eyes in the dark","They never ask Hero"],[9,37,43,55]],["How much does Margaret know?",["She talks with Borachio at the window dressed in Hero's clothes","Borachio insists she 'knew not what she did'","She is absent from the wedding and silent about it afterwards","Leonato says she was at fault 'against her will'","Is she a careless accomplice or another victim?"],[26,55]],["What if things had gone differently?",["If the Watch had not overheard Borachio","If Leonato had listened to Dogberry before the wedding","If Claudio had asked Hero privately","If the sonnets had not been found","Each 'if' shows how much the plot turns on chance and on who listens"],[30,41,55,61]],["Is this really a comedy?",["A woman is shamed and thought dead","A father wishes his child dead","But the audience knows from Act 3 that the truth is out","Dogberry keeps the mood from becoming tragic","It ends with weddings and a dance"],[42,45,53,63]]],"symbols":{"Noting and nothing":"\\bnothing\\b|\\bnote\\b|\\bnoted?\\b|\\bnoting\\b","Horns and cuckolds":"\\bhorns?\\b|cuckold","Honour":"\\bhonou?r","Fashion":"\\bfashion","Eyes and seeing":"\\beyes?\\b|\\bsee\\b|\\bsaw\\b","Masks and visors":"\\bvisor|\\bmask"},"stagerules":[["garden|orchard","garden"],["church","church"],["prison","prison"],["street","street"],["house|room|hall","house"]],"solo":{"241":1,"254":1,"344":1,"421":1,"429":1,"464":1,"991":1},"iago":[["a1s3","Hears from Borachio that Claudio will marry Hero, and vows to spoil it."],["a2s1","Tells the masked Claudio that Don Pedro is wooing Hero for himself."],["a2s2","Agrees to Borachio's window plan and promises him a thousand ducats."],["a3s2","Tells Don Pedro and Claudio that Hero is 'disloyal' and offers proof."],["a3s3","Borachio woos Margaret at the window while Don John shows the princes."],["a4s1","Backs up Claudio's accusation at the altar."],["a4s2","Flees Messina secretly."],["a5s1","Exposed by Borachio's confession."],["a5s4","Captured and brought back to Messina."]],"rel":{"1":[["CLAUDIO","HERO","love"],["BEATRICE","BENEDICK","hate"],["BENEDICK","BEATRICE","hate"],["DON PEDRO","CLAUDIO","trust"],["DON JOHN","CLAUDIO","hate"],["BORACHIO","DON JOHN","trust"]],"2":[["DON JOHN","CLAUDIO","deceive"],["DON PEDRO","BENEDICK","deceive"],["CLAUDIO","HERO","love"],["BENEDICK","BEATRICE","love"],["BORACHIO","MARGARET","deceive"],["LEONATO","DON PEDRO","trust"]],"3":[["HERO","BEATRICE","deceive"],["BEATRICE","BENEDICK","love"],["DON JOHN","CLAUDIO","deceive"],["DON JOHN","DON PEDRO","deceive"],["CLAUDIO","HERO","hate"],["DOGBERRY","BORACHIO","hate"]],"4":[["CLAUDIO","HERO","hate"],["LEONATO","HERO","hate"],["BEATRICE","HERO","love"],["BENEDICK","BEATRICE","love"],["BEATRICE","CLAUDIO","hate"],["FRIAR FRANCIS","HERO","trust"]],"5":[["BENEDICK","CLAUDIO","hate"],["LEONATO","CLAUDIO","hate"],["CLAUDIO","HERO","love"],["BENEDICK","BEATRICE","love"],["DON PEDRO","DON JOHN","hate"],["BORACHIO","MARGARET","trust"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present the relationship between Beatrice and Benedick?",[0,2,22,48,57,61]],["How is Claudio presented as a lover?",[6,7,8,20,43,56]],["Explore how deception is presented in Much Ado About Nothing.",[11,25,26,30,47,55]],["How does Shakespeare present Beatrice?",[3,16,24,36,50,51]],["How are ideas about honour and reputation presented?",[37,42,44,45,59,60]],["How does Shakespeare present Benedick's change of heart?",[9,27,31,33,48,62]],["How are men and women presented in the play?",[15,16,28,29,51,52]],["Explore the importance of noting and overhearing.",[1,30,34,43,46,55]],["How does Shakespeare present Don John?",[12,13,14,37,38]],["What is the role of Dogberry and the Watch?",[41,53,55]],["How is marriage presented in the play?",[9,10,17,31,63]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short, embedded quotations","Analysed key words and images, not just the meaning","Linked to context (honour, chastity, noting/nothing, Elizabethan comedy)","Tracked change across the whole play: beginning, middle, end","Considered the audience and dramatic effect (comedy turning dark)","Mentioned an alternative reading or critic","Wrote a conclusion that answers the question","Checked spelling of names: Benedick, Beatrice, Claudio, Leonato, Don Pedro, Dogberry"],"place":{"a1s1":"Messina","a1s2":"Messina","a1s3":"Messina","a2s1":"Messina","a2s2":"Messina","a2s3":"Messina","a3s1":"Messina","a3s2":"Messina","a3s3":"Messina","a3s4":"Messina","a3s5":"Messina","a4s1":"Messina","a4s2":"Messina","a5s1":"Messina","a5s2":"Messina","a5s3":"Messina","a5s4":"Messina"},"models":[[0,"Beatrice and Benedick's relationship is presented as a contest of wit that hides real feeling.","Leonato's metaphor \"a kind of merry war\" joins happiness and conflict, preparing us for sparring that is also a kind of courtship.","The audience enjoy their insults while guessing they protest too much.","Witty banter between lovers was a popular feature of Elizabethan comedy.","Some productions suggest a past romance, since Beatrice hints Benedick once won her heart \"with false dice\"."],[7,"Claudio is presented as a lover who judges by appearance.","\"In mine eye she is the sweetest lady\" stresses sight, and the phrase \"In mine eye\" hints his love is only as deep as looking.","The audience may find his quick love sweet, but it prepares his quick rejection of Hero.","Arranged marriages were linked to wealth, which his question about Leonato's heir shows.","Some critics defend him as young and misled; others see him as shallow throughout."],[30,"Deception is shown as both kind and cruel, depending on who uses it.","Claudio's metaphor \"Bait the hook well: this fish will bite\" makes the trick on Beatrice a fishing game.","The audience enjoy the gentle deception before the play turns to Don John's harmful one.","Eavesdropping scenes were a favourite comic device on the Elizabethan stage.","The Friar's plan to fake Hero's death shows deception can also heal."],[51,"Beatrice is presented as fierce, witty and limited by being a woman.","\"O God, that I were a man!\" is an exclamation of frustration that shows her anger at a world where only men can fight for honour.","The audience feel the injustice done to Hero through her rage.","Women had little legal or social power to defend their reputation.","Her demand \"Kill Claudio\" is sometimes played for shock laughter, which changes the tone."],[42,"Honour and reputation are shown as fragile, especially for women.","Claudio's metaphor \"rotten orange\" makes Hero look sound outside but decayed within, publicly destroying her name.","The audience know she is innocent, so the scene is painful dramatic irony.","A woman's chastity was tied to her family's honour, as Leonato's reaction shows.","The men's honour is restored more easily, which highlights the double standard."],[48,"Benedick changes from a scornful bachelor to a sincere lover.","\"I do love nothing in the world so well as you: is not that strange?\" uses plain words and a question that shows his surprise at himself.","The audience enjoy the moment when the mocking wit admits real feeling.","Comedies usually end in marriage, so the audience expect his conversion.","Some readers argue he was in love all along, and the trick only gave him permission."],[29,"Men and women are shown in conflict, with men often failing women.","Balthasar's song line \"Men were deceivers ever\" states a general truth that the plot then proves.","The audience hear the song as a warning before Claudio's betrayal.","Songs in comedies often comment on the action.","The song advises women to \"sigh no more\" and be merry, which could be seen as accepting men's faults."],[46,"Noting, or overhearing, drives the plot and shows how easily people misread.","The Friar's \"By noting of the lady\" shows the right kind of noting: careful observation of Hero's real reaction.","The audience notice the pun on the title, as \"nothing\" sounded like \"noting\".","Elizabethan pronunciation made the pun clear.","The Watch, who are foolish, still note the truth, which shows insight can come from unexpected places."],[13,"Don John is presented as a villain who openly enjoys his own bad nature.","\"I am a plain-dealing villain\" turns a phrase of honesty into a boast about villainy.","The audience see a malcontent who needs no reason beyond resentment.","Don John is a bastard brother, which links him to the stereotype of the resentful bastard.","His lack of a strong motive makes him less frightening than Iago, which suits a comedy."],[41,"Dogberry and the Watch bring comedy and, by accident, resolve the plot.","\"Comparisons are odorous\" is a malapropism for \"odious\", showing his love of big words he does not understand.","The audience laugh, but also see that the foolish uncover what the clever missed.","The comic constable was a familiar type, played by the clown Will Kemp.","Some critics argue the Watch show that truth comes out despite human folly."],[17,"Marriage is shown as both desirable and risky.","Beatrice's simile comparing \"wooing, wedding, and repenting\" to three dances suggests marriage moves from excitement to regret.","The audience laugh, though the play tests whether her view is true.","Marriage was expected of women and usually arranged.","The play ends with dancing and two weddings, which can be seen as answering her joke."]],"lang":{"un":[[421,"Happy are they that hear their detractions","They that hear their detractions are happy"],[423,"Against my will I am sent to bid you come in to dinner","I am sent against my will to bid you come in to dinner"],[1021,"Done to death by slanderous tongues Was the Hero that here lies","The Hero that here lies was done to death by slanderous tongues"]],"pun":[[357,"Notes, notes, forsooth, and nothing","nothing","pun","Nothing sounded like noting: watching, overhearing and musical notes. The title of the play carries the same joke."],[280,"civil as an orange","civil","pun","Civil means polite, and it sounds like Seville, the bitter oranges: Claudio is sour with jealousy."],[674,"hath indeed comprehended two aspicious persons","comprehended","malapropism","Dogberry means apprehended and suspicious. His mistakes are funny, but his watch has caught the truth."],[429,"there's a double meaning in that","double meaning","wordplay","Benedick squeezes hidden love out of Beatrice's rude message, a joke about his own new habit of reading too much into words."]],"st":[[773,"I do love nothing in the world so well as you",[["I","A confession of his own feeling, at last."],["nothing","Plays on the play's title and his old habit of mockery."],["you","Turns all his attention on Beatrice."]]],[786,"Kill Claudio",[["Kill","Shocking: love demands violence."],["Claudio","Names the friend Benedick must choose against."]]],[796,"O God, that I were a man",[["God","A cry of despair to heaven."],["I","Her frustration that she herself cannot act."],["man","Attacks a world where only men may fight for honour."]]]]}};
